      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>BUNYAN'S LIFE AND</head>
        <p>BUNYAN'S LIFE AND</p>
        <p>WORKS</p>
        <p>Discussed By Members Of Sankey Bridges Men's Adult School.</p>
        <p>The lecturette at Sankey Bridges Adult School on Sunday morning was by Mr. Rupert Jones on the subject of John Bunyan. Introducing his subject, the lecturer gave a brief resume of John Bunyan's early life and environment, showing some of the forces which moulded his character. Bunyan was born at the village of Elston in Bedfordshire, in 1628, the year in which the House of Commons forced Charles I. to consent to a Petition of Rights. Milton was a son of the middle classes; the father of Shakespgaro was a respectable tradesman; but 131111V/11 was the son at a tin- ker, in days when tinkers were ,mostly gipsies and vagabonds. "I was," he says, ".2t low and inconsiderate generation, my father's house being of the rank that is meanest and most despised of all families in the land." It was this working tinker in whom God kindled the light of genius to which the world owes "The Pilgrim's Progress from Earth to Heaven." Bunyan's childhood fell in a time of wild religous and political ferment, and in an age when men's beliefs were more concrete, less shadowy, and more intensely real than now. Born with a vivid imagination he was, even in his childhood, so conscious of his boyish faults that he was scared and af- frighted with fearful dreams and visions of devils. He fell into the sins of swearing and lying, and looked with terrified misgiving and remorse-on amusements which in themselves were perfectly innocent. He had no books ex- cept "The Romance of Sir Bevis of Sonthainp- ton and the Bible, of which he, nnderstood 'every word in a most literal sense. Twice Saved From Drowning.</p>
        <p>So sensitive was his conscience, that, at a single rebuke he gave up the habit of swearing. John Bunyan was twice saved from drowning find once from the bite of an adder, and once. when he had enlisted in the Puritan army, a gentinal who had asked to take his kilacewo ohot throuo the heart. *tulta _ ueepeneu his diviue providence over him, and mar; his congcienee still more acutely sensi- tive, though he was entirely free from all the more flagrant and debasing forms of vice. Bunyan was neither drunkard nor libertine. On the disbandment of the army in 1646 he returned to his native village and resumed his trade. At 19 years of age he married an orphan girl, whose only worldly riches seemed to be two books "The Plain Man's Pathway to Heaven" and "The Practice of Piety." These were suited to Bunyan's state of mind and tem- perament and he told how he benefited from them greatly. He became convinced of the necessity of the new birth, and a conscious- ness that he had not attained to it. This un- healthy self-introspection drove him to the very verge of madness. But after a time he pined firm ground, and while still a young man in 1657 he was called to be a minister amongst the Baptists.</p>
        <p>Written In Prison.</p>
        <p>At the Restoration he was imprisoned in Bedford Gaol because he would not promise to givi• nip preaching. For twelve years he con- tinued in prison, supporting himself and his family by making tags for b otlaces. It was whilst in prison that Bunyan passed through his creative period as a great writer. Three of his books, produced in prison, were now world-classics in religous literature namely "The Pilgrim's Progress," "The Holy War," and "Grace Abounding". Kr. Jones quoted many extracts from these works—displaying singular beauty and imag- ination—the finest word pictures in the lan- guage. An instructive discussion followed the lec- ture, in which Bunyan's place in English literature came in for some attention.</p>
        <p>One member said that Bunyan's books were the reflex of the purity of his life, and the charity of his vision; and that much of his hold on literary critics of the past and present was due to the vividness of imagery and the purity of the idioms of his language. Another member ventured to express the opinion that it might be well if Bunyan was revised and adapted to present-day thought, but it was generally felt that any attempt to do this would destroy the beauty of "The Pil- grim's Progress" as literature.</p>
      </div>
