      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>PRETTY LOCAL WEDDING.</head>
        <p>PRETTY LOCAL WEDDING.</p>
        <p>HUGHES--BIBBY.</p>
        <p>A very • pretty wedding was solemnised en Tuesday at the Reformed- Episcopal Chi.rch. Bold-street, in the presence of a large congre- gation, the contracting parties being William. the youngest son of the late Mr. and Mrs. W. Hughes (Alaw Manod), Welsh music composer, of Festiniog, and Ada, the youngest daughter of Mr. and -Mrs. William Bibby, of Arpley- street, Warrington. The bride, who was given away by her father, was daintily attired in a silk crepe-de-chene dress with a satin full Court train covered with a hand-embroidered net, the work of the bride herself. The bridesmaids were Miss Maggie Hughes (sister of the bride- groom) and the Misses Bessie and Connie Dickens, Dora Bibby, Kathleen Bibby (nieces of the bride) and Elsie Lees (cousin of the bride). They were prettily attired and each wore a gold brooch, the gift of the bridegroom. The ceremony was performed by the Rev. W. T. Jaye, assisted by the Rev. D. James, of the Welsh and English Church at Arhosfa, Flinty formerly of the Boteler max School. - Suitable selections were played on the organ during the service by Mr. J. W. Jowett. After the ceremony a reception was held at Atkinson's Cafe, where about 80 guests were invited. The happy couple left in the evening for their 'honeymoon tour. of North, Wales. Lance-Corporal T. R. Bibby very efficiently discharged the duties of best man. A cable was received at the reception from the bride's sister and brother-in-law—Mr. and Mrs. H. Ingram, of Winnipeg, Canada—con- veying good wishes for their future happiness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>LIST OF PRESENTS.</head>
        <p>LIST OF PRESENTS.</p>
        <p>The following is a list of the presents received : Bride to Bridegroom, gold albert. Bridegroom to Bride, fur coat. Father and Mother of Bride, canteen of mtte•y. Mr. and Mrs. A. Dickens, pair of mahogany stands. Mr. and Mrs. John Bibby, toilet set. Mr. and Mrs. Wm. Bibby, jun., wicker bedroom</p>
        <p>chair. Mr. and Mrs. Green, silver flower stand. Mr. and Mrs. Harry Bibby, silver tea and coffee</p>
        <p>service. • Mr. Torn R. Bibby and Miss H. Davies, overmantel, vases and cut-glass flower stand. Mr. Ewart Bibby, silver sugar basin. The Dickens Family, household utensils. Dora Bibby, cut-glass pickle jar. Willie Bibby. set of jugs. Jackie Bibby, vases and pan. Kathleen Bibby, picture. Dorothy Greene, egg cups and vinegar bottle. Misses Kate and Maggie Hughes (Festiniog), copper</p>
        <p>spirit kettle. Mr. Charles Fisher (Festinieg), barometer, Mr. and letra. H. 0. Hughes (Old Colwyg), tea</p>
        <p>service. Mr. and Mrs. Ellis (Liverpool), silver crumb swop, Mr. Hughes (Wrexham), pickle jar. Mr. and Mrs. Owen and family (Festiniog), brass</p>
        <p>candlesticks. Mr. and Mrs. Thomas, carpet sweeper and coal box. Mr. and Mrs. Griffiths, pair of silver flower vases. Mr. and Mrs. W. T. Williams and Willie Williams, silver cream jug and pillow cases. Mr. and Mrs. Price, salad bowl and service. Miss Roberts and Rev. and Mrs. James, silver</p>
        <p>serviette rings. Miss J. Humphries, fruit dish. Mr. and Mrs. Jones, tea pot, jug and cress dish. Mr. and Mrs. Woodward, cheese dish. Miss Owen (Appleton), sugar sifter. Mr. and Mrs. E. E. Hughes, ,eiderdown. Mr. and Mrs. Morgan, plant pot. Miss P. Owen (Cardiff), case of afternoon tea knives. Mr. Kneilson (Manchester), quilt. Miss E. E. Brown, worked table cover. Mr. Hughes (Festiniog), jam dish. / Miss S. C. Jones (Festiniog), marmalade jar. Mr. and Mrs. T. Knight, tea service. Mr. and Mrs. Robert Lees, dinner service. Mr. R. Lees, jun. and Mrs. and Miss If. Booth,</p>
        <p>vases and flower stand. Mr. and Mrs. W. H. Knight, pair of pickle tars. Mrs. J. Knight, linen tablecloth. Mr. and Mrs. J. R. Calvert (Southport). clock. Mr. and Mrs. V. Calvert (Southport), silver jam</p>
        <p>stand. Mr. and Mrs. Stoddard (Blackpool), serviettes. 11150 Nellie Stoddard (Blackpool), oak tray and cloth. Rev. and Mrs. jays, rosebovii. Mr. and Mrs. Thos. Locker, cut-glass vases. Councillor and Mrs. J. T. Locker, clock. Councillor and Mrs. Halliwell, table and _supper cloths. 7. Coancia3: and Mrs. Lindley, inlaid mahogany tray. Mr. and Mrs. zitchley, picture. Dr. and Mrs. Martin, C silver :ugar.basin. 14. f, pnd Mrs. T. Nixon. Wedgwood ;Taos and plant</p>
