      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>VOLUNTEERS.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEERS.</p>
        <p>Cheshire Regiment Nearly Ten</p>
        <p>Thousand Strong.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Cheshire Volunteer Regi- ment was held at Crewe this week, under the presidency of Colonel George Dixon. It was reported that the regiment was now nearly 10,000 strong, and that on August Bank Holi- day the men would assemble in uniform on the Roodee at Chester, when they would be in- spected by General Sir 011oore Creagh, V.C. Major V. Astley Hampshire, of the 7th Bat- talion The King's (Liverpool Regiment), was appointed commandant of the Wallasey Bat- talion, which is now over 830 strong. The com- mittee (Ire in negotiation for the purchase of rifles for the regiment. LANCASHIRE MOVEMENT TAKING PRACTICAL FORM.</p>
        <p>The first meeting of the new County Com- mittee formed to consider the best way of utilising Lancashire Volunteer Training Corps was held at Preston on Monday. A resolution was passed that a county organisation of affiliated Volunteer Training Corps be formed, and that the Lord-Lieutenant be requested to invite the Volunteer Training Corps to become units of a county regiment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>EDUCATIONAL ECONOMY IN</head>
        <p>EDUCATIONAL ECONOMY IN</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE.</p>
        <p>The Director of Education for Lancashite has forwarded a letter to the various local Higher Education Committees in the county requestin them to reduce their estimates as far as p 'hie this year owing to the war. It is satisfactory to report, the Central Com- mittee state, that the authorities generally have recognised the necessity of economy in the pre- paration of their estimates. In many cases the estimates show a material reduction, while those districts in which increased contributions are required have been able to urge special reasons why these have been rendered neces- sary. The total estimated expenditure on higher education in non-county boroughs for the year amounted to £99,197, of which sum .e 40,619 was the County Council's contribution. With regard to elementary education, the estimate was £554,790, and the actual expsndi- tura £541,296.</p>
        <p>Fooled the Baby. Hewitt: Does your baby keep you awake? Jewett: No; I fooled him; as soon as he was born I got a job working nights.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>+ + + + 4. +4. + 4. + +4. ++++++ +4. ++++++++++++++++++++ +++++++ + + + ++ + + +4. ++ ++ + +4. +4., ++++++++++++++++++++ ++++4.4.++++++++++++++ +++44+4.4.0+ +++++++++++++++ + + ++ 4. 4. +4. 4. 4. 4. 4. + + ++ 4. 0 . + 4ifb +</head>
        <p>+ + + + 4. +4. + 4. + +4. ++++++ +4. ++++++++++++++++++++ +++++++ + + + ++ + + +4. ++ ++ + +4. +4., ++++++++++++++++++++ ++++4.4.++++++++++++++ +++44+4.4.0+ +++++++++++++++ + + ++ 4. 4. +4. 4. 4. 4. 4. + + ++ 4. 0 . + 4ifb +</p>
        <p>WOMAN'S WORLD.</p>
        <p>headquarters at Chelsea. It was formed some time ago, with the object of meeting the sore need of dressings of all kinds, such as bandages, swabs, pine-dressings, splints and anti-septic field dressings. The work of preparing these requisites require much patience and not a little skill, for it must be carried out with precision and exactitude to be of use, conse- quently the Society has engaged a qualified in struct,or, who teaches new membersow to roll bandages and make swabs and splint pads. Many, women lack the physical strength neces- sary for nursing or other strenuous war work, and to these, if possessed of nimble fingers, the object of the Society should appeal.</p>
        <p>A Problem of Etiquette. The war ought to solve the problem whether a man should salute or raise his hat when he meets a lady friend or acinaintance in the street. Every winter objections are raised to the accustomed practice, lest some poor man sliould catch cold in his head, and the war is tending to install the military salute in place of the hat-raising recognition. In France the woman waits to be acknowledged, and she is troubled how to show her regard for her wounded countrymen whom she meets in the streets. It is strange to French etiquette for her to bow first, as is the custom in this country, and the man cannot of his own accord greet a lady to whom he is a stranger. A Paris newspaper is trying to solve the problem, which in the early stages of the war presented no difficulty to a well-known lady in Brighton. Seeing a man in mufti, walking with a stick and a crutch, she went up to him and said 1" You are a brave and noble fellow, here is a packet of cigarettes for you." The man thanked her and took them, but he was not a returned, wounded soldier from the Front, simply a viotim of a bicycle accident.</p>
        <p>Holiday Outfits. Holidays will be both circumscribed and restricted in duration this year. But as, according to the old proverb, all work and no play makes Jack a dull boy, change and recreation of some kind will be even more necessary this year think in times of peace, since never before have women of all classes worked so strenuously as during this last year. Hence the necessity for holiday outfits, which, in common with this season's modes, are more than usually practical and sensible. Under the latter category come the tailored suits of cot- ton corduroy, casement cloth, coloured pique,</p>
        <p>and, of course, thin black or navy serge. The first mentioned, in some dark colours, makes a smart and useful suit, that will stand a con- siderable amount of hard wear. The same may be said of casement cloth, which in addition to being substantial, always looks cool. Pigue, like good wine, requires no bush, its sterling qualities have stood the test of•time, with the result that it is as popular as ever. These three mentioned materials are to be had in beautiful shades of blue, tan, grey, golden brown, pink and green, as well as in dark tones.</p>
        <p>Norfolk Coats. White casement cloth makes a most satis- factory holiday suit, or tub frock. Perhaps the modifidd Norfolk coat is the most suitable design, as well as, the most practical for suits of pique, cotton 'corduroy, or casement. cloth. But to be successful, good tailoring is essential. The pinafore style suits the tub frock admir- ably. A very pretty model in casement cloth in a lovely shade of pink, was worn under a blouse of white ,Organdi muslin.. Large pockets of the slit variety truly adorned the, pinafore, for they were heavily and beautifully embroidered in white silk. The holiday suit often consists of a plain skirt and plaid check, or striped coat, or vice versa, and cotton materials of all kinds are to be had this sum- mer, in the greatest variety of design and colouring. Any cotton suit may be finished by touches of black; nothing is smarter for a Norfolk coat than a narrow black leather belt, slipped, through slots of the material.</p>
        <p>Cretonne Gardening Frocks. The vogue for cretonne may probably be traced indirectly to the war. Not only are women taking ,up gardening seriously as ‘a career, but many amateurs in the country are filling the place of gardeners who have joined the Colours. Perforce therefore dainty outfits for this always interesting hobby had to be designed, and among suitable materials cre- tonne has been given the first place. It is in- expensive, artistic, durable, and it will stand a considerable amount of hard wear, and also wa,shee fairly well. The flowered designs are the more popular for gardening frocks, since they harmonise best with the occupation and surroundings of their wearers. Gardening frocks are more often of the pinafore type than any other, and are liberally provided with capacious pockets. The hat, or sunbonnet that accompanies the frock, is often of cretonne to match, dainty bags for ordinary use are made of this fascinating fabric.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>The Women's Farm and Garden Union. The Colonies, and more especially Canada, have set the Mother Country a splendid example in the matter of instruction of the most practical kind, not only for men but for women; on all subjects relating to agriculture. In the United States many farmers have at- tended with their sons and daughters, the agricultural colleges, so valuable and up-to- date is the teaching given. That agriculture 'is a highly technical industry is only beginning to dawn upon us, but the Colonies have for years farmed scientifically. Women have always been more or less fascinated by garden- ing and farming, but many who took up these industries, as careers, failed for lack of train- ing. The above mentioned Union, which has the Duchess of Argyle for its president, offers expert advice, and practical business know- ledge, and it also brings together the women who want work and the farmers who require help. Women as Jurors.</p>
        <p>Women are replacing men in every profession and industry. The Theatres Committee of .the L.C.C. have decided to raise no objection" to their employment—when competent—as kinema operators. But such work demands consider- able technical knowledge and long training, for a proficient operator is not made in a month. Women, however, can do anything nowadays, and a young girl has beaten the men of this year, and taken the Jacob Bell Scholarship for pharmaceutical students, the first time the scholarship has been awarded to a woman. Gas companies are employing inspectoresses, railway companies collectoresses, hotels page- boyesses, and so on. A woman has suggested that elderly members of her sex should offer— now that the National Register is at hand—to serve as jurors,- and thus relieve business men from a duty that possess hard upon them in war time. A Chelsea Society.</p>
        <p>A War Society that does its work quietly and Unobtrusively is the Surgical Requisites Association, which works in connection with Queen Marrs Needlework Guild, and has its</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>HOME NOTES.</head>
        <p>HOME NOTES.</p>
        <p>Profitable Recipes and Useful Suggestions,</p>
        <p>The Skin.</p>
        <p>To make the skin soft and white, use fresh lemou julee4 two ounces; glycerine, one ounce; rose water Or rain water, with three or four drops otto of roses added, one pint. Anoint the hands and face three or four times daily and allow to remain on several minutes before wiping.</p>
        <p>Why Men Are The Best Cooks. They were talking about Escoffier. "Yes," said a man, "men make the best cooks."</p>
        <p>"Of course they do," said a girL "Men, the world over, are the gluttons. Women's thoughts fly higher than the table. Where is the woman who is proud of her ability to tell canvas-back from mallard or Chateau Yquem from Haut Brion? No such woman exists. Men alone take their stomachs seriously. Naturally enough, therefore, the Escoffiers all come from the .retnks of men." Catsup. Mushroom catsup is made :—Sprinkle the trimmed tops with salt, stir thenrcitccasionally "for two or three days, then slightly press out the juice; add to each gallon of this one-half ounce each of bruised mustard seed and cloves and one ounce each bruised allspice, 'black pepper and gently simmer for one hour in a pprcelq!n-lined iron vessel; cool, strain ana Intt-19,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>Marrow.</head>
        <p>Marrow.</p>
        <p>At this time of the year, in almost any gar- den, marrows are plentiful, and it is a mistake that more use is not made of them. There is a mass of valuable food neglected in this way by rich and poor alike, the common way being to dish it up in a watery mess. The various kinds seem about the same in value in cookery, but some prefer the compact forms as more nutty in flavour. Cottagers very often let them grow much too large. It is best to choose the small land tmedium-sized ones, such as tive Italians slice into thin strips in their cookery.</p>
        <p>VEGETABLE MARROW AND TOMATOES. —Take three good-sided tomatoes, take out seed, place in a stewpan with one large potato, one large onion, sprig of thyme, and parsley, fry together a few minutes, add one gill of stock, boil for fifteen minutes, pass through a hair-sieve, put into stewpan, add one tablespoonful grated cheese, season, cover the marrow, place in oven ten minutes.</p>
        <p>SAVOURY VEGETABLE MARROW.—PeeI a marrow, cut it into convenient pieces, place them in a stewpan with half a sliced onion, spread over with butter, and season. Moisten with a little white stock and white sauce, and cook till tender. Reduce the sauce to a glaze, add a pat</p>
        <p>of butter, a little lemon-juice, sprinkle with chopped parsley, dish up, and serve. VEGETABLE MARROW WITH CHEESE.—PeeI two green marrows, not too large in size; cut them lengthwise into quarters, scoop out the seedy parts 7 iec thm in a Baiat6-pan with loz. of butter, seation with salt and pepper. Put thy pan on the fire for a quarter of an hour, shak- ing it from time to time; moisten with a little cream, and add a tablespoonful of grated Par- mesan cheese. Dish up the marrow neatly on an oblong dish; sprinkle with breadcrumbs and Parmesan cheese; place in a hot oven for a few minutes, and serve with piquante sauce poured round the dish.</p>
        <p>VEGETABLE MARROW A LA LYONNAISE.—PeeI the marrow, cut it in half, and remove the seeds; then cut it into small cubes about 2in. long. Place them in a basin, with a tablespoonful of salt; cover, and let stand for twenty minutes. Wash well, then drain. Put them in a sauce- pan with loz. of butter, a pinch of pepper, a teaspoonful of sauce, a bouquet-garni, and a little stock. Braise and drain when cooked. Sliced a peeled onion, toss in butter, and when of a nice colour pour off the butter and mix with the marrow. Stew gently for a few min- utes. Add a little chopped parsley.—" Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>+++•++++++o 0••:••:. +++4.44 + 4.</head>
        <p>+++•++++++o 0••:••:. +++4.44 + 4.</p>
        <p>_ , •:. : Children 8 Corner.:: 0</p>
        <p>o 4. • Conducted by UNCLE NED. 0 • 4. . 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. .:. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4 . 4. 4. 4. 4.</p>
        <p>CHILDREN OF ALL Aozs are cordially invited to join this Corner if they read it regularly. Application for membership, stating name, address and age, should be made to " Uscul NED, 'Examiner' Office, Bewsey- street, Warrington." Every girl or boy, whose name is in the Big Book, is entitled to enter the competitions held every week, to the winners of which useful book-prizes are sent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>ABOUT UNCLE NED.</head>
        <p>ABOUT UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>Mr DEAR NEPHEWS AND NIECES,— Allow me to introduce myself. I am Uncle Hubert. I have nothing whatever up my sleeves—as the conjurers say—but have promised to fill this column in the brief absence of your revered Uncle Ned, and so must set about my first letter to you all, whether I have anything to say or not. Perhaps you are wondering what has become of Uncle Ned. Well, I'll tell you, if you can keep a secret: the dear old fellow has gone off for a fortnight to bathe his poor old corns in the briny ocean. Yes, your old uncle is a gay dog when he goes for his annual holiday. He will laugh with any of you at the niggers on the sands, and splash like a two-year-old when, in his lovely, striped bathing costume, he goes for a dip. Indeed, I'm not so sure he would turn up his nose at building castles atid digging moats and channels for the water to come, along. At any rate, it's not so very long since be used to enjoy this sort of thing, just as much as you do.</p>
        <p>Of course, you won't any of you let Uncle Ned know that I've been giving him away. Not that it would matter a great deal, because I have known our dear old friend since he was a boy, and am therefore allowed to talk about him in a way that other people would have no right. He's such a charming old fellow that I really couldn't say anything about him that I thought would seriously offend him. It is be- cause he is so old a friend of mine that I have undertaken to perform his very pleasant task till be comes back to the office as brown as a black pudding and fairly bursting to do some more work. Well, it will Bowl be your :Uncle</p>
        <p>Hubert's turn to go for a holiday, and then, I suppose if Uncle Ned finds out that I have told you about his corns and his striped bathing costume, he'll pay me back by telling you all about how bald I am.</p>
        <p>Well, we all have our tender points, but I'll tell you a story so that Uncle Ned can't tell it against me. I remember how, when I was standing on the top of Blackpool Tower, talk- ing to some lady friends, my hat blew—but, there, I'll leave it to Uncle Ned, or else there'll be no room for all those nice letters that nephews and nieces have sent this week.</p>
        <p>"A DEAR LITTLE MOUNTAIN PLACE." As you all know, the subject of last week's essay was: "how I would like to spend my holidaYs." A number of excellent little com- positions have come to hand, but most of the essayists have told me how they have spent previous holidays, or what arrangements have been made for their holidays this year. It is all very interesting, indeed, but the idea was for you to think•of what you; would most like to do and where you would most like to go, and then write all about it. So, you see, whilst there are several efforts that are in some ways as good as that which wins the prize, I am tak- ing into account the fact that Niece Lilian Himsworth has kept more closely to the sub- ject. Lilian's letter is as follows:— "15, Fitzherbert-street, Warrington, July 18th, 1915. " Dear Uncle Ned,—You asked me how I want to spend my holidays. Well, if I my choice I would like to spend my holidays in a dear little mountain place somewhere in Wales. It is always my greatest pleasure to climb and revel among the hills, but I suppose that is because I have lived the greater part of my life among the hills.— Yours, with love, from your loving niece, LILIAN HIMSWORTII. "P.S.—My brother and I would like to know our numbers." Very nice, Lilian! I believe you are as fond of the hills as your old Uncle Hubert is. Have you and your brother had no numbers yet? If not, yours will be 464, and his 465.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>HERE AND THERE.</head>
        <p>HERE AND THERE.</p>
        <p>In Denmark girls insure against becoming old maids.</p>
        <p>As a general rule, *heti tt.giri has hoatitiful hands it isn't any sign that her 'nether has.</p>
        <p>An Ideal Guest. She was a most accommodating old lady as this incident at an afternoon tea proved: "Will you have sugar?" she was asked. "If it's in, leave it in," she replied, "and if it's out, leave it out. It doesn't make a bit of difference to me."</p>
        <p>The Meaning of It. Unsophisticated Cook: If you please, mum, the butcher says 111 get 5 per cent. on all the orders I give him. What does that mean? Mistress: It means, Mary, that we shall have another butcher.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>Before and After.</head>
        <p>Before and After.</p>
        <p>"Booth Tarkington, at a theatrical supper, spoke rather well," said a playwright, "on marriage. One remark in his speech struck me particularly by its epigrammatic truth. It was this:</p>
        <p>" ' Before she marries him, a girl's opinion of a young man is the same as his mother's; after marriage she comes round rather to his father's view.'"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>GOOD EXAMPLE.</head>
        <p>A GOOD EXAMPLE.</p>
        <p>Here is a letter to cheer me up—and we all want cheering up now and then, especially in these dark days:— " 6, Milner-street, Warrington, 18th July, 1915. "Dear Uncle Ned,—You asked me to write about how I would spend my holidays_ I always try to have a new experience. I go to places where I have not been before to find things out that I did not know. Some- times I .make a tent and go for a day in a field near the river. I cannot spend every minute on myself, because I have to bring</p>
        <p>the food for my elders, who are working. You don't like long essays, and so I will draw to a close.—From your loving nephew, SAM BACKHOUSE. "I have got three new, members—my bro- ther William, aged 9; James Chetter, 12, Milner-street, aged 11; .Tames Ernest Ash- worth, 5, Goulden-street, aged 13 years."</p>
        <p>_ Bravo, Sam; you have set a. good example. Your brother's number will be 466; James Chetter can be 467; and the other James we will call 468.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>HINTS ON HEALTH.</head>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTH.</p>
        <p>Useful Information In Popular</p>
        <p>Language.</p>
        <p>Infant Mortality. In Dr. Dharmavir's experience injudicious feeding is the most common cause of infant deaths. "An infant begins to cry with pain as a result either of gulping down milk toO quickly, or of feeding on bread and milk, arrowroot or other starchy food, or some patent food. After vainly endeavouring to still the cries of the infant, the mother rushes to the chemist in order to seek relief by means of soothing syrup, which, though it soothes the child to sleep for a short time, ultimately undermines its health. Matters do not im- prove, and the child succumbs to diarrhoea, &amp;c. It must be borne in mind that a little advice with regard to the feeding, if properly carried out, would, in a great majority of cases, save the child much suffering and the mother many a sleepless night and the ultimate loss of her child." Dr. Dharmavir has tried the effect of sending leaflets to mothers, after receipt of the weekly returns of registered births. But too often the advice arrives after the mischief has been done, and he considers earlier notification of births and the appointment of a health visi- tor urgently needed in his district.—" Medical ()tam"</p>
        <p>Oltieable rood nrd4oll bread used in place of white will help kilt) food value of a meal. Wholemeal makes more nourishing puddings, cakes, 'sauces, than does white bour. The steaming of vegetables, instead of boiling, will prevent the most valuable part of these foods being lost. If boiling be preferred, the water in which they have been boiled should be utilised for scup stock, just iu the same way as meat stoat is used.</p>
        <p>Potatoes should always be boiled, baked, or steamed in their skins.</p>
        <p>Beans, peas, lentils, cheese or nuts contai% weight for weight, more nutriment than do beef, mutton or poultry. Buttermilk is a cheap and valuable food. A. pennyworth of buttermilk, served with pota- toes, is a cheap and wholesome dish. Dried fruits, such as dates, sultanas, figs, &amp;c., eaten raw, or stewed, with wholemeal bread, are enjoyed by children, and form an excellent meal.</p>
        <p>Fresh fruits are wholesome and palatable food and should be bought when possible. The fruit acids act as cleansing agents for the alimentary canal.</p>
        <p>Fresh salads of uncooked vegetables, such as cabbage, red or white, onions, carrots, turnips, &amp;c., grated, or finely shredded, are wholesome, cheap, and purifying. Hard cheese is more digestible if grated. Cer- tain people can eat cheese prepared in this way, who cannot digest it in any other form.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>HOLIDAY RESOLUTION.</head>
        <p>A HOLIDAY RESOLUTION.</p>
        <p>An admirable letter is the following : "11, Heathside, Warrington, July 19th. "Dear Uncle Ned,—l was very _pleased ,to</p>
        <p>see that my friend Marian received- the prize , for our last competition. I will, now tell yo# ' what I am going to do 'during the holidays. First of all 1 am going to try and answer all the competitions that occur during the four weeks. Neat Thursday lam going to visit one of your nieces who lives in the country. I, will not ,forget. our soldiers and sailors at my holidays. My brothel. and Tare going to Frodsham for a day; we will enjoy ourselves picking blackberries. While I have a chance I will go into the country as much as pos- sible. I will now close because I know you cannot bear long letters.—l remain, your affectionate niece, No. 381, MONA ARCHER. "P.S.—Please let my brother know his</p>
        <p>number; his name. is Maurice Archer."</p>
        <p>I envy you, Mona. When you are out black- berrying, think of your poor old Uncle, whose blackberrying days are over. And mind you don't bring all the fruit home inside you, or else you may have a bad pain Alas! bow your uncle wishes he could have blackberry- pain instead of gout. Oh! I nearly forgot Maurice's number. Tell him he is 469.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>LOVER OF NATURE.</head>
        <p>A LOVER OF NATURE.</p>
        <p>Here we have a good letter from a Nature- lover :</p>
        <p>"2, Guclfrey-atreet, Warrington, July 18th, 1915.</p>
        <p>"Dear Uncle Ned,—My brother and I have had scarlet fever, which has prevented me from entering the competitions, but I am sending you an essay on 'How I want to spend my holidays.' "First, the war has prevented people from going far from home, so I shall content my- self with long rambles in the woods, and try and study Nature in all its aspects, and also reading good books, which will improve my mind. I will do a bit of gardening also. I must close now, hoping to have a very aappy time.—From your loving nephew, HAROLD JONES.</p>
        <p>"P.S.—Will you please Jet me knew my I number?"</p>
        <p>Yes, Harold; yol: are number 470.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>Tburrij Reins.</head>
        <p>Tburrij Reins.</p>
        <p>BISHOP AT ST. BARNABAS'.</p>
        <p>Some Of The Needs Of The Church And Of The Parish.</p>
        <p>As was briefly reported in our last issue, the Bishop of Liverpool (the Rev. Dr. Chavasse) paid a visit of inspection to St. Barnabas' Churoh and Parish, and subsequently preached to a large congregation in the church. After referring to several small improvements which had been carried out in the church during the last fifteen years, his lordship went on to say that he had also visited the school and he warmly thanked Mr. Walker for the admirable work which was being carried on there. He was very much impressed by the brightness and eagerness displayed by the children, who bade fair to fill with credit the places of the older people in the parish work. The only thing lacking at the Sunday school was a sufficiency of adult teachers, and he hoped that while some of the men were away fighting for King and country, others would step forward and fill their places. The Bishop then spoke of a few of the needs of the,' church. One' thing wanted was money to enable the church to be completed. A curate was also badly wanted, it being impossible for the vicar to alone cope with the work of such a large parish. He also thought that the vicar should receive a larger stipend than he at present got. His Lordship added that he was sorry to hear that recently there had not been good congregations at the church. Surely at this awful time they should all hear the voice of God calling, beseeching them to come and worship in His house.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>"INTO THE HIGHWAYS</head>
        <p>"INTO THE HIGHWAYS</p>
        <p>Free Church Council Hold Successful</p>
        <p>Street Meeting.</p>
        <p>A successful open-air meeting arranged by the Warrington and District Free Church Council was held around the Market Steps, in Corporation-street, after church-time on Sunday night, and was attended by some three hundred people. Mr. Thomas Dale presided, and in the course of a few prefatory remarks ventured to think that the Free Church Council had no, need to apologise for holding a meeting in that fashion on a Sunday night. They had come out, as it were, "into the highways and hedges" to tell men of the good news of God and of the mercy vouchsafed to the children of men, through the Lord and Master, Jesus chxist.</p>
        <p>The Apeaker was the Rev. J. S. Chessum, Primitive Methodist minister, who spoke upon the roomier, the more spacious life into which the Christian man and woman were ushered. He drew attention to the fact that no man could be content with a limited sphere and a very narrow environment, and argued, from that, that it was the duty of every, man to se- cure for himself as large a horizon and as roomy a world as he could possibly find. There was no horizon that was so wide, there was no life that was so full as that of the Christian. In coming out into the streets that night the Free Churches had no personal ends to serve, and they were certainly not studying their own conveniences, but they were, purely and simply. out to make known to all men the wonderful roominess, spaciousness, of the Kingdom of God.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>LESSON OF THE FLOWERS.</head>
        <p>LESSON OF THE FLOWERS.</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. Ogden on Divine Prodigality.</p>
        <p>Special and highly successful "flower" ser- vices were held at the Golborne-street Baptist Church on Sunday, when the preacher for the day was the Rev. H. Ogden (pastor). Large congregations were the order and special and appropriate music was rendered by the choir. In the morning the Pastor preached from the words: "The flowers appear in the earth," and expressed the opinion that, in many ways, the Song of Solomon, from which his text was taken, was most exquisite and choice. For long there was contention as to whether the Song should be received into the Canon, because then, as now, there was, and still was, the possibility of misreading the purpose of the writer. But surely, said the preacher, a book was not to be blamed for the thumb-marks that were upon it, and the moral was not to be measured merely by the mind of the reader. If a person of depraved mind were to read the Song of Solomon he might take away dtpriwed ideas, but "to the pure, all thing Were pure Turning to the subject of his text, the preacher said that the flowers were always a wonder to the e:,-t that could see and the mind that could perceive.</p>
        <p>Amazing Confratt IN Man's Utilitarianism. Th gardbii and the flowers excited in the huntbie mind a thrill of delight and wonder; they helped men and women still to live in ,a wonderland, and from them they could draw a lesson as to the prodigality of God to His crea- tures. The flowers were over and above the ordinary measure of the means of life; they were proof of the fact that God was no mere utilitarian, but that His bounty was prodigal. The flowers were, so to speak, the lobelia bor- der to the garden of God's wonderful munifi- cence. Utilitarianism seemed to mark all the ways of men and God's prodigal gifts were in amazing contrast. CHILDREN'S FLORAL OFFERINGS.</p>
        <p>In the afternoon Mrs. Moores presided over a special children's " flower " service, an in- teresting feature of which was the number of bunches of flowers that were brought by the scholars of the Sunday school. These were</p>
        <p>received by two of the teachers—the Misete Freeman and Bamford—and they were artis- tioally grouped round the pulpit by Mr* Bucksby. In the course of a short address.: Mrs. Moores expressed her delight at bell*" present and said that the flowers spoke to men :and women. and -children of the goodness_lf God, telling them, among other -things, of N4lO delight in colour, fragrance and abundance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>Lovelier Blooms Than Garden Grows.</head>
        <p>Lovelier Blooms Than Garden Grows.</p>
        <p>- -- - Miss Dutton, of Moore, was the speaker, WI she emphasised the fact that, lovely as the, 'flowers were, there were still lovelier bloonaß than those that were to be found in the ordin- ary garden. Those lovelier blooms were the children themselves, who were like the blO5 some that, being guarded and nurtured, would eventually grow into beautiful fruit. Several appropriate hymns were sung by thf scholars.</p>
        <p>"Consider the Lities -."</p>
        <p>"Consider the lilies of the field; how they grow," was the text taken by the Pastor for his evening sermon, in the course of which be' said that the lilies, which symbolised fat flowers' grew without the fret and strain szta worry that characterised the labours of man, and that there was no sedative for the mai" whose nerves were jaded or shattered like rest- ing ,amid:the flowers, the fields,- and the woods- Duiiiig the evening service Miss Bates 84111 P a suitable solo.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>'TEMPERANCE QUESTION.-</head>
        <p>'TEMPERANCE QUESTION.-</p>
        <p>The Churches Unite On The Work Of</p>
        <p>Constructive Reform. We learn that a notable decision respecting Temperance Reform was reached at a recent meeting of representatives appointed by the Temperance Committees of the Christian - Churches.</p>
        <p>The Temperance Organisations of ten Chris- tian Denominations have united in the fortna- tion of a Temperance Council of the Christian Churches of England and Wales. Invitations' were issued to the Archbishop of Canterburf., Cardinal Bourne, the Rev. W. B. Selbie, aIY and General Bramwell Booth, to serve aff Presidents of the Council, and each has sine&amp; accepted the invitation. The new Temperance Council of the Chris- tian Churches has come into being as a result" of the recent public discussions of the liquor question, and of the conviction that in the near future—either at the close' of the war, or earlier—a determined and united effort should be made by: the religous forces of the country to secure adequate legislative treatment of the drink problem. In addition to a concerted at- tempt to obtain legislation, the Council is authorised to act in respect of other aspects of _the drink problem, and a Committee is al- ready at work on the subject of Constructive Temperance Reform. The comprehensive character of the new Council is shown by the list of its constitOnt Temperance organisations. These are The Baptist Total Abstinence Society. The Catholic Total Abstinence League of the Cross The Church of England Temperance Society' The Friends Temperance Union. The Presbyterian Church of England Teta- perance Committee. The Presbyterian Church of Wales Temper- ance Committee. The Primitive Methodist Temperance Cl2' mittee. The Salvation Army. . The United Methodist Temperance COlll- - The Wesleyan Methodist Temperance Cora' mittee. • The Chairman of ,the new Council is the Bishop of Croyden, and the Revs. Dr. Scott Lidgett and Sir Alfred Pearce Gould are the Vice-Chairman. The joint Honorary Secre- taries are the Rev. Henry Carter (SecretarY of the Wesleyan Methodist Temperance Cole - mittee), the Rev. J. F. Moyniham (Secretarl of the Catholic Total Abstinence League of the Cross), and the Rev. Gerald A. ThompseA (Secretary of the Church of England Temper- ance Society.) The resclution constituting the Council , which was adopted at the meeting convened , 1 for that purpose, is as follows: . "This meeting of reprc:sentatives, duly OP-- pointed and authorised for the purpose, herebY resolves itself- into the Temperance Council of the Christian Churches of England and Wales' The purpose of the Council shall he united' action to secure legislative and other TemPer- once Reform., The Council shall be representa- tive of the Temperance organisations of the, Christian Churches. The work of the Council shall be of a strictly non-party character.".</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>SUNDAY SCHOLARS. ;</head>
        <p>SUNDAY SCHOLARS. ;</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Conference Discuss itt</p>
        <p>Continued Decline.</p>
        <p>At a meeting held on Monlay at Birminghtiel in connection with the Wesleyan Conference the Rev. J. W. Butcher, dealing with the ques- tion of the Sunday school, said that there were signs of brightness in the report, but sole° ominous signs which they must face . They re- ported a decrease for the ninth year, but he he' lieved that if they would work their machine: the decline could be stopped. He had 0° intention of suggesting new legislation. Several speakers urged the importance ministers taking more part in the Sundw schodi work. Mr. Redman, of Hebden Bridge, urged that ministers should give less time t° trying to" vitalise fossilised seniors" and tall their attention to the young people. A motion. was carried requesting the Pastoral Session t° - recommend superintendents to place the Stlex day school services on the circuit plan tUI regularly appoint ministerial visitation. Wesley Guild. The Rev. W. B. Fitzgerald brought up tI affairs of the Wesley Gaills. There are guilds with a membership of 140,856. other young people's societies number 364, with 'll membership of 17,000. The Rev. T. E. Westerdale suggested that, pending the decision of the committee 101 unifying of the young people's departments, tn designation of the guild secretary be deferr for a year. This was seconded, and gave rise Lv.</p>
        <p>a li7els, debate. The Rev. D. Lidgett and J. A. Sharp , ously opposed the amendment. The vol.' placed the Rev. W. Fitzgerald as Secretarr designate for 1916, and the nomination o.° passed on to the Pastoral Session.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>KING AND THE CHILDREN'</head>
        <p>THE KING AND THE CHILDREN'</p>
        <p>The Ring, as Patron of the National Socicti for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children, 11116; through Lord Stamfordham, sent a graciou message to the Director of the Society, exPre6„sl ing His Majesty's gratification at "the 10Y-f and devoted services given by the members c'd the staff of the Society, both at the Front en] at home during this time of great nationxa: emergency." One-fifth of the Society's ins Pet, tors are with the Colours, and sixty per Ce4o of the total eligible members of the office are serving with the tiorces.</p>
        <p>A Delicate Position. "This, then, Miss Jones," said the 10 51 man, as he started for the doors "is your fin- „._</p>
        <p>decision?" " It is, Mr. Wick," said the young girl firli3l/„T; "Then" he replied, his voice betraying 1,, unnatural calmness, "there is._ but one 0° - -</p>
        <p>thing to add." ' senti/ " What is that?" she asked, toying ab with the lobe of her shell-like ear. T retnril .` It is this," he muttered. "Shall . .-- vou those black satin suspenders by mail or vri" '</p>
        <p>have them • now?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>POULTRY PEN,.</head>
        <p>POULTRY PEN,.</p>
        <p>WINITs AND INTERESTING FACTS AND CHATS FOR AMATEURS,</p>
        <p>PRACTICAL POINTERS.</p>
        <p>TO meet the strain of feather growth it is essential to feed generously on nourishing nlaterials. Birds kept in close confinement generally raoult earlier than those on a free grass range. t. Chickens will be growing very rapidly at this flille• With the present high price of poultry L00d; weeding out the surplus youngsters should commenced as soon- as possible. „In confined runs all green food not eaten by "le next morning must be removed, and not Cowed to lie about and rot. Let the birds have plenty of shelter and ;:bade during these .hot days. We often get ,:eavY rains even in summer, and the birds tilire some sort of retreat where they can "01) dry. -Iteating foods, such as barley, maize or maize 11'teal should be carefully avoided, especially for birds kept in confined runs. The food should, -wever, be varied as much as possible, as p°riety in diet goes a long way towards pro- Ilging egg production.—" Poultry World."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>ECONOMICAL RATIONS.</head>
        <p>ECONOMICAL RATIONS.</p>
        <p>- - Since war broke out last August we have rau studying and discussing economical rations Poultry-keeping almost every week through tl .118 column. We have discovered valuable 400de which before had rarely been' used for 0?-oltry diet, and which have proved more effi- t, probably, than our original luxurious Ladnne• It is indeed a fact that personally it Itetually costing me less to feed my flock than " did in normal times, and the birds have done 4„inte as well, if not better, than formerly. I a staunch believer in good feeding, and if i; PaY6 to keel) stock at all it certainly pays r f: , ' feed well. But what may be considered good 'euing though the food supplied may be of the ~t quality, may be altogether unbalanced, and :pilsequently wasteful. Such a diet is not only ‘:)8t137 in itself, but it is provocative of dis- '"e, and reduces the birds to an unprofitable odition. This was the effect created by the starchy diet we were all accustomed to, ~.7crre, or less, in normal times, and which re- Zpted in an unnatural fatty condition of the giving birth to liver trouble and its 'mod complaints. To meet the times I last year experimented ‘"gely with • brewers' offals—i.e., brewers' grains, malt cubes, &amp;c., in conjunction with n, harps, bran, and some biscuit meal; whilst Later I employed fish meal and Soya meal. tgiscuit meal has become almost prohibitive in Price, but I have had a blend of the above foods in constant use for nearly twelve months with most satisfactory results indeed. I not only used it effectually for egg production, but in a tuodified form I used it also for chicken raising. In this latter respect it has been particularly eessful, and I never remember having young- eters do better. the, 'have said, I have frequently advocated Wastee of' the more bulky food in brewers' AgriTitil,nilets for poultry feeding. A Board iti feedin,„ ;ire pamphlet, in discussing economy spent includes brewers' grains, Malt calms, tr,exttei4'ilye,aan and dried yeast, and the two for- says, can be used suitably to bulkv of one-third of the wet mash.„ Being ninsll,:oode, more concentrated substances pose. I' need in chnjunction, and. for this pur- Inast' rnYeelf find SOya bean meal and fish meal therecinseful both in price and effect.—" Fea- World."</p>
        <p>CHICKENS FOR MARKET. 411,_ the available chickens should be mar- • immediatelY they are large enough, for „e, sooner that they Can he turned into money ill!: better, especially with feeding stuffs so in price. During the three weeks imme- i atelY prior to killing they should be placed 114 a cage or coop, for the less exercise they ete, the more rapidly do they add 'on *flesh. ullring the twenty-four hours immediately fol.; saving being 'placed in the coop no food what- or is given to the birds, since this gives them • gc'od' appetite for their first meal amid their and somewhat strange surroundings. At „ °tend of this time, when the birds are thor- -411ct,„-nIY hungry, they are given their first meal, this should consist of ground oats mixed 1.%1th skim milk. There is no food to equal this Its flesh-forming properties. rt is by no ii--eane a cheap food, but it produces flesh of infierb colour, texture, and flavour, and at the T:lne time there is an almost entire absence of ~.80 objectionable a feature in many fattened 'c'ene For cheaper specimens other foods b 0 used, such as barley-meal, middlings, nlaize meal, but these all produce corn- -11,-it.atlvely poor quality flesh. There is really tlaZting so good as ground oats, and it pays to Rh' notwithstanding its rather high. price. It thnuld be mixed with sour. milk, and given to I,Lhirds of the consfttency of very thick cream. th Ing should take place twice daily, dividing tra-„t•laY as nearly as possible into two equal 8; convenient hours are 6.30 a.m. and 5.30 T• he birds should be allowed to eat as 40" en as they like at a time, but when their *-111'!etites show signs of being satisfied, any food remains should be removed, and no more till the following meal. Eating between p,za is very bad • indeed for the - fowls.- 131 and Garden."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>SOLDIER-HARVESTERS.</head>
        <p>SOLDIER-HARVESTERS.</p>
        <p>Von Ihitt • °wing the Army Council's announcement rini iltinctb:urlough will be granted to a c lori rof soldiers of the Regular and Terri- tieo44,Porces for work in the harvest condi- 4rh,e 'lave been. issued which provide that the ht Will undertake to pay each soldier svit (:04_e, farmer's request to any of the following tillit.—"es. Cambridgeshire, Essex, Huntingcont: laocie. Isle of Ely, Lincolnshire, Norfolk, East ktikd 'Joke of Peterborough, Suffolk, the Ridings of Yorkshire, 6s. a day for hoi !taw irs if the soldier provides his own bioa_lo,ll. littg "ging, or 4s. 6d. a day if board and od„ goo,tti,ro provided by the farmer, and 6d. an or overtime.• ,177441er,„ remaining counties in England and 18.-a day Of ten hours, or 35. 6d. a day. kter—'ll. and lodging are provided by the far- ,4l„ll) Ications Tid sd. an hour for overtime. from farmers who desire to Y. soldiers in the harvest must be mae tabocilni ae possible to the Board of Trade he till' Exchankes, when the application will Por,l'a•nsmitted to the military authorities. 'clears for the pOrpose are obtainable from the ‘4l3oiir Exchange, the address of which '43 obtained from the nearest Post Office.-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>COVERED YARDS FOR CATTLE..</p>
        <p>Few farmers would be found to dissent from the view that covered or partially covered yards or courts are better than open yards for the fattening of cattle. A roof keeps the animals dry, warm and comfortable. Without using an excessive amount of litter, the floor can be kept clean and dry, which gives the cattle every inducement to lie down; and every farmer likes to see them rest after feeding, so that they may digest their rations and gain the greatest benefit from them. Nor does the advantage end with the cattle themselves; there is the manure to be considered. One of the chief objects in fattening cattle on most farms is to convert the home-grown roots, hay and straw into manure. It is, therefore, important that this manure should be made as good as possible. In an open yard it is difficult to avoid the washing out of the valuable soluble in- fradients of the manure, which is consequently seldom as -rich as that from covered yards.— Agricultural Economist and Horticultural Review."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>SEEING RESULTS.</head>
        <p>SEEING RESULTS.</p>
        <p>Last summer a farmer reported that he set for his two little girls the task of cutting out the dandelions in the door-yard. He was to give them 10 cents. a square rod. They worked at times during one day and then gave it up, discouraged, declaring that they just could not get every one, and that they could hardly see the effects of their work. They did about 10 cents. worth. Last week a howling Iwind- storm scattered little twigs from the trees all over this farmer's yard. These would inter- fere with the lawn-mower, so another bargain was made with the girls. This time it hap- pened that he offered them 5 cents. for each basketful of twigs they would gather up. That evening there were two baskets filled with twigs, and the girls proudly announced that there were no more twigs to be found. Each received a bright nickel and raced away to put it in her little bank. The father made a hurried inspection of the yard, and sure enough they had gathered all the sticks. Then they built a fire with the twigs and while the girls watched the flameS he reasoned on their work. It simmered down to this: It was hard to see where the dandelions had come out of the big yard, but it was easy to see the sticks accumu- late in the basket. He would try dandelions again the new way. A bargain was struck at 10 cents. a bushel for cutting dandelions and sure enough they made them fly. They could see results then. That was the secret. We all like to see results whether it is in making an income from the farm or picking up sticks.— "Breeders' Gazette."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>LAND SETTLEMENT FOR SAILORS AND</head>
        <p>LAND SETTLEMENT FOR SAILORS AND</p>
        <p>SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>The President of the Board of. Agriculture and Fisheries has appointed a Departmental Com- mittee to consider and report what steps,. can be taken to promote the settlement or employ- ment on the land in England and Wales of sailors and soldiers, whether disabled or other- wise, on discharge from the Navy or Army. The committee will be constituted as follows: Sir Harry Verney, Bart.,. M.P. (chairman), Earl of Northbrook, Right Hon. Henry Hob- house, Major-General Sir Charles Crutchley, K.C.V.0., and Messrs. Sydney Mager, Vaughan Nash, C.V.0., C. 8., F. H. Padwick, G. H. Roberts, M.P., and Leslie Scott, K.C., M.P., The secretary of the committee will be Mr. F. L. C. Floud, assistant secretary of the Board of Agriculture and Fisheries, to whom all com- munications should be sent at 4, Whitehall- place, S.W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>CANTERBURY BELLS.</p>
        <p>Recently when visiting a country garden where large breadths of these are grown, we were much struck by their value when cut for placing in large vases for the house. We have also noticed how useful they are in town gardens and how beautiful' in their varied colours. We visited a friend the other day in whose garden they were largely grown, and though surrounded by houses the plants were blooming freely and promised to keep up a long succession. This old favourite deserves- more attention,- for wherever placed the plants when in bloom are always effective, and especially so when in large groups, as in the country garden referred to. Beeds sown thinly now in boxes will make strong plants, that can be pricked out where they are to flower, but if Space can be spared, it is bet- ter to prick the seedlings out for a time to allow them to gain strength previous to putting the plants into their permanent positions. Many so-called improved forms have been raised, but to my wird ...ne-e Is nothing to °qua: for effect a good strain of single forms. The mllites are pure, the pink .hades vn-y delicate, also t-r.se of the lilacs, while a c deep blue forms an. telling. Another advantage is that the flowers when cut last in good cqndition for some time, while another point in their favour is that the plants come into bloom when flowers are some- times scarce in gardens.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>ANNUALS.</head>
        <p>ANNUALS.</p>
        <p>Lately in a large garden, the owner of which grows annuals largely, we were much struck with the beauty of the cornflower, sown_in_the autumn. The following, too, in spite of the dry weather, were in fine bloom. For a really fine blue there was nothing to equal the Gentianette (Phacelia campanularia), seen as a ground work to tea roses. This is one of the best of hardy annuals. Convolvulus minor was in fine bloom. Among drought-resisting annuals may be mentioned the Godetia and Bartonia aurea, while worthy of note was Linaria maroccona, which at one time we used to grow largely as an edging plant. Eschscholtzias, too, deserve attention, while dwarf nasturtiums also are very useful.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>SWEET PEAS.</head>
        <p>SWEET PEAS.</p>
        <p>If you want to prolong the season of bloom- ing of the sweet pea then gather the flowers very closely, so as to prevent the formation of seed pods, as nothing checks the growth so quickly. See to it, too, that the plants are well nourished, by placing a mulch of rotton manure alongside the rows so as to invigorate the plants, and thus pave the way for a succession of the welcome flowers.—Wick, in "Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>BOYS IN THE HARVEST FIELD.</head>
        <p>BOYS IN THE HARVEST FIELD.</p>
        <p>A Question Of Fair Wages.</p>
        <p>The Board of Education last week issued a circular-letter to local education authorities drawing their attention, to a reply by the President of the Board of Education, made in the House of Commons on the Bth inst., to a question by Mr. Noel Buxton, asking whether he had taken steps to secure the payment of a fair wage to children of school age employed in agricultural work. The circular says that in Mr.. Henderson's opinion the local education authorities which take the responsibility of excusing boys from school in order to serve the special needs of the country in the present emergency cannot reasonably disclaim responsibility for seeing that fair wages are paid to the boys thus ex- ceptionally excused from attendance. He re- cognised that the matter is not easy to deal with either in detail or by fixed rule, but apart from all other questions of humanity and fairness he regards it as indisputable that un- less it is worth the while of the employer to pay something substantial for the services of a boy those services cannot be of substantial value to the ,country, and that in the interests of the boy and of the country as a whole he had better be at,' school.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>MEDICINE SHORTAGE CAUSES RISE IN PRICES.</head>
        <p>MEDICINE SHORTAGE CAUSES RISE IN PRICES.</p>
        <p>One of the consequences of the war has been to reduce considerably the import of certain medicines known as synthetic—that is artificial —drugs, and naturally to raise prices. This happily does not apply in any way to the well- known remedy, Dr. Cassell's Tablets. The in- gredients of these Tablets are natural products derived from our own colonies and from America, and no shortage is anticipated. Prices remain the same, viz., 104 d., 1/1k and 2/9, at all chemists. For the nerve strain which this terrible war entails there is no better sustainer than Dr. Cassell's Tablets. They should' be taken for all run down conditions, for loss of energy, sleep- lessness, irritability and other indications of nerve disturbance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>EXAMINER, 'SATURDAY,'</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, 'SATURDAY,'</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AGRICUL- TURAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Entries Close Monday.</p>
        <p>We draw our readers' attention to the closing of .entries for ,the above, which is on Monday next, the 26th inst. •</p>
        <p>The show, which is held this year in the Brewery Field, Wilderspool, on August sth, is Confined to Shire horses and dairy cattle only. The public will be admitted at 9 a.m. on pay- ment of is.. each, and pass-outs ;will be issued up to one o'clock, with. re-admission later. The judging of dairy cattle will commence at 10 o'clock, and the judging of Shire horses at 1.30.</p>
        <p>There will be-a parade about 3.30. ' Entries to be made at the Walton Estate Office.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>GREAT CAUSE.</head>
        <p>A GREAT CAUSE.</p>
        <p>Sir Gilbert Greenall's Appeal For Ally Farmers.</p>
        <p>Sir Gilbert Greenall has issued the following circular on behalf of the Warrington and District Agricultural Society and the Warring- ton Shire Horse Society in aid of the Agricul- tural Relief of Allies Fund :</p>
        <p>Walton Hall, Warrington. Dear Sir,—You are doubtless aware that the Royal Agricultural Society of England has initiated a fund for the relief of our brave Allies in those countries which have been devastated by the war, and for their rein- statement on their holdings after the war is Over. '</p>
        <p>Absolute ruin has followed in the track of the armies of Germany and Austria, the whole of the countries which have been overrun by these hordes •of men having been entirely wrecked. To assist the unfortunate farmers to put their buildings and lands in order after the war is the desire and aim of this appeal which through the Royal Agricultural Society is made to agriculturists and all interested in the land.</p>
        <p>His Majesty the King is Patron and Duke of Portland, 8.G., is President of the fund, and the circular issued by the committee and signed by the Duke of Portland, very clearly gives the vital reason for our sincere sympathy and liberal support:— "The state of affairs prevailing in various parts of the theatre of war, and especially in Belgium, is one which we find it very difficult to picture and understand. It is probable, indeed, that we could never understand it fully unless we saw our buildings and crops going up in flames; our ,servants and perhaps our families maltreated or killed, and our cattle butchered or driven away by the invader." All the counties are helping in this great work, and it has been decided that the Coun- ties of Lancaster and Chester shall combine to make a grand collection through their various Agricultural and kindred Societies; a Central Committee has been formed by the Federated Society of Lancashire and Cheshire Agricultural Societies, with the Hon. Arthur Stanley, M.P., as President; Mr. Birtwistle as Hon. Secretary; Mr. E. Bohane, Hon. Treasurer; your represen- tatives on that committee being Mr. Roger C. Parr and Mr. Joshua Ball. All sums collected will be credited ,to their various counties, and I hope that Lancashire and Cheshire will ex- ceed all others in the amount subscribed. I appeal to you to give to this fund liberally, as a large sum will be needed.</p>
        <p>We have been mercifully saved from the un- speakable atrocities from twhich they have suf- fered; our prorierty has not been wrecked, our families are safe, and our work and markets are going on_much as usual.</p>
        <p>_ You can, I am 'sure, give something out of your plenitude to assist in the gigantic task of reinstating the occupiers of land, and to purchase for them machinery, implements, stock, seeds and other necessaries, all of which have been entirely swept away. lam relying upon the agriculturists of this district to give liberally for this great cause. Donations may 'lib sent to me, or to Mr. W. Bainbridge, at the Walton Estate Office, near Warrington.—Yours faithfully,</p>
        <p>GILBERT GREENALL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>HAY FOR HIS MAJESTY'S FORCES.</head>
        <p>HAY FOR HIS MAJESTY'S FORCES.</p>
        <p>Official Assurances.</p>
        <p>In consequence of the erroneous idea which prevails in some districts that the Military Authorities intend to commandeer the stocks of hay in the country at whatever price they deem reasonable, and -without regard to the amount required for use on the farm, the President of the Board of Agriculture and Fisheries desires to make known as widely as possible the policy of the Military Authorities in respect of the acquisition of hay.' I.—The Military Authorities are ready to buy at its fair market value any suitable hay which is offered to their Purchasing Officers by the grower.</p>
        <p>2.—lt is not the intention to acquire hay which is proved to be wanted for the use of the stock on a farm, and instructions have been issued to Purchasing Officers not to requisition hay actually required by a farmer for the use of his animals. Should any question arise on this point it is to be referred at once to the Forage Committee, who will take immediate steps to deal with it.</p>
        <p>3.—Soft meadow hay and clear clover hay is not required, as a rule, for Army purposes but should seed mixture, sainfoin, lucerne, or up- land meadow hay be purchased by anyone be- fore it has been offered to and refused by the Military Authorities, such hay will be at once requisitioned. 4.—in view of the short hay crop this year the Military Authorities have decided to reduce materially the quantity of hay purchased in this country. This should insure an adequate supply of hay remaining for farm stock, and prevent inflation of prices, and it is possible that after a lapse of a few months the Military Authori- ties will not be large buyers in the home market at all.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>Value of Requisitioned Hay.</head>
        <p>Value of Requisitioned Hay.</p>
        <p>s.—Under the Army Acts 1879 to 1915, any difference respecting the amount of payment when hay has been requisitioned shall be deter- mined by a County Court judge, but with the object of preventing the inconvenience which an appeal to the County Court may cause, the Army Council have appointed for England and Wales a Committee for the Northern Area and one for the Southern Area, for the purpose of hearing the views of the owner on the value of the .hay requisitioned from bin and the views of the District .Vurchasing Officer on the same. The Committea in each case will give their opinion on the price which should be paid for the hay in question, and this opinion the War Office will accept, but if the owner of the hay is dissatisfied with the opinion of the Committee which has considered his case, it is still open to him to apply to the County .Court judge. 6.—Each Committee will consist of three mem- bers: (1) a farmer selected from one of the Farm Produce County Committees 9f the area concerned; (2) a hay merchant; and (3) an Officer from the Purchasing Establishment of the War Office. The Secretary to each Com- mittee is Lieutenant C. B. Rolfe, 64, Whitehall- court, London, S.W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>CALVES FOR POULTRY FOOD.</head>
        <p>CALVES FOR POULTRY FOOD.</p>
        <p>Lancashire Farmers and Government Order.</p>
        <p>At a council meeting of the Lancashire Farmers' Association, at Preston, repre- senting 3,000 members, ,interesting revela- tions were made concerning the effect of the Board of Agriculture's rec&amp;at order of prohibi- ting the slaughter of calves under twelve weeks old.</p>
        <p>It was stated that farmers could not keep them up to that age and sell them at a profit, and several members supported the statement that large poultry farmers were, buying newly- born calves at 2s. each and boiling the carcases as food for fowls.</p>
        <p>A resolution was adopted urging the Board of Agriculture' to revise the present restric- tions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>DANGEROUS PRACTICE.</head>
        <p>A DANGEROUS PRACTICE.</p>
        <p>"Free Ride" Behind Cart Costs Another Boy His Life.</p>
        <p>The dangerous practice so common amongst young boys of stealing surreptitious "free rides" by clinging to the tail-boards of carts and waggons has once again led to a fatal accident locally, the victim being Richard, the six-year-old son of Private and Mrs. Philip Thompson, whose home is at .2, Hale-street Special sadness attaches to 'the fatality •by reason of the fact that the father of the dead boy is away from home, serving his King and country as a member of the King's Liverpool Regiment.</p>
        <p>• The mother told the jury that, after school on Thursday the child came home for tea and then went out to play. He never returned, and later on, when she made inquiries for him she learned that a small boy had been run over by a motor car and had been taken to the Infirmary. She went to that institution and found that it was her wn. At five o'clock next morning she received word that he had passed away.</p>
        <p>Miss Kate McMahon, a nurse at the Infirm- ary, said that the child, when admitted to the Infirmary, was suffering from concussion of the %rain and died at 3.15 on Friday morning. How the Acc'dent Happened.</p>
        <p>- - Major Pole, a recruiting officer for the 40th Regimental District, who resides in Winmar- leigh-street, stated that at 6.35 on Thursday evening he was driving his motor car, which was of open touring pattern, along Winwick- road. His chauffeur was seated besides him and his wife was in the tonneau. The car was proceeding at about eleven miles an hour. Just after it had passed under the railway bridge witness noticed a brewers' dray coming along the middle of the roael and from the opposite direction. As he approached it his chauffeur drew his attention to the fact that two motor cars were approaching from behind the dray. The first of the other two cars reached the brewer's dray just as witness's car did and it was safely passed, but almost in the same instant the deceased ran out from behind the dray right in front of witness's car. Witness instantly pulled out the clutch, applied the brakes and swerved the car in order to avoid him, but the near side front mudguard struck him and knocked him down. Witness pulled up his car within a distance of six yards and his chauffeur alighted and,, picked up the child. They were unable to find who his parents were and so they took him straight to the Infirmary in the motor car and reported the accident to the police. Witness saw nothing at all of the boy until he ran out from behind the dray right into the track of the car.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Did All He Could to Avoid the Accident.</head>
        <p>Did All He Could to Avoid the Accident.</p>
        <p>In answer to his legal representative, Mr. T. S. Steel, the Major said that he did every, single thing that he possibly could to avoid running into the boy; he could not possibly have done anything else. He had said that he was going eleven miles an hour at the time, but that was an outside figure, and his own personal belief was that he was not going more than nine miles an hour. He was extremely distressed by the accident; it was the first accident he had ever had, and he had been driving a car since 1909. The Major's chauffeur gave corroborative evidence and said that the accident was quite unavoidable.</p>
        <p>Frederick George Hughes, of Grappenhall- road, Stockton Heath, said he was driving a motor taxi-cab behind the brewer's dray. He noticed the deceased and another boy hanging on the chains at the rear or the dray and as he could see that they were in a dangerous position he slackened his speed. Just as witness was about to overtake the dray the two boys let go of the chains and one ran away on one side of the dray and one on the other. The deceased ran out on the right hand side and clean into Major Pole's car, the Major having absolutely no chance to pull up in time to avoid the acci- dent. Witness sounded his motor-horn several times to warn the boys of his approach. The Coroner said that this was one of those sad accidents which could not possibly be avoided; lads would be lads, and they would play about the streets. The jury returned a verdict of "Accidental death," adding a rider that no blame what- soever attached to Major Pole. Mr. Steel, on behalf of the Major, expressed deep sympathy with the parents of the child. and assured the Court that the accident had greatly distressed the Major.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>SWALLOWED A PENNY.</head>
        <p>SWALLOWED A PENNY.</p>
        <p>Ex-Warrington - Fishmonger's</p>
        <p>Strange Suicide.</p>
        <p>The circumstances of the death from self- inflicted injuries of William Jelf, aged 44—a labourer, of Doddenham, near Worcester, and recently engaged in the fish trade in Warring- ton—were inquired into at an inquest held at Worcester Infirmary on Tuesday. The evidence of the widow was to the effect that Jelf went to Doddenham, with his family of eight children, about two months ago, hav- ing previously been in the fish trade in War- rington—a trade he had been compelled to give up owing to the war. Latterly he had been engaged picking fruit, and he had not seemed well, the loss of his business preying on his mind.: Whilst'she was getting tea ,ready on the evening of July 14th she heard her husband biting a penny. Then he apparently swallowed it, remarking "It's' gone. Tried to Force Bottle Neck Down His Throat.</p>
        <p>Next he took a bottle, and bit the neck off, and tried to force it down his throat. Witness struggled with him, but was pushed away, and she ran for help. She went half a mile to her mother-in-law, and said her husband was going mad. On returning to the house she found the door locked, and, through the window, saw her husband lying in a pool of blood. His throat had been badly cut, and a blood-stained knife was found on the table, also a sharp blood- stained spike. The next witness was Lewis Smith, a farmer, who deposed that on the evening of July 14th he met deceased's daughter in the road with a chopper, and when he asked her where she had got it from she replied, "I've got it from daddy." Smith went to the house and found Jell on the floor with his throat cut. "Banged the Side of His Head With A Chopper."</p>
        <p>Beatrice Jelf, the daughter of the deceased referred to by the previous witness, stated that she saw the struggle between her father and mother, and added that afterwards her father banged the side of his head with a chopper and fell down. She picked the chopper up and ran away, meeting Mr. Smith in the road. Mr. Allport, clinical assistant at Worcester Infirmary, deposed that when admitted to the Infirmary there was a wound about six inches long across the deceased's throat, also a cut on the right side of the neck. There was a deep incision in the windpipe. On the head there were nine cuts. The wounds healed up very well, but on Thursday Jelf became violent, a fact which witness attributed to the man's bad mental condition. The deceased talked in-</p>
        <p>, - coherently about cherry picking, submarines, and -ways of making money., The jury returned a verdict to the effect that the deceased committed suicide whilst tempor- arily insane, and they handed their fees to the widow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>MERSEY MYSTERY.</head>
        <p>A MERSEY MYSTERY.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Coroner's Court on Mon- day evening, Mr. S. Brighouse held an inquest on the body of an unknown man, which was discovered in the River Mersey on Saturday. John Roach, weirman at Howley Weir, said he was going about his duties when ho noticed a strange looking object floating in the river. On examination he found it Was the body of a man, and with assistance he pulled it out, and then went and informed the police. Detective-Sergeant Francis Summers, of the Warrington Borough Constabulary, said that he had searched the deceased's clothing, but had failed to find anything likely to lead to identification. There was a cheap metal watch in one of his pockets and threepence in coppers. The face was much decomposed and absolutely unrecognisable. Inquiries had been made with a view to establishing identification, but, so far, without result. Identification could only be established by' means of the clothing, if at all, for the body was.in such a. bad stater So far as could be judged the body was that of a man about thirty-five years of age. The jury returned a verdict of "Fouild drowned."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>WHERE HE HID.</p>
        <p>Absentee Under Bed In Ladies' Sleeping Carriage.</p>
        <p>At Monday's Borough Police Court, Walter Horsfall, a private in the Royal Inniskilling Fusiliers, was charged with being an absentee from . his., regiment, and was ordered to be handed over to a military escort, which had been telegraphed for. Titn evidence was to the effect that a police officer on duty at Bank Quay Station found prisoner hiding under a bed in a lady's sleep- ing compartment bn a train which arrived in Warrington in the small hottrs of Sunday morning. He hauled the man out and asked him for his pass. He replied that he had not got one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>PRISONER'S VAIN BOAST.</head>
        <p>PRISONER'S VAIN BOAST.</p>
        <p>Said Couldn't Be Locked Up; Soon Found lie was Wrong.</p>
        <p>James Welsh, a stranger to the town, was charged at Monday's Borhugh Police Court with having been drunk and disorderly in Buttermarket-street on Sunday.—He told the Bench that he did not know whether he was drunk or sober.</p>
        <p>The evidence was to the effect that at about 8.20 on Sunday evening he was seen by a j olice officer staggering about Buttermarket-street. He went up to three. men and started begging off them. He then noticed the officer on the other side of the road and shouted across to him "I don't care a for any policeman. No policeman can lock me up; I'm a British working man I am!" He soon Found that he was wrong, for the policeman forthwith haled him off to the Police Station, where he spent the night in the cell. The Chief Constable said the man had only been in the town a—short time, but he had already troubled the police twice, the first occasion being so recently as June 26th. Prisoner was fined 135., and in default was ordered 14 days' imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>FATHER CHARGES SON.</head>
        <p>FATHER CHARGES SON.</p>
        <p>Threatened With An Axe In The 'Course Of A Quarrel.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington. Borough Police Court on Monday, before the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) and other magistrates, Wm. Bram- hall, of 95, Hume-street, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly on Saturday, and also with having used threats to his father, Jas. Bramhall.—He denied the threats.</p>
        <p>A police officer deposed that at 11.40 on Satur- day night he received a complaint from prisoner's father to the effect that prisoner had chased him and his wife out of their house in Hume-street. Witness went to prisoner .and found that he was drunk, so took him into custody. When on the way to the Police Station he became very disorderly and used bad language.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>Dangerous When Drunk.</head>
        <p>Dangerous When Drunk.</p>
        <p>The father, a joiner by trade, said that at 10 o'clock on Saturday night his son came home in a drunken condition and commenced to quarrel with witness and his mother. " I spoke very little, if at all," added the witness, "for the old woman' kept saying 'Let him alone!' Knowing his temper, and also because of his condition, I kept quiet. He then com- menced to argue about his trade—he is a joiner—and because he couldn't .have his Sown way, lost hi S temper. He will get on to an argument when he is in his humours,' and the Devil himself couldn't stop him. (Laughter.) He is right enough when he is sober, but when he gets drink in his head he is not fit to be in the company of anybody. He can't say any- thing too bad, and he's dangerous as well. Suddenly. he jumped over the head of the sofa, dived under a cupboard and picked up an axe. It was a question then as to whether he would throw it at me or at our Arthur. I, naturally, bolted. lam afraid of him, but not if he would keep to his fists."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>When Father Is Not Afraid 'Of Him.</head>
        <p>When Father Is Not Afraid 'Of Him.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable: I take it, you ask the magistrates to make him find sureties to keep the peace? When he's like this, are you afraid of him?</p>
        <p>The Father: Not when he has nothing in his hands. He is all right when he is not in drink, but when he is he goes a bit touched here (pointing to his head). Prisoner said he was very sorry to find him- self in Court again. The Chief Constable said that, as a matter of fact, the police knew that the father had a terrible time with his son, and, despite what he had said, there was no doubt that there were times when he was afraid of him. On this occasion the son ran after him with an axe, and there could be no telling wha't might have happened if a constable had not opportunely appeared on the scene. The prisoner was mad drunk. There were a number of previous con- victions against him. The Mayor, in announcing that prisoner would be fined 275., or would • have to go to prison for 28 days, said that some of the magis- trates thought he "might to be examined and tied up somewhere."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>DISAPPROVING DOG.</head>
        <p>THE DISAPPROVING DOG.</p>
        <p>Caught Hold Of Quarrelsome Lodger's</p>
        <p>Nether Garments.</p>
        <p>At Monday's Police Court, before the Mayor and a full Bench of magistrates, James O'Neill, a young man with, a decided brogue, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly and also with breaking two panes of glass, valued at 35., in the windows of the house in which he had been lodging, 39, Pierpoint-street. —Prisoner denied, the wilful damage. P.C. Armstrong said that at midnight on Saturday he saw prisoner in Bewsey-road. He was surrounded by a crowd of people and was shouting and swearing and wanting to fight. He was bleeding from a wound on the left side' of the head. Witness took him to the house at which he was lodging and there he learned that he had broken a window, so locked him up. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Stormy Supper-time Scene.</head>
        <p>A Stormy Supper-time Scene.</p>
        <p>Margaret Varley, a grey-haired old woman, who said that she was the wife of Martin Varley, and resided at 39, -Pierpoint-street, said that prisoner had lodged with her and her husband for over twelve months. He came horne 'about 11.30 on Saturday.: night in a . drunken condition, and; after having his supper, com- menced to be quarrelsome. Witness asked him to- go to bed, but instead of doing so he chal- lenged her son out to fight. He continued quarrelling and .would not go to bed, so witness was compelled to get assistance to put him out of the house. As he was going out he struck witness's daughter, in the face and used dis- graceful language. When they got_him outside</p>
        <p>he went rou-nd-to the back of the house and threw large pieces of flint through one of the windows, smashing two panes of glass. He then went into Bewsey-road, where be wa.F. arrested.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Said He Was Hit With A Poker.</head>
        <p>Said He Was Hit With A Poker.</p>
        <p>Prisoner: Why did your son hit me on the head with a poker when I was standing at the side of the door?</p>
        <p>Witness: He never handled the poker. Did you hit me?—No. Prisoner: You did, and so did all the daugh- ters, only I was not hitting women back. The womett held me and -the men hit me, whilst the dog had me by the trousers. (Laughter.) The Chief Constable: Even the dog didn't approve of his conduct. (Renewed laughter.) Prisoner: And, besides, how in the world could I break the back window when I was in the front street? It was all through the brother-in-law; he's a bad man when he's in drink, although he's a nice man when he is sober. (Laughter.) I was going upstairs when he rushed at me and then they all shoved me about. I went outside the door and asked for my coat and hat. The daughters rushed after me and the son came out with the poker and hit me. Then the others came after me and set about me.</p>
        <p>Mobbed by the Neighbours.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said that stones similar tce those that broke the window were taken from the prisoner's pocket and handed to the police. No doubt prisoner was mobbed by other people in the street, but he deserved all</p>
        <p>he got. They apparently knew his landlady, and they went for him, and it was, perhaps, a lucky thing for him that the police came up and rescued him from their clutches.</p>
        <p>Prisoner: I deny the assault, but I admit that I was half-drunk.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable: There, your Worships, he only admits that half-way. There are two previous convictions against him, and there can be no doubt that he is practically mad when he gets a droll of drink. Prisoner was fined 19s. 6d., or 14 days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>THREE NIGHTS.</head>
        <p>THREE NIGHTS.</p>
        <p>Child Neglect And Girl's Thought For</p>
        <p>little Sister. Sarah Ellen Humphries, widow, of no settled abode, was charged at the Police Court on Tuesday with neglecting her two children, Theresa (14) and Emily (10), in a manner likely to cause them unnecessary suffering and injury to health. Mr. A. Browne undertook the prosecution on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C. Coun- cillor Roberts presided over the Bench, and his colleagues were Mr. J. Rymer Young, Mr. C. Jackson Holmes and Mr. T. Locker.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>"Not Going to the Workhouse."</head>
        <p>"Not Going to the Workhouse."</p>
        <p>After an opening statement by Mr. Browne, Inspector Wainwright, N.S.P.C.C., said that at 9.30 on Tuesday night the prisoner came to his office and said she was destitute and asked what she should do to find lodgings for her children for the night. He told her she could go to the Workhouse, and she replied that she had never been there and didn't 'intend to go. Witness replied that if she exposed the children it would get her into serious trouble. She then said she would go to the Workhouse. He asked her where she had come from, and she said that she had been lodging with a Mrs. Corder, in Bridge- street, Wigan. Prisoner then left the office. Subsequently witness made inquiries and ascer- tained that prisoner had not been to the Work- house.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>Couldn't Find Mother.</head>
        <p>Couldn't Find Mother.</p>
        <p>The two children came round to witness's offioe on Saturday evening at 5.30, and said they hadn't seen their mother since norni Witness sent a message to Mrs. Gallop with whom daughters of the prisoner were living), 4"king her to keep the children till the mother was found. On Monday afternoon, prisoner came to witness's office and said she had heard he (Inspector Wainwright) was looking for her. He replied that he was, and wanted to know where she had been, and why she had neglected to provide shelter for the children. She started blacken- ing the character of the woman who was look- ing after the children. Witness refused to listen to her. He had known prisoner for the last two years. He had seen her in drink.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>Motherly Little Maid.</head>
        <p>A Motherly Little Maid.</p>
        <p>Theresa Humphries, a pale-faced little girl of fourteen, was next called into the witness box. She stated, in reply to a number of questions by Mr. Browne, that until recently she had been employed at a milk shop in Wigan, receiv- ing ss. a week in wages. Her mistress was very kind and gave her a frock and bought her a pair of boots. She also provided her with a bed. On Saturday week, witness, her sister and her 'mother came to Warrington and went to Mrs. Melbourne's, in Mersey-street. On the follow- ing Tuesday her mother went to the' house where witness's sister, Louisa, was living, and asked her for some money for lodgings. Failing to get any, she said she would pawn her child's boots. In reply to this, Louisa said to witness: "Don't let them go." Witness and her mother and her little sister, then Went to Inspector Wainwright's office. Witness didn't go inside. That night they all three slept in a closet in Bridge-street. On Wednesday night they slept in the same closet.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne: Wag your sister cold? Witness: I put my coat round her. Mr. Browne: Did you sleep yourself? Witness: No, sir. Boots Pawned While She Slept.</p>
        <p>The young witness went on to say that she and her little sister and mother passed the Thursday night in Mrs. Melbourne's closet. In answer to Mr. Browne's query as to whether it was true, as her mother said, that Mrs. Mel- bourne knew they were in the closet, and, in fact, gave her mother the key, witness said: No! On Thursday morning her mother told Mrs. Melbourne that she had been sitting up all night at Gallop's. Mrs. Melbourne sent wit- ness upstairs to bed. Witness left her boots downstairs and slept till half-past one, when she went down and found that her boots had dis- appeared and that her mother was drunk. Later she found out her mother had pawned the boots. On Friday night (a pouring wet night) they slept in Mrs. Melbourne's back room. Struck in the Mouth, Thumped and Kicked.</p>
        <p>The child went on to tell of how her mother sent her to her sister for some money, and when she returned with only 3s. 10d. her mother hit her in the mouth, causing it to bleed. After that she thumped and kicked her. On Saturday afternoon witness and her little sister received a penny each from their mother to go to "the pictures." Instead of going to "the pictures," they went and bought some- thing to eat. Then they visited a number of public-houses looking for their mother. Not finding her, they went to Mr. Gallop's and he had kept them since. Seeking For Her Children.</p>
        <p>Jas. Gallop, a labourer, living in Back Crossley-street, said that when prisoner came round on Saturday night and asked if the chil- dren were there, he told her they were not. He thought they would be better out of her keep- ing, after what they had undergone during the week. She was half-drunk when she came round. Prisoner's elder daughters were living at his house; they would not live with their mother.</p>
        <p>Didn't Like to go to the Workhouse. Prisoner said that she didn't like to take the children to the Workhouse. She added that if she had been allowed to take the children away when she called for them at Gallop's on the Saturday night, she would have taken them to Rogerson's, where she had arranged to hire lodgings for the week. The Chairman said the Bench considered the case a very disgracefuLone, and prisoner would have to go to prison foil two months. Children to go to Catholic Home.</p>
        <p>In the matter of the future of the children, Mr. Wainwright had (said Mr. Browne) written to a Roman Catholic Home at Bolton.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: The elder child appears to be a _nice, intelligent girl. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne--; The other child looks half- starved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>MOTHER AND DAIIiIITER IN TIIE DOCK.</head>
        <p>MOTHER AND DAIIiIITER IN TIIE DOCK.</p>
        <p>Sequel To A Sunday Afternoon Assault.</p>
        <p>At Wednesday's Borough Police Court, before Mr. •J. W. Hayward and other magistrates, Elizabeth Ward, a married woman, of 9, Black hmst-street, and Katherine, her daughter, were charged with assaulting Margaret Milligan, and, further, with doing wilful damage to the extent of 4s. Gd. to a window in the complain- ant's house, 11, Blackhurst-street, by smashing two panes of glass.—The prisoners pleaded not guilty. The complainant's story was to the effect that at 3 o'clock on Sunday afternoon the prisoners came to her house and violently assaulted her, without any cause. The neighbours had to come to her assistance. Pulled Her Hair and Knocked Her Down.</p>
        <p>Margaret O'Neill, aged is, who lives near to the parties in the case, deposed that at 3.30 on Sunday afternoon she heard the sound of smashing glass and saw Mrs. Milligan come out of her house into the street. The elder prisoner ran up to her, caught her by the hair and knocked her down. Then the prisoner's daughter, Kate, ap- peared on the scene, and hit the prostrate woman in the side with a brick. She then put the brick down and pummelled the woman with her fists. Neighbours rushed up and restrained her. As the neighbours were tfkking the complainant in- doors the elder prisoner heaved a brick at her. It just missed her and, in witness's opinion, had it hit her, might have killed her outright. Two other girls corroborated this witness's evipience. Detective Green deposed to arresting the daughter, who in reply to the charge of assault,</p>
        <p>said "It's a lie; I never touched the woman !" Detective-Sergeant Summers, who arrested the mother, stated that in answer to the charge she said "Yes, but I did it in self-defence." in reply to the second charge she said "No; I didn't smash her window." On the way to the cells the woman remarked to witness "Had I known that you were getting me, you would not have got me so soft as you did." Witness added that he knew the elder prisoner to be a violent- tempered woman, and he quite appreciated what she meant when she used the words "You would not have got me so soft as you did." Detective Anderson said that when he had occasion to visit the elder prisoner in the deten- tion room she said to him "I wish I had known early what I know now, and then I would have given you the islip until to-morrow morning. Fancy being locked up here for a woman like that."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Prisoners' Version.</head>
        <p>Prisoners' Version.</p>
        <p>The elder prisoner's story was that she was standing peaceably at her front door showing phi:4os to a friend when the complainant threw plates, knives and a glass sugar basin at her (prisoner's) daughter. She then made for wit- ness and caught her by the hair and together they fell into the ashbin. ,All that happened in the back yard. Complainimt afterwards came to the front of the house "so that Richmond's men could see us .have it out." After calling, her a vile name complainant threw her shawl into the roadway and 'caught hold of Witness's hair, the pair of them falling into the roadway toegther. Then the neighbours intervened and parted them.</p>
        <p>- The daugnt-r allege 1 that the complainant called her "foul names" and then heaved plates at her, following those up ith a fusillade cf bricks, rubbing-stones, &amp;e. A.'4er that ooiti- plainant went out into the street and chal- lenged witness's mother.</p>
        <p>Annie, another daughter of the elder prisoner, gave corroborative evidence.</p>
        <p>- The Chief Constable told the Court that the elder prisoner was a past-mistress in the art, of tale-spinning. The woman and her daughter were before the Court a week previously for using indecent language and were fined 7s. 6d., with the alternative of seven days' imprison- ment. Ever since the Police Court proceedings last week there had been trouble in Blackhurst- street and it would require half-a-dozen police- men to control the people in that locality. The mother was fined 20s. and ordered to pay 4s. 6d. damages, whilst the daughter -was mulcted in a penalty of 10s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>FOGGY NIGHT.</head>
        <p>A FOGGY NIGHT.</p>
        <p>Motorist Fined _ For _Driving Without</p>
        <p>A Light.</p>
        <p>George John Kelly, of 308, Stanley-street, Bootle, Liverpool, was summoned at the Borough Police Court on Tuesday for driving a motor car at Warrington without a light. The evidence was to the effect that the imp was in a very dirty condition when the motor was stopped. It was all clogged up with soot. Mr. T. S. Steel, who represented defendalit, pointed out that the bad state of the lamp was due to the fogginess of the night. Defendant had had trouble with the light nearly all the way from Manchester, and thought it was alight, although he knew it was rather feeble owing chiefly to the fog. His client was •en- gaged in a business at Bolton. He had a clean record.</p>
        <p>A fine of 20s. was imposed on defendant, who did not appear.</p>
        <p>MOTOR VAN DRIVER FINED.</p>
        <p>Herbert Edmondson, of 144: Richmond-,w, Liverpool, was fined lls, for failing to produce his licence when driving a motor van at War- rington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>TRIED TO DROWN HERSELF.</head>
        <p>TRIED TO DROWN HERSELF.</p>
        <p>Woman Who Suffered From Delusions</p>
        <p>At the County Petty Sessions held at War- rington on Wednesday, before Messrs. J. Rymer Young (in the chair), W. Fairclough and W. Furness, Lily Brown, a married woman of 2.24, Winwick-road, Warrington, was charged with attempting to commit suicide by drown- ing herself. , The evidence was to the effect that at seven o'clock that morning, Frank Weir, a lock- keeper, employed by the Manchester Ship Canal Company, saw the prisoner jump into tha water at Bewsey Lock.. With the assistince of a man named Joseph Belton the witness got her safely out. Dr. Peacocke said he had examined the woman and she was clearly suffering from delu- sions and wa's unfit to be at large. She was committed to the Workhouse for 14 days for treatment and in order that observa- tion may be kept upon her.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>BELLICOSE WINDOW CLEANER.</head>
        <p>BELLICOSE WINDOW CLEANER.</p>
        <p>Police Court Sequel To A Street</p>
        <p>Scuffle.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. S. Wright (in the chair), Alder- men Bolton, Smethurst and Pierpoint, and Messrs. G. Piling .A. J. Jolley and W. Fur- ness, Wm. Leather, of 59, Howley-lane, was at yesterday's Borough Police Court summoned for assaulting Thomas Crofts, who resides at 261, Lovely-lane. Mr. Swallow, solicitor, who appeared for the complainant, stated that his client and the de- fendant were window cleaners carrying on busi- ness in Warrington. On Meador morning Crofts was engaged cleaning windows in Bridge- street, when the defendant came across the street, caught hold of him by the collar and attempted to butt him in the face. Defendant's attack, however, was warded off and in the struggle the two men fell to the ground, .but neither was seriously injured. After hearing the evidence the magistrates hound defendant over and ordered him to -pay 5-8. costs and the advocate's fee of half-a-guinea.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>£220 FOR A HUSBAND.</head>
        <p>£220 FOR A HUSBAND.</p>
        <p>Widow Obtains Award Under VVork-</p>
        <p>men's Compensation Act.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thurs- day, before his Honour, Deputy-Judge Sellars, Mr. Arthur Browne, solicitor, Warrington, applied on behalf of Elizabeth Peplow, of Mill street, Warrington, for an award ender the Workmen's Compensation Act for the sum o? £220, which had been lodged in Court by the St. Helens Cable Co.' Warrington, as compensa- tion in respect of the death of her husband, George Peplow, who was killed whilst engaged in shunting operations on May 15th of thie year. Mr. Browne explained that the original claiin was for .1;256, but there was a question as to what the wages of the deceased were to be, seeing that he had only worked one day for the Cable Co. when he was killed. The com- pany had _originally offered a sum which was substantially too small, but after a discussion between the representatives of the parties in- terested a compromise had been effected, under which the widow would receive £220. Mr. Healey, solicitor, of Manchester, repre- sented the Cable Co. and assented to the award being made. He also applied for an indemnity trver and against Messrs. Joseph Dolan and Son, builders, of Warrington, on the ground that the deceased was killed through the negligence of their cranedriver at Bank Quay.</p>
        <p>Mr. Longland. solicitor, Warrington, for Messrs. Dolan4 agreed. • His Honour made the award accordingly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
