      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>BURY THE PAST.</head>
        <p>BURY THE PAST.</p>
        <p>Mn. LLOYD GEORGE has shown those in- terested in the South Wales coal trade " how to do it." Had ha been a pompous, arro- gant, or vain-glorious species of Statesman —had he gone to South Wales to tell the masters what they must do and the men what they would have to do—the consequences might have been serious. He went to effect a settlement to make that peace at home without which victory abroad is impossible, and, having by his own sympathetic per- sonality largely brought about that settle- ment, he, with a detachment and a breezi- ness that even in the most critical situations, is hi ,s own, told his fellow countrymen to "forget the past and concentrate on the present and future_ The moment the strike was ended it was essential that any bitterness it had engendered should be for- gotten.. Who could tell the'miners that, in language they could understand, better than their own LLOYD GEORGE? Taking his figures of speech from their daily ex- periences and surroundings he said he was not going to refer to the past. " Let it be buried in the deepest pit you have got in South Wales," he said; "flood it if you can; out of sight let it be." There is nobler work on hand than brooding over personal wrongs, real or imaginary. Then to the miners he said, "Let us work to- gether." In these few words the MIINMONS MINISTER laid the simple duty of the country bare. Let us bury the past, in so far as that past has held a menace to our task of downing a foe that will assuredly down us if we fail; and "let us work together." Mn. LLOYD GEORGE asked his hearers not only to forget the past, but to make up for , the time lost by increased devotion. Do your best, as much as is in you, to make up for lost time, and show the democracy of France, at any rate, that you are prepared to assist them in the common struggle for the freedom of the world. I want you to do it for the sake of the British Navy which defends our shores—the Navy that makes it impossible for the ruth- less German to trample our coalfields and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 24, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 24, 1915.</p>
        <p>elrni'.elds as they are trampling those of E, -twee.</p>
        <p>Fill the bunkers—it means defence; it means protection; it means an inviolable Britain; it means that Britain, through your help, can still defy the most potent enemy in the world. It depends upon you to help the British Navy to defend our shores. Upon the cbal you turn out depends the steel, the explosive, the material that helps our men to meet the enemy on fairly equal, terms.</p>
        <p>Shell, shot, cannon, machine guns, rifles— the enemy has got them. Give these valiant comrades of ours a chance to face them with an equal equipment. Peace at home is essential to victory abroad.</p>
        <p>As we have hinted, we have no desire to go into the past, being content to rejoice at the settlement and look to thb future. It may be interesting, however, as a matter of record, to state that a series of conferences between MR. LLOYD GEORGE, M. RIINCLIIAN and M. HENDERSON, acting for the Govern- ment, and representatives of coal-owners and miners, resulted in the production of a new agreement, which was submitted to the miners' delegates at Cardiff on Wednesday. The new terms are to be binding upon both parties until six months %after the. conclu- sion ,of peace, and terminable thereafter by three months' notice on either side. The terms negotiated by M. LLOYD GEORGE and his colleagues conceded the men's demand that the 1879 standard wage should be replaced by a new standard rate 50 per cent. higher. Surface-men are to receive a 10 per cent. minimum, advance on new standard surface-men rates. Only mem- hers of the Federation will obtain the benefit</p>
        <p>of the concessions, non-unionists being ex- cluded from it. Questions of interpretation which may arise are to be referred to the President of the Board of Trade, whose decision will be final.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>THRIFT.</head>
        <p>THRIFT.</p>
        <p>Warrington is to make an effort to bring itself into line with the rest of the country in its effort to be thrifty; and, with that end in 'view, the MAyon has called a public meeting to be held in the Town Hall on Monday evening. All are invited, and it is to be hoped that the response will be on a large scale. Warrington is one of the places where wages are being earned on a much larger scale than in normal tlimes, and the difference between normal earnings and the present earnings ought certainly to be saved and devoted to national purposes. More than that ought to be.,done, but it is the very minimum that we have a right to expect. In other cases every chance to effect an economy ought to be seizee. It is idle to preach economy 'to the poor while other classes fail to practise the virtue._ We ought surely to have heard the last of labour disputes for the duration of the war, for, apart from the wages lost, the time consumed in waste can never be made good —a serious point, to be considered before people enter upon a dispute while a powerful and brutal enemy is knocking at the gate.</p>
        <p>The folloTing are a few points we might all observe for our own good and the public advantage :</p>
        <p>Eat less. Drink less. Be careful with bread. Waste nothing: to waste food' is as bad as to waste munitions.- • Save especially in all things which have to be got from abroad; that is to say, food and drink of all kinds, tobacco, (Ice. Use home products whenever possible, and use them sparingly. Before you spend anything, think whether it is absolutely necessary to do so. If you have the opportunity—grow your own vegetables, &amp;c. Support the MAYon at the meeting on Monday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>WORKS WILL HELP.</head>
        <p>THE WORKS WILL HELP.</p>
        <p>The works are, in many cases, helping their employes to save by offering them facilities for doing so. One large firm recently gave the following advice away with the wages they paid out</p>
        <p>"ENGLAND EXPECTS EVERY MAN TO DO HIS DUTY."</p>
        <p>Personal Thrift the National Need.</p>
        <p>All unnecessary expenditure on food, drink, dress or pleasure reduces the National Wealth and diverts labour from more necessary work. Extravagance is a great enemy. Save money for the slump which will come after the war. Put something by each week. Don't hoard surplus money in .the house. The Bank is the right place for money.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>Proposal To Suspend Them By</p>
        <p>New Act.</p>
        <p>Mr. Long, in the House of Commons on Thursday, presented a Bill to postpone elections of local authorities and other bodies and the preparation of the Parliamentary and local government registers and for purposes incidental thereto. The Bill was read a first time., The Government hope to pass the Bill before the recess.</p>
        <p>Casual Vacancies to be Filled By. Co-option.</p>
        <p>The effect of the Bill is to suspend all pre- paration of lists of voters, both Parliamentary and local government, until Parliament other- wise directs. With regard to local bodies, no elections are to take place for a year and any casual vacancies are to be filled by co-option.</p>
        <p>It is understood that there is no intention of interfering with the present course of events in the case of Parliamentary vacancies. The • . . existing register will remain in force, and should contests occur in by-elections, polling will take place on the present voting lists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>BANK NOTICES.</head>
        <p>BANK NOTICES.</p>
        <p>LORD EMMOTT AND THE LANCASHIRE AND YORKSHIRE.</p>
        <p>Lord Emmott, G.C.M.G., has been appointed a Director of the Lancashire and Yorkshire Bank Limited. Lord Emmett was M.P. for Oldham, 1899-1911, Deputy Speaker and Chair- man of Ways and Means House of Commons 1906-1911, Under Secretary of State for the Colonies 1911-1914, and First Commissioner of Works from 1914 until the formation of the Coalition Government,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>HOLY CATHOLIC CHURCH.</head>
        <p>HOLY CATHOLIC CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Canon Willis Replies To 'Roman Bishops'" Scornful Language."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>SOME CHURCH HISTORY.</head>
        <p>SOME CHURCH HISTORY.</p>
        <p>Last week, at the opening of the new Roman Catholic Church of St. Benedict, Warrington, several references were made to buildings and the doctrines of the Church of England; references which called forth a reply from the Rector of Warrington (Canon Willis), who occupied the pulpit of the Parish Church on Sunday evening..</p>
        <p>Taking for his subject "The Holy Catholic Church," the Rector said he had been led to choose that subject by the visit of the Roman Bishops and their utterances during the pre- ceding week. "I have read in the news- papers," he said, "as I suppose you have read, the sermons preached in the new Roman Church last Sunday and Monday. cannot imagine for a moment "arty Bishop of the Church of England using such scornful lan- guage, nor do I think the Roman clergy resi- dent in Warrington would use such language regarding those who are earnestly trying to do God's work." • THE PLEA FOR UNITY.</p>
        <p>In St. John xvii. 21, it was written: "That they all may be one;, as thou Father art in me and I in thee, that they also may be one in us; that the world may believe that thou has sent me." It was the outward unity that the world could see for which our Lord here prayed. The word Church was derived from Greek words-13cclesia, "called out," and Kurios, "belonging to the Lord "—and so the Church, which was ft divine institution, embraced the people who had been "called out of the world" by holy baptism.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>ROME'S "PRECEDENCE."</head>
        <p>ROME'S "PRECEDENCE."</p>
        <p>As to the history of the Church, in the time of Our Lord, the Roman Empire consisted of three provinces in Asia, Europe and Africa. Those three became the patriarchates of the Church, which were founded in very early times—soon after the time of Our Lord. The chief town in each of the patriarchates was Antioch in Asia, Rome in Europe and Alex- andria in Africa. Later on, in 381, a fourth patriarchate was founded at Constantinople, and in 451 a fifth was founded at Jerusalem. These five stood on a perfectly equal footing. In seniority, according to the date of their foundation, the order of the five patriarch- ates was: Antioch, Rome, Alexandria, Con- stantinople and Jerusalem. At the first great Council of the Church, held at Nieces., in 325, Rome was given the precedence because it Was "Old Rome." , It was not given, as Romans contend, because Our Lord had given to St. Peter the Primacy, but it was simply given the first -place on account of its being the capital of the Roman Empire. Ultimately the seat of the Empire was moved from Rome to Constantinople, and in the year 381 it was decided that Constantinople should take the second place because it was "New Rome." The order then became: Rome, Constantinople, Alexandria, Antioch and Jerusalem. Those five remained on a perfectly equal footing; there was no distinction whatever between them except just an order of precedence, and so it remained until about the year 1000, when the great schism took place between the East and the West. INDEBTED TO ROME BUT</p>
        <p>I want particularly, proceeded the Rector, to point out in connection with the Church in this country our indebtedness to Rome. I don't hide that for a moment. There was a time when it was taught, out of prejudice against Rome I think, that we got our Christian sources directly from the East. Historians are now agreed that we are deeply indebted to Rome, that we got our Faith, Orders, and Worship from the East through Rome, about 200 years after Christ. This country was in the possession of the Roman Empire for 400 years. Armies were continually passing between Rome and here; is it not natural and likely that our Christianity would come that way? I am quite willing to acknow- ledge that; but that does not, for one moment, make ours the Roman Church. Ours is the National Church. We should not say the American Church is part of the Scotch Church, though America got her Orders, from Scotland. We kin the same way got our 'Faith and our Orders and Worship from Rome; but we have always been the National Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>PERIOD OF DOMINATION.</head>
        <p>PERIOD OF DOMINATION.</p>
        <p>The Anglo Saxons, who' were heathen, came to this country about 450 years after Christ. They over-ran the land and drove the Church into Cornwall, Wales, Scotland and Ireland. Then about 600 years after Christ, Gregory the Great sent Augustine as a missionary to this country, and it is to him that we owe the English Church. We, therefore, owe the British Church to the East through Rome, and the English Church to Gregory, who sent Augustine to this land. From 664 to 1534 the Church of England was under the domination of Rome. In the year 664 the great Council of Whitby was held and the British Church then gave way to Rome. On one side there were representatives of the British. Church, and on the other side were representatives of the Roman Church. They appealed to the King, who said to the Roman party "What do you say?" and they replied "We represent St. Peter, who has the keys of the Kingdom of Heaven." The King thereupon said, "Well, if St. Peter has got the keys, I shall side with the Roman party lest when I come to heaven he may refuse to let me in," and so the Romans won the day. Then began the domination of Rome and it continued until the year 1534, when Henry VIII. threw off the Roman yoke. Henry VIII. was a very bad' man indeed, but he was God's instrument. The Romans did all they could to persuade Queen Elizabeth to acknowledge Papal supremacy. She refused, and in the year 1570, Rome sent over the Italian mission to this country—the present Roman Church, which only dates from the year 1570. The Church of this land -never belonged to Rome. It was under the power of Rome, in- creasingly so, for nearly 1,000 years, from 664 to 1534, when the Roman yoke was cast off.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>ESSENTIALS OF CATHOLICITY.</head>
        <p>ESSENTIALS OF CATHOLICITY.</p>
        <p>Referring to the meaning of the Holy Catho- lic Church, the Rector explained that there were two words that were frequently used. The Eastern Church always used the word orthodox, "right thinking"; and the Western Church, particularly the Roman, used the word Catholic, "universal." We use the word Catholic in the Church of England—in the Prayer-Book, in the Creeds, Litany and Holy Communion. He went on to speak of the four essentials of Catholicity.</p>
        <p>The first, he said, is that we must have been baptised; that is the one and only entrance into the Holy Catholic Church. Everybody who has been baptised is in a sense a Catholic. Even Nonconformists, if they have been bap- tised aright with water and the words, are Catholics. The second step is that we must hold the Faith; we must not add anything to it or take anything away from it. The third essential for a Catholic is that we must use the means of grace—the Sacrament of Our Lord, Holy Baptism, 'Confirmation, and lIPly Com- munion.. You may use—you will find great profit from it if you use it aright—the sacra- mental ordinance of Penance. You must use the first three, or you cannot be a Catholic. The fourth essential is that you must live the Christ-like life. If you have been baptised; if you know the Faith; if you use the means of grace and live the life, then you have a right to call yourself a good Catholic. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>WISE AND RIGHT COURSE.</head>
        <p>THE WISE AND RIGHT COURSE.</p>
        <p>The wise and right course for members of the Church of England to adopt, the Rector said, was first to know the Catholic Faith. Our Churchpeople, he said, are not at all well in- structed and will not take the pains to be instructed. The children are instructed very well in our Church schools and very often they know more than their parents, who had not the opportunity when they were young and who will not take, advantage of opportunities now. Then we should know the history of the Church Catholic in this land. The third thing is not to teach to do just the opposite of Rome in every way, but to do what is right and to teach what is really right whether Rome does it or not. You know how very much afraid People are sometimes of being thought to copy Rome. Some thought last Sunday that we were copying Rome because we had a proces- sion thrcugh the streets. I think nobody thinks that now; they are very glad that we had that procession and it made a very deep impression upon the people.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>SOUTH LANCS. AT THE FROWT.</head>
        <p>SOUTH LANCS. AT THE FROWT.</p>
        <p>Present Work 13 Light— Comparatively."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>T3-3ANKS FOR GIFTS.</head>
        <p>T3-3ANKS FOR GIFTS.</p>
        <p>The Town tleik (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle) has this week received two letters from the Front— one from the 2nd Battalion and another from the 4th Battalion South Lancg. Regiment, from which it appears that our local heroes are taking a well-earned rest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>SECOND BATTALION.</head>
        <p>THE SECOND BATTALION.</p>
        <p>Captain Sydney a . Boast, writing to the Town Clerk, says : "I beg to thank you for your letter of Ird July, 1915. The three cases of cigarettes arrived safety to-day and were at once tributed amongst the men. The labels on the cases bear the words From your frien.ls at Warrington. Good Luck.' On behalf of the men of the Battalion I send sineere thanks to,tho donors of the smokes for thoir kind thoughts and good wishes, which -.%e all appreciate so-much. The Battalion is at xe- sent well in the rear of the fluing-line, having been withdrawn a few days ago for a t% earned rest, which, needless to say, everyone is fully enjoying. I hope to take the oppor- tunity shortly of letting our friends at home know, through our local papers, something of our doings since my last report—as 1 know how interested they all are in the doings of their 'local ' regiment. "Again thanking you for your letter and with best wishes to yourself and all old friends."</p>
        <p>THE TERRITORIALS. "Flanders July 16th, 1915, "Dear Mr. Whittle,—On behalf of the Battalion I wish to thank those kind friends who have sent the cigarettes. These arrived at a most appropriate time, just as the Battalion got down from the trenches for a good long rest, which is badly needed. I have heard it said that 'change of work was as good as a rest.' Our work is changed, but up to now it is light work, comparatively. " With the best of wishes from all ranks out here, to those 'doing their bit' at home, " I am, "Faithfully yours, , " Brereton Fairclough, "Lt.-Col. 4th South Lanes. Regt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>"GERMAN SPIES" CAUGHT.</head>
        <p>"GERMAN SPIES" CAUGHT.</p>
        <p>Arrested With A Camera At Stockton Heath Bridge.</p>
        <p>In these troublous times it is unwise, un- fashionable and generally unpleasant to bear even the remotest semblance of a German. If anyone feels inclined to doubt this statement, let him go to Mr. Arthur Clark, of Wilderspool- causeway, and his friend, Mr. Harry Sanford, both on holiday after a voyage as stewards, and ask them all about it. It was shortly after tea-time one fine evening when these two bold bad bare- boozlers sallied forth to bamboozle. They wore no Schmidt goggles; neither did they carry sinister notebooks and pencils with well-chewed ends. They did not approach the Stockton Heath Bridge stealthily and by devious ways; they walked boldly up to the closed gates and waited for a vessel to pass. This all seems very harmless; but they had a camera; and it was the camera that caused the trouble. It was not the camera alone, but it was that that made the sentry suspicious; and a suspicious sentry is a man to beware of, unless you like finding yourself playing the "star" role in the centre of a crowd of the curious—one or those crowds that will assemble on the slightest provocation, from a dog-fight- to a fainting episode. Once a sentry's suspicions are aroused, nothing can stop him. He will examine you from a springing distance, make a German spy of you in about two-and-a-half minutes, and then come down on you like a load of bricks. After all, what is he there for? But we digre_ss. Our worthy sentry, who, as the reacrbr will agree, had gathered no moss, spotted the camera. Then he looked for further traces. And he had little difficulty in finding them, for the fair-haired Mr. Sanford (who is, like his friend, a British subject) certainly has a, suggestion of the German about him. The com- bination of circumstances was sufficient for the sentry—and too much for the suspects. They, were apprehended, marched off to the Corporal 'koff the Guard and cross-examined. They so far satisfied this gentleman as to obtain their release for the night, but it was not till the following morning that the camera was returned, together with an apology for the mistake. The one exposed plate had been de- veloped, to make sure that there was nothing on it that would be of value to the Kaiser Wilhelm. The picture must have pleased His Madship had he seen it, but it was hardly of strategic importance, being merely a snapshot of a charming young lady not unknown to the "German " culprits. There is a moral to this tale of woe. It is, like the cinema pictures, in three parts. It is this: Firstly, don't look like a German; secondly., if you can't help looking like a German don't carry a camera; thirdly, if you can't help looking like a German and must carry a camera, don't produce it when you are in the vicinity of anything that might be deemed of military importince.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. J. R. WOOD.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. J. R. WOOD.</p>
        <p>Well Known Locally As A Musician And Chess Player.</p>
        <p>We regret to record the death of Mr. John Richard Wood, music dealer, of Old London- road, Stockton Heath, who passed away on 'Sunday at the age of 47, after an illness lasting less than two days. A Yorkshireman by birth, and a native of Ravensthorpe, near Dewsbury, Mr. Wood came to the Warrington district about ten years ago and took up his residence at Latchford, remov- ing later to Stockton Heath. He was one of the promulgators of the Stockton Heath Trades- men's Association, and played a large part in the formation of the Tradesmen's Social Club, less than a year ago. , As a chess player Mr. Wood was well known. He was a prominent member of the Warring- ton Chess Club, and had held the offices of secretary and treasurer in that institution. For three years he was champion of the Warrington district, and for many years he held the club trophy. For many years Mr. Wood was a member of the Warrington Musical Society's Orchestra. With him was apprenticed the late T. W. Pearce, the very promising young musioian who was taken up by the Manchester College of Music, and who died a few months ago, shortly after his marriage. Mr. Wood was well known in local musical circles.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wood was held in high esteem by a con- siderable circle of friends, amongst whom there is a feeling of profound sympathy for the be- reaved widow.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place in St. Thomas' Church, on Wednesday, when there were num- erous mourners and a large collection of floral tributes, including tokens from the following:— " From Alice," "His brothers and sisters," Mrs. R. Wood and family, "Margie and Brian," Mr. and Mrs. Lindley, Mr. Exley, Mr. and the Misses Moore, Mr. and Mrs. Barnes and "Flossie," Mr. and Mrs. Woolhouse, Mrs. and Miss Sykes, Mr. and Mrs. S. Taylor, Mr. and Mrs. Hamilton Smith, Mr. and Mrs. Wor- rall, " Vashti," "Fred," Mr. and Mrs. A. Algate, The Tradespeople of Stockton Heath, Mr. and Mrs. Hitchen, The Tradesmen's Social Club, The St. Thomas' Women's Bible Class, Mrs. Hayward and "Elsie," "The Wives and Mothers, Rol! of Honour, Stockton Heath."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>National Thrift.</head>
        <p>National Thrift.</p>
        <p>All inteiested in the question of national thrift are invited to attend a meeting to be held in the Town Hall at 7.30 on Monday even- ing, when it is hoped to form a representative committee, with a view to bringing the whole subject before the public. More Than Double.</p>
        <p>Last week the births in Warrington numbered 45 and the deaths only 22. Of the new arrivals 23 were boys and 22 girls. Of the deaths 12 were. of males and 10 of females.</p>
        <p>There were two deaths from scarlet fever and three from pneumonia.</p>
        <p>Qualifying..</p>
        <p>The local results obtalned— in Part 2 of the Preliminary Examination for the Elementary School Teachers' Certificate, 1915, shows that all the candidates from the borough schools were successful in passing, one passing with two distinctions, three with one distinction, and seven without distinction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>St. Benedict's Church.</head>
        <p>St. Benedict's Church.</p>
        <p>In our account of the opening of St. Bene- dict's Church last week we stated that Father Iluggins was Father Swarbreck's predecessor at the church. In this we were in error. The name -should have been the Rev-. J. W. Baines, who was the first -priest of the Orford-lane parish.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>To Represent Education Committee.</head>
        <p>To Represent Education Committee.</p>
        <p>Mrs. C. Jackson Holmes, Mr. John Upson and the Director of Education (Mr. J. Moore Murray) hare been appointed the Education Committee's representatives at. the• annual meeting of the Union of' Lancashire and Cheshire Institutes, to be held at Wallasey on Friday, the Ist October, 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Brilliant Student.</head>
        <p>A Brilliant Student.</p>
        <p>The first Warrington scholarship holder .(Mr. William E. Mason) has passed all parts ef the Ist M. 8., Ch.B. examination at Manchester University and has been awarded the Univer- sity prize in Zoology; besides having won the Senior "Dauntesey " Medical Scholarship on the total results of the Ist M.B. examination..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Electric Light Dearer.</head>
        <p>Electric Light Dearer.</p>
        <p>The ,Corporation give notice that awing to the increase in the price of coal, and the greatly increased expenses, due to the war, the price of electric current will, from August Ist,, be increased. For lighting and tramway pur- poses the increase will amount to 10 per cent. and for heating and power purposes, except in a number of sp4cial cases, 15 per cent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>Stockton Heath Garden Society.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath Garden Society.</p>
        <p>The fourteenth Annual show in connection with the Stockton Heath Garden Society will be held in Wilderspool Park on August Bank Holiday and will be opened by Mrs. Frank Cooper, of Hill Cliffs. An attractive schedule has been arranged for the benefit of exhibitors, and visitors will find that adequate entertain- ment in the shape of concerts, children's sports, &amp;r., has been provided for them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Examination Success.</head>
        <p>Examination Success.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. P. Greaves, eldest son of Mr. and Mrs. G. E. Greaves, of Tsolo, Marsh House-lane, has been successful in passing the final examina- tion of the Institute of Chartered Accountants in England and Wales, held in Manchester, May- 26-28. He was articled with Messrs. Broderick. Boardman and Co., 49, Spring Gardens, Manchester, and received his educa- tion at the Boteler Grammar School.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>Deafness and Blindness.</head>
        <p>Deafness and Blindness.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ison (of Ison's Eye and Ear Dispetnsary, Ltd.), 71, Great George-street, Leeds, has a—re- markable record of success. His methods are up-to-date and without operation, and he effects marvellous cures in apparently hopeless cases. Write for testimonials and printed form of questions, sent post free. Mr. Ison attends at the Lion Hotel, Warrington, Wednesday, July 28th, hours 11 to 2-i Union Hotel, Liverpool, Wednesday, July 28th. hours 3.30 to 8.---{Advt.]. Completing Oakwood-avenue School. In connection with the recently received sanction of the Local Government Board for the completion of the Oakwood-avenue Coun- cil School, it has been resolved, by the Educa- tion Committee to accept the tender of Messrs. Craven, Dunnill and Co., Ltd., for the glazed tiling of the dadoee at a cost of £987 10s. 6d., and that of Messrs. Oates and Green, Ltd., for the filling up of the lavatories, &amp;c., at a cost of £lB3.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>St. Barnabas' Sunday ,Sehool Treat.</head>
        <p>St. Barnabas' Sunday ,Sehool Treat.</p>
        <p>A treat in Bewsey Park replaced the usual Walking Day festival so far as the St. Barnabass Sunday' School children and Bible Class members vrere.concerned. The treat took place on Saturday last. There was no proces- sion, the children assembling in the park at •" their own sweet will." The afternoon and evening were spent in sports and games, and in between the numerous events and frolics tea was served. The Vicar (the Rev. J. H. G. Frollingworth), Mrs. Bollingworth and the Sun- day school superintendents and teachers accom- panied the children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>" Swapping " Schools.</head>
        <p>" Swapping " Schools.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the School Attendance Com- mittee, approved at Monday's meeting of the Education Committee, contained the following em :—" The committee having considered cer- tain cases in which children had been re- moved from one school to another, it was resolved that the removal of children from one school to another shall not be permitted if the sole reason therefore be one arising out, of a breach of the disciplinary" measures afiecting the school, and that instructions be given to the head teachers accordingly. No Loans at Present.</p>
        <p>The School Management Committee reported to the Education Committee, at their monthly meeting on Monday evening, that a letter from the Board of Education was read .to them at their last meeting, the same stating, with reference to the proposed purchase of land in Museum-street, that, having regard to the decision on ,the restriction of capital issues recently made by H.M. Treasury, no loan could at present be sanctioned for the pur- chase of the site in question, and it was decided by the committee that the Town Council be informed accordingly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>'Wycliffe P.S.A. Brotherhood.</head>
        <p>'Wycliffe P.S.A. Brotherhood.</p>
        <p>"God's Presence, God's Leading and God's Protection" was the title of the address given to the members of the Wycliffe P.S.A. Brother- hood, at their weekly pleasant afternoon ser- vice on Sunday, by Mr. William Street. Just, he said, as ammunition was needed for fight- ing at the Front, so also was ammunition needed for the souls, not only of fighting men, but for all those who had to stay at home. The latter kind of ammunition could be found in suffi- ciency in the Word of God, and it was the only kind that was of any service in the battle against sin and for the establishment of Christ's Kingdom amongst men.—Mr. Street also ren- dered an appropriate solo.—An expression of sympathy was accorded to six members of the Brotherhood who were prevented by sickness from attending.—The meeting closed with the singing of the hymn "Blessed Assurance !"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Leather Trade Good.</head>
        <p>Leather Trade Good.</p>
        <p>Trade, says the Warrington correspondent of the "Leather World," has, developed a much more all-round character, all selections being well inquired for, with the result that turnover is more rapid, and the greater encouragement offering is inducing some of our tanners to pro- duce more largely in the lines suitable for the general trade. A strong inquiry is made by manufacturers for a fair class foreign bend. Government requirements are still urgent, and most of us have time and opportunity to handle nothing other than pelts imitable for service work.</p>
        <p>Of late there have been extensive inquiries for lighter leathers for France; some consider- able orders have been placed, and -"Freh-ch buyers have been personally over the ground. A serious drawback is still the extreme difficulty in obtaining official permission to export. Prices steadily advance, all classes partici- pating, save Army bends, and according to agreement the price of these for delivery to the end of the year is to remain unaltered. Hides still decline, making the situation ranch easier, and if, as now seems probable, they get down to about 1141., I look to sea a steadying of feather prices.</p>
        <p>County Court Judge Dead. His Honour Judge H. E. Gurner, County Court Judge for this district, to which 01ce he was appointed a little over twelve mouths ago, died, at the age of 61, on Wednesday, after two days' illness, at his residence, 36, Ball- ston Gardens, London, S.W. The late Judge was the eldest son of the late Henry Field Gurner, Crown Solicitor, of Melbourne, Aus- tralia.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Comforts for Irish Soldiers.</head>
        <p>Comforts for Irish Soldiers.</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of the Warrington Branch of the Irish Women's Association a special performance was given at the Star Kinema in aid of the fund for buying .9 =- forts for Irish Regiments and prisoners of svar. The programme cunsisted of Irish pictures and instrumental and vocal music, the vocalist being Madame J. Armstrong, whe sang "Kathleen, Mayourneen," "Killarney" and "Songs my mother used to sing." The pro- ceeds totalled nearly .220.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Year of War.</head>
        <p>A Year of War.</p>
        <p>In connection with the Warrington _Free Church Council a solemn intercession service will be held in the Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church at 7.45 on Thursday evening next. It has been arranged specially to mark the ap- proaching conclusion of the first twelve months of the war. The chairman will be the Rev. W. Thompson Derry (superintendent minister of the Wesleyan Churches) and the speaker will be the Bev. C. P. Pitt (of the Elmyrood- avenue Congregational Church). A colledtion will be taken on behalf of the Belgian Relief Fund, when it is hoped that a goodly amonnt will be contributed to a very needy object. The service is being held in accordance with the suggestion of the National Council of Feder- ated Evangelical Churches, and it is hoped by the promoters that it will be largely attended.</p>
        <p>£4OO Haystack Blaze. Two haystacks—this year's produce—the pro- perty 'of Mr. Warburton, of Bewsey *ere, shortly before Ii o'clock on Saturday night, found to be on fire. The news was im- mediately conveyed to the Warrington Fire Station, and the steam engine "Major," in charge of Deputy-Superintendent Cook, who was accompanied by a good muster of Alen, were soon on the spot. An excellent supply of water was obtainable from the canal, ckse by, but so firm was the hold secured by the flames that the firemen were engaged until 2.30 on Sunday afternoon, when they returned to the station. They succeeded in saving about /5 tons of hay. The damage, estimated at about ..Z4OO, is covered by insurance. The,out- break is thought to be the work of-, an incendiary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>PRESSMEN'S HOLIDAY.</head>
        <p>A PRESSMEN'S HOLIDAY.</p>
        <p>Scribes With The "Jack ". And The A Knife And Fork.</p>
        <p>The Pressman is a retiring species and rarely puts himself or his affairs into printer's ink, fond as he is of performing that function for other folk. Still, there are occasions when he allows himself a small dole of limelight, and we feel that we are entitled to a few lines in which to place on record some few doings of not insignificant note in the archives of War- rington journalism. To come to the point: The local scribes had their annual outing on Saturday, which, thanks to a pair of object-lesson horses, which took mountain sides at a gallop and only Ns-anted water to drink at the end of it all, they ' had an expeditious and exhilarating drive to Grimsditch, where they played bowls and did notable things round a long table with a white cloth—and sundry other items. No outing of Warrington and district journalists would be complete without the presence of Mr. T. '5 -- Steel, that ex-journalist who has found the law less arduous and more lucrative than literature (with apologies); and Mr. Steel wan in his usual place at the head of the table. As for the bowls, several novices surprised themselves, and there were some Titan struggles between mustard-keen beginners with hope-instilling leads and hardened experts "scratched" beyond recognition. The partY mustered over a score and the sport throUgh- out the well-handicapped competition was g.ood• A first-rate and well-earned half-day's diver- sion ended fittingly with a delightful drive home, in which act the horses shone no lees than on the outward trip, landing the party hack in Warrington well before ten o'clock, which, it will be agreed, was only right -and proper for an eminently respectable outing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>ADVERTISE</head>
        <p>ADVERTISE</p>
        <p>"The Examiner" Will rind VOll Customers, Tenants, or Audiences. "The Warrington Examiner," being one of the best read papers in the provinces, 19 always an admirable vehicle by which te convey messages between seller and bnger, employer and employed, landlord and tenallt, entertainer and entertained; in fact between all who wish to do business of any kind. "The Warrington Examiner" is now read every week by thousands of readers more thEilv ever before, its stirring war stories written 1:!Y local men at the front and its excellent portraits of local men who have fallen, bear' wounded or won honours, having resulted in substantially increased circulation. It follows that " The Warrington Examiner ° is more valuable and becomes increaslngl,l more valuable to advertisers. Advertising its own reward if done judiciously, scienti6. cally and in the right class of newspaper. We supply the right newspaper, and our staff ILI at the disposal of customers to give advice all° assistance wherever required.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>WAR LOAN SUCCESS.</head>
        <p>WAR LOAN SUCCESS.</p>
        <p>The greatest loan that was ever raised 1,1 any nation has just been raised in this cour try; and the Chancellor of the Exchequer, Ic announcing this result, expressed, on behalf the House of Commons, hearty thanks to the Press (along with the banks) for its assistance/ in "securing the success of this great nation° effort." The appeal for applications for the loan wail the best advertised appeal on record: ffte resui. was also the best on record.</p>
        <p>Nothing to Offer. "Have you," asked the judge of a reonarit convicted man. "anything to offer the Cisll before sentence is passed?" "No, y'olar honour," replied the prisonpfP "my lawyer took my last farthing:" One Sufficient. Her Father: You must remember, B',t vt.g only have one daughter. Her Suitor: Well—l—er--er—only Want- °lle'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>ReST FOR THE rAim AND FEW CASUALTIES.</head>
        <p>A ReST FOR THE rAim AND FEW CASUALTIES.</p>
        <p>• The Teri:itorials are now away from the ttring-line. Last 4Veek we had no additions to "Ixrt to the Territorial Roll of Honour; and this week there are only three casualties, Which, although new to our list, are not of recent date. One of them appeared in the list af casualties after the great charge. This was Private J. Heyworth, of Golborne, who has now died of wounds then received. These</p>
        <p>three casualties are:— DIED OF WOUNDS. Private J. Heyworth (Go'borne). WOUNDED. Corporal George Cliffe. Private J. Skelhorn. The total losses of the Battalion now Stand : Killed Wounded Missing .444</p>
        <p>Total</p>
        <p>very quiet and we were getting nicely away; indeed we were only a few yards short of being out of rifle range. Then, all of a sud- den, whiz came a bullet right into the pack on ray back. It felt as if somebody had hit me flat on the back with a plank. We continued the march and the chaps talked about my luck and said that I must be bullet-proof. When I came to look in my pack I saw the bullet had gone through two waterproof sheets which were rolled up in it, and also three-parts through my overcoat, so you see that by putting my coat in my pack I had saved-myself."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>CORPORAL GEORGE CLIFFE</p>
        <p>In " A Little" Pain, But "Nothing 're</p>
        <p>Worry About:'"</p>
        <p>News has reached his home, 11, Battereby- Nut, that Corporal George Cliffe, of the 4th gcruth Lancashires, has been wolinded. Two letters describing how he got. his hurts have Itlsq. been received by his parents, who live in Wellfield-street. One of them is from a com- rad% Private Peter Pritchard, who, Writing 4Tukr date Sunday, July 11th, says:— "it is my painful duty to have to inform you 44' Your son George's accident. Buried By Exploding Shell.</p>
        <p>"Part of the trench in which he and his "91Vades were stationed was blown in, the Shit) wounding three and burying George, who, ,when he was dug out, was found to be .unhurt. ft las while he and others were re-building ...ale trench parapet that be got hit in the arm. did not witness the affair myself, but went Ili) to the trencheS with rations the same night. lind to my surprise I found George with his lora, in •a sling, sitting in a dug-out smoking a cigarette. I went in to see if he was in pain,</p>
        <p>Wounded..</p>
