        <p>CORPORAL G. cugFE:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Bullet Knocked His Hat Off.</head>
        <p>Bullet Knocked His Hat Off.</p>
        <p>In another letter the gallant Private told the story of a second narrow escape, this time whilst on early morning duty. "I happened," he wrote, "to be looking down when a bullet struck the sand-bag in front of me. It came through the sand-bag and hit me on the centre of the head, but only marked my hat; it was spent. It rolled off my hat on to the ground. I picked the bullet up; it was hot. I enclose it, and hope you will keep it as a souvenir. I hope this is the only bullet that was made to hit me and then I will be all right" He addff; "I think we have got the Germans beat." "Stepfather Also In The "Terriers."</p>
        <p>Private Skelhorn is only nineteen years of age and he received his education at St. Ana's I and St. Peter's Schools. Up to the outbreak of war he was employed by Messrs. Randall Bros., Ltd., Tanner's-lane. He had been two years in the Territorials when war broke out. His stepfather, Private P. Harper, is also serv- ing at the Front with the "Terriers!' Prior to volunteering for active service, Private Harper worked for the Mersey White, Lead Co., Limited.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>PRIVATE ERNEST WTLLOTT.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE ERNEST WTLLOTT.</p>
        <p>Making For Recovery From Wound</p>
        <p>In The Head.</p>
        <p>Private Ernest Willott, of 9, Howard-street, who was wounded in the head during the " Terriers' " magnificent charge on Tune 16th, is making wonderful-progress towards recovery and expects to be sent over to Eugland at an early date. This welcome news was conveyed through the medium of a letter written to the gallant Private's mother by an Army Scripture Reader, who visits the base hospital in France in which Private Willott is lying. "I thought" he writes, "it would help to cheer you. to know that somebody from the dear old *homeland visits him. I just sit by the bedsides of the wounded men, read God's word to them and pray with them, and my ambition is to win them out and out for Jesus."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>Twelve Years a "Terrier."</head>
        <p>Twelve Years a "Terrier."</p>
        <p>Private Willott, who is a married man, ilas been a member a the Battalion for twelve years. He formerly worked at the Whitecrose Works, and was educated at Orford School. A brother of his. Private Stanley Willott, is serving at the Front, also with the Territorials</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>LATE CORPORAL JOHN</head>
        <p>THE LATE CORPORAL JOHN</p>
        <p>ROSE.</p>
        <p>Doctor Writes That His End Was Quite Peaceful.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Ross, of 66, Forster-street, Warrington, the widow of the late Corporal John Bose, who died from .wounds received in the fighting which followed the memorable charge, has this week received a letter from Major Horace G. Pinches, of the R.A.M.C.' who is in charge of the Lahore (British) General Hospital, "somewhere in France," and who tells her of her husband's last moments alid of his resting- place. "Your late husband," the kindly Doctor writes, "arrived in . this hospital from the Front on June 18th, with a very severe gun- shot wound in the head. He was trephined, but, in spite of every care, he gradually sank, and died on line 24th. His death was quite peaceful and. he suffered very little pain. He was buried on June 25th, in the Calais Ceme- tery, where a large plot of ground has been set aside for the burial of brave British soldiers. The grave has a cross on it and is carefully looked after."</p>
        <p>Corporal Rose was only 23 years of age and his widow is left with one young child. He was th 4 son of a well-known Liberal, and he was prominent in local junior football circles.•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>MISSING!</head>
        <p>MISSING!</p>
        <p>PRIVATE NORMAN WILSON.</p>
        <p>Mother Anxiously Awa i ing Tidings</p>
        <p>Of Her Son.</p>
        <p>An official intimation from the War Office has this week reached Mrs. Wilson, of Selby- street, to say that her son, Private Norman</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <p>PRIVATE J. W. SKELHORNE.</p>
        <p>Wounded.</p>
        <p>111_,Y /light leg gets set. My left leg is t„e ei right now; it was only burned when txbie ell exploded. . . . I am very comfort and am doing as well as can be expected— Pital nice little bed in the cosiest little hoe- Prance." He adds a plea for a few It-bread cakes and " some .nice chocolate:,,</p>
        <p>Two Narrow Squeaks. an earlier letter he urged his mother to tt„,,l'eaP her heart up," and said that he was 114 "Lt the thigh, and in a yet earlier one— wsritte before he was wounded—he reco.unts. e etails of a narrow escape which he had. wet was," he wrote, "very late at night and 11.61'e making for our ,billet. All seemed</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <p>PRIVATE NORMAN WILSON. Missing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <p>Wilson, is missing. She hl - heard nothing from her son since the big charge on June 16, in which he took part. His name had not, however, appeared in any casualty list issued since that charge. Mrs. Wilson trusts that wounded Warringtonians who find their way to the various military hospitals will make inquiries concerning him and that should they hear any tidings they will communicate the same to her.</p>
        <p>—private Wilson, wfio is 19 years of age, was educated at St. Barnabas' School. He was, until the outbreak of wiz, employed in the coopers' department at the works of Messrs. Joseph Croefield and Sons, 'Ltd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>WOUNDED WARRIORS. '</head>
        <p>WOUNDED WARRIORS. '</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSOCIATION</p>
        <p>SEWING CLASS.</p>
        <p>Parcels of gifts have been sent by this class to Raddon Court Hospital, Mrs. B. Fairclough, for the Territorials; Lord Derby Hospital, &amp;c. The President (Lady Crosfield), the committee and the Honorary Secretary (Mrs. E. Osborne) desire to thank all those who have helped and to intimate that the activities of the Sewing Class are suspended until September 2nd, when they hope again to meet all friends and helpers.</p>
        <p>"HANDKERCHIEF TEA" TO PROVIDE</p>
        <p>COMFORTS.</p>
        <p>On Thursday afternoon Mrs. roster- Jackson, of Brookfield, Lower Walton, with the assistance of a committee of ladies, gave a very sucCessful "Handkerchief Tea" in aid of the fund for the wounded in •the Lord Derby Military Hospital, Warrington. About sixty ladies were present, and -a very enjoyable after- noon was spept. The sum of X 7 15s. was realised and has been sent to the committee in charge of the hospital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>MUSIC AT WHITECROSS.</head>
        <p>MUSIC AT WHITECROSS.</p>
        <p>A concert was given by Mr. W. Taylor's War- rington Concert Party in the Whitecros,s Hos- pital on Friday evening of last week. Miss Annie Tayldr was loudly applauded for her rendering of " Little Grey Home in the West" and "Somewhere a Voice is Galling," as also were Miss Cissie Rigby and Mr. George Taylor for their various items, especially the duet, "Larboard Watch," which was rendered in capital stile. Messrs. J. Handley and W. ' Brobbin's concertina duets were among the features of the evening, and the humorous efforts of Mr. J. H. Bibby were loudly encored. Songs Were well rendered by Mr. Robert Fors- ter, whilst a pianoforte solo by Mr. Bibby was much appreciated. • Miss Eva Shaw proved a very efficient accompanist. The entertainment was brought to a conclusion by the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>RYLANDS'S CRICKETERS TO VISIT WINWICK.</head>
        <p>RYLANDS'S CRICKETERS TO VISIT WINWICK.</p>
        <p>Rylands's Rees, engagement with the wounded soldiers at Winwick was unavoidably post- poned from last' week until next week when Rylands's Recs. C.C. will be represented from the following players ,:--E, Saunders (capt.). G. Duckworth (Boteler Grammar School C.C.), G. Woodcock (St. Catherine's C.C. Cambridge), E. Parsons (Chester College C.C.), W. H. Bell and W. Bennett (Warrington C.C.), J. Con.</p>
      </div>
