      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>OF THE HUNS.</head>
        <p>OF THE HUNS.</p>
        <p>with the Territorials, having been a member of that Force practically ever since its incep- tion. Another brother, Private Frank Gan- non, of the Lancashire Fusiliers, has been a prisoner of war since September last. He went out to the Front within a few days of the de- claration of war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>PARCELS FOR PRISONERS.</head>
        <p>PARCELS FOR PRISONERS.</p>
        <p>Tins Must Not Be Used For Consign- ments To Germany.</p>
        <p>With reference to the Order in Council pub- lished on June 24th prohibit:2lg the export of tin to ports in Norway in addition t.) ports in Denmark, the Nethertanqs, Rad Sn eden, the public are requested not to send tins to prisoners of war in Germany. This applies equally to tins containing food and to tin boxes, biscuit tins, &amp;c. used as outer covering for parcels.</p>
        <p>It-is recommended that parcels for prisoners of war shotld be packed in (1) strong double cardboard, or strawboard boxes, or (2) strong wooden boxes, or (3) several folds or strong pack- ing paper. If paper or linen or canvas is used for outer wrapping a duplicate addressed sheet of paper or other material and length of string should be , enclosed inside. Certain articles which are normally packed in tins can be packed in earthenware or glass, and care should be taken to protect them by strong outer ...packing. Attention is again drawn to the necessity of careful and secure packing of articles sent to Germany for prisoners of war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>WHAT WARRINGTON IS DOING AND MIGHT DO.</head>
        <p>WHAT WARRINGTON IS DOING AND MIGHT DO.</p>
        <p>Week by week we have chronicled the pro- gress made by the various relief funds, show- ing their relative needs, and this week we de- sire again to draw our readers' attention to the urgency for a prompt and generous response to the appeals now being made on behalf of prisoners of war who were residents of War- rington or who belonged to the South Lan- cashire Regiment I,3riefly put, the lot of our men in enemy prisons is not a happy one. With the object of making it easier for our gal- lant soldiers to bear their captivity, and to supply them with food to which they are used, and thus sUpplement their niggardly allow- ances, parcels (value about 35.), we are pleased to learn, have been regularly sent (thanks mainly to regimental support) from the Parr Hall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>Men In Captivity.</head>
        <p>The Men In Captivity.</p>
        <p>We desire to emphasise the necessity of this fund. We learn that the names of about 180 prisoners of the South Lancashire Regiment have already come to hand, and this 'number is daily being added to when, and as, the men in captivity find that their friends at home are not forgetting them. We should ask our readers first of all to im- agine what it, is to be cut off from all friendly intercourse, suffering, often, as the result of wounds, to be surrounded by barricades, armed guards night and day, and, worst of all, to be insufficiently fed, -</p>
        <p>Then we would ask them to sit down and write a cheque for the fund, and afterwards bring the fund to the notice of others who are in a position to do likewise. Everyone can help in some degree Every workman in the town could also do his "bit" as each penny counts. Reckoning their numbers approximately at 10,000 our working- men could, themselves, enable the work to be continued by contributing one penny each (which represents a total of about £4O) per week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <p>stable and F. Lemon (Warrington Conserva- five C.C.), J. Roberts (Little Lever' C.C.), S. Carter (Leigh R.F.C.), Bert Renwick (All Blacks R.F.C.), A. Bramhall and F. Horrocks (Warrington R.F.C.), G. McGowan, T. Knight, and J. Wrench.</p>
        <p>'Bus leaves Central Station at 1.15 p.m. Match starting at 2.15 p.m. The Band of the 2nd South Lancs. will be in attendance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Mr. Stewart Royston's Appeal</head>
        <p>Mr. Stewart Royston's Appeal</p>
        <p>To Children.</p>
        <p>Mr. Stewart Royston, Sunnyside, Padding- ton, writing to the members of the Pansy League, in "Great Thoughts" for July, says:— The winter proved very trying and, for some weeks I have had to battle with a serious illness, and since recovering have been think- ing only of the nation's trials, and longing to de something, however little. It makes me wonder if members of the Pansy League are doing their share in thinking of our brave soldiers and sailors who are being maimed,. in thousands, to protect us in our island home. In February I joined hands with a valued Ealing friend to help cheer prisoners who are interned in Rolland and the effort has resulted in a supply of literature going across the North Sea until the terrible war is over. In letters from these men their one wish is for liberty, so that they could join the Allies in France and Belgium, but they are com- pelled to Stet in Holland till peace is de- clared! Oh, that our brave prisoners in Ger- many were being treated as well as the Dutch are doing to-day! Evert single Pansy League member should be doing something, even if it is saving their spare pennies and not wasting them on sweets and senseless literature, but placing their pennies in the P.O. and other Savings Banks. Our greatest thought should be to cheer the soldiers in their sufferings, for they are suffering for US.. It is during the time our brave mo are in hospital that Pansy Leaguers should think of them. When the days seem long and weary and they are so far away from their dear ones, how welcome magazines, illustrated weeklies, games, &amp;c., would be, and lots of these things are found in our homes to-day. I appeal to Pansy Leaguers to hunt them up and send or take them to the nearest military' hospital, where our fighting men are being made well. It will cheer them as well as the self-sacrific- ing nurses who are doing their part so nobly, Now, members of the Pansy League, don't forget the reason you joined, to be thought- ful for others," and our soldiers and sailors, should come first in our thoughts to-day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>"TERRIER'S" GALLAJIT ACT.</head>
        <p>"TERRIER'S" GALLAJIT ACT.</p>
        <p>Writes Home That He Has Won The D.C.M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>DUG COMRADE OUT UNDER FIRE.</head>
        <p>DUG COMRADE OUT UNDER FIRE.</p>
        <p>A gallant member-of the Warrington Terri- torials has written home to inform proud relatives and friends that he has been awarded the Distinguished Conduct Medal for gallantry in action. He is Lance-Corporal Joseph Eaton who, prior to going to the Front, made his home with his brother-in-law and sister, Mr. and Mrs. Hughes, at 23, Dickenson-street.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal Eaton, D.C.M., is a modest hero, and the first intimation which he vouch- safed to his friends that he hzd been singled out for special honour on account of his gallantry, was contained in a laconically- breezy letter in which he enclosed a card printed in gilt letters, signed by Major-General A. Haldane, commanding the 3rd Division, a •dr*ading: -</p>
        <p>An Incident Of The Great Charge.</p>
        <p>that he had been recommended for his signal distinction or that he had been given his stripe. He communicated both facts in a letter to his sister, in the course of which he said: " I don't feel 'fed up' at all! I shall be all right wfien I have seen the lads again. We had a good time on the way back --41enty of free feeds and drinks; indeed, plenty of everything that we required. From Euston to Victoria we had s taxi-cab; it was just like old times... . . When I arrived back 1 learned that I had been made a Lance- Corporal four days previously, and that I had been recommended;. in fact I was pro- moted the very day I left for home. Next time you write me, prase remember my new title and address, your humble servant, as Lance-Corporal J.Eaten, 1961, D Company, South Lancs. Regiment." • AN OLD NATIONAL SCHOOLBOY.</p>
        <p>The plucky Lance-Corporal is a son of the late Mr. Joseph Eaton, who was widely known in the Orford-lane district. The Lance-Corporal will be 28 years of age in the forthcoming November, and altogether he has had somewhere about five or six years in the Territorials. He had finished his term be- fore the war broke out, but when the call came for men he lost no time in re-joining. Of unassuming disposition, the Lance-Cor- poral enjoys great popularity with his com- rades, as well as with a wide circle of acquaintances. He was educated at the National Boys' School in Church-street, and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>To 1961 Private J. Eaton, 4th South Lancs. Regt.,</head>
        <p>To 1961 Private J. Eaton, 4th South Lancs. Regt.,</p>
        <p>Third Division, British Expeditionary Force. Your Commanding Officer and Brigade Commander have informed me that you dis- tinguished yourself by your conduct in the field on the 16th of June, 1915. I have read their report with much pleasure and have brought it to the notice of a higher authority.</p>
        <p>THE DEED THAT WON THE MEDAL. The 16th of June was the date of the ever- memorable charge in which the Warrington Territorials took part, helping to capture</p>
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL EATON.</p>
        <p>D.C.M.</p>
        <p>three lines of the enemy's trenches at the point of the bayonet, and losing heavily in the doing thereof. Private Eaton, as he then was, was home on three days' leave early in the present month and, bit by bit, proud relatives dragged out of him the story of his gallantry—" and it took some doing, for he is not one to talk about himself," his sister ati,o..ed our r,,presentative. It would appear from what the relatives could gather that the gallant " lerrier " was in that portion of one of the trenches where the enemy's shells were falling thickest Five men near him were either killed out- right, or mortally wounded and a sixth— Drummer Peter Wright, whose home is at (A, Scott-street, Warrington—was almost completely buried under a heavy fall of earth which followed the explosion of the self-same shell. Lance-Corporal Eaton heard the half-buried young drummer Call- ing for help and, perceiving his awful pre- dicament, without a moment's hesitation seized a trenching tool and atarted to -dig him out. Before he heard the Drummer's cries the Lance-Corporal had moved to a less exposed part of the trench, and despite the fact that the spot which he had left was open to the enemy's fire—the parapet having been blown away—he ran back and dug away for an hour and three-quarters` and, eventually was able to haul the, by then, almost com- pletely exhausted lad out of what must have proved his grave had no 'comrade been at hand to save ,him.</p>
        <p>DRUMMER WRIGHT. Rescued by Lance-Corporal Eaton.</p>
        <p>was, at the time of volunteering for the Front, employed by the Firth Mattress Co-, as a wire-worker.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal Eaton has a younger brother, Harry, serving under him as a Private in D Company.</p>
        <p>In the course o-f a-brief letter to a friend in Warrington the Lance-Corporal states that he has been decorated with the medal by the general commanding the brigade_ to which he is attached. DRUMMER WRIGHT DESCRIBES THE BRAVE DEED.</p>
        <p>A day or two after the memorable engage- ment Drummer Peter Wright wrote home to his sister, Mrs. Hilton, describing his narrow d- escape from being buried alive anLance- Corporal Eaton's gallant rescue. A ' coal-box,' " he said, "dropped into our trench and buried six of us when it ex- ploded. I was the only one of the half-dozen to come out alive, the other five poor chaps being suffocated. As I lay underneath the soil•I prayed, and my prayer was heard. A chap named Joe Eaton must have heard my cries, for he started digging, and after about an hour and threequarters' work, he dug me out. May God bless him and bring him home safe as a reward for his brave deed, and it was a brave one, for as he worked to..,extri- cate me shells were whizzing past, and some only missed us by inches. . . . I have lost the use of my right arm, but the doctor says I will regain it in a few months." `. His letter, which was laboriously written with the left tand, added that at the time of writing he was in the Canadian Hospital ar Le Touquet and was expecting to be moved to England at any hour. "It is a fine place here, and all the people are very kind," he says, writing of Le Touquet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>SHELLS BURSTING ALL AROUND.</head>
        <p>SHELLS BURSTING ALL AROUND.</p>
        <p>All the time the Lance-Corporal worked the shells of the enemy were bursting above and around, but he seemed 10 lbw a charmed life and he escaped without injury. When the young Drummer was at length e, tr;cated it was found that he had been badly injured in the right arm and, indeed, had temporarily lost the use of that member. His rescuer saw him safely bestowed after his _ terrible ordeal, and subsequently the injured lad was sent across to England and was treated in a military hospital at Sheffield. As Lance- Corporal Eaton worked away with his trench- ing tool the German gunners found the range of the trench_ so well that, by the time he had finished his task, breastworks had en practically swept level.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>PROMOTED WHILST ON LEAVE.</head>
        <p>PROMOTED WHILST ON LEAVE.</p>
        <p>It was not until he had returned to duty after his richly-merited three days' home leave that the gallant Lance-Corporal learned</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>BANDSMAN D.C.M.</head>
        <p>BANDSMAN D.C.M.</p>
        <p>Fsi</p>
        <p>One of the two members of the 2nd Battalion South Lancashire Regiment Band, whose gain- ing of the D.C.M. we reported last week.</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>t 1 .•.;i5ii...::i..:....1...V.i.it•-ii..:..1:1;.:4:;.; . .. ......', •, . , .. :,,,.. ......,,,... —— • . . ..., ... • : - ...............,..„: ~ .. . . , . ... ......., . • .., • :.*** .. .... . ~* .. , ~.:.::,,,:,....:.‘ ,_.., ~.. ...,......... , . .. ... ~ . . ,?.u,.;i:.,;,.•?.. . • '::. ~!..;;,', ,i,... , ' . '.T,',..,:::-.. ~ , , At%•:':'..,:. . . ~.:.,.. ... s ....-- '-'... . ~.,....:..... ... . .... ~.. ~ ' . • '..... Ai&amp;5.§..;:5:,:i..:.. .., ''.';,:•.':::::..,.,.,.: ....... . ~...... . . ' • .:;P:,..c:,....:.,.. :- .... .. . , ~,..,...::"...::::„...,..i'?:y.,..,.,..... . . . • i': ,*.:,.... ...... .. . . .. ;,.-..:,:......,,,:f.:.,...:,............ ........,:, s . ... . . ... ' .......:,?......'...., .; ' . . . .... •*:,,,,, ...,,,.. , • .... • ". * A **,... *** .. ' .*::: ''.... *** . * .. * .. .. ..,.. . .. **; ......, .. ,* ~.... , . .. • ~. .. • • /.: .. . • ~*.f......, . • ...:'.. • . * ....V, .4....,.....,. ' ' ' ' .. ..... . * '.:.• . ?..:.:..1.:,. * .. * ... , .. • • , • . : . . 'i ' :' * ... * * . 5- ...':r..:* .: . ' .....'4. *. • ..:.* . 3.1017.,;,ci ... ....., * .... :,.. s</p>
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL YORKE,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>RESCUED MAN.</head>
        <p>THE RESCUED MAN.</p>
        <p>Drummer Wright is now in the Wharn- cliffs Hospital, Sheffield, where a relative visited hini this week and found him pro- gressing favourably. He is 23 years of age, and he has been a citizen-soldier upwards of twelve years, joining the old Volunteers when eleven years of age, and continuing to serve when the Territorial Battalion was consti- tuted. He received his education at the National School, and, prior to going on active service, was a regular attendant at the Parish Church. lie made his home with his brother-in-law and sister, Mr. and Mrs. John Hilton, at 64, Scott-street. His father was a keen Volunteer and was awarded the Long Service Medal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>War Letters and Photographs.</head>
        <p>War Letters and Photographs.</p>
        <p>We Invite our readers to submit to us letters received from their soldier friends. We shall also be glad to receive photo- graphs of soldiers who have been killed, wounded, mentioned in dispatches or other. wise become a source of interest to the public. The letters will be carefully edited so that nothing will appear in print that ought not to appear.</p>
        <p>The photographs, when suitable, will be published in "The Examiner," which has an established reputation as a picture paper. All letters and photographs are returned after use, undamarid, to the owners.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
