        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>ON HONOUR'S SCROLL.</head>
        <p>ON HONOUR'S SCROLL.</p>
        <p>DIED OF WOUNDS.</p>
        <p>CORPORAL S. C. PECK.</p>
        <p>No Hope Of Recovery From The nrst.,</p>
        <p>Corporal S. Clement Peck, of the 2nd Sbut Lancs. R,egt., has died of wounds receitiod action. News to this effect reached Ns puts, and Mrs. C. Peck, of 148, Liverpool-load, through the medium of a letter written by the sister in charge of No. 2 Clearing stated that Corporal Peck was very sal:pray</p>
        <p>CORPORAL PECK. Died of Wounds.</p>
        <p>wounded in the neck on June 15th, and pabs.e4 away two days later. Everything th4t was possible, the sister added, was done for his tote. fort, by the surgeon and nurses, but from the first there was no hope of his recovery.</p>
        <p>"Lost Some Of My Mates From My Side." Corporal Peck, in a letter to his faaer, written some time back, related some of his experiences in the &amp;ensiles. "We have," he wrote, "been in the firing-line almost all the time since I got here. I have lost some et my mates from my side, but so far, thank Iltod, I've been kept safe, although I have had %Imo narrow escapes. For 14 days I never had-my shoes off, only at odd times. .. . I got made Lance-Corporal for taking a wounded man Prom the trenches under fire. I carried another:Man down from the firing line on Monday." Another letter, written on May 30th, r " I am just within a stone's throw of the biggest ruin in_the history of war—a place whieh_haa not a single building that is not damaged ;,jet, only a short time ago, it was a city withildne squares, fine buildings and a big population., I have passed through it a few times lately,.und seen the ruins, the shell-marked squares, .the burning buildings. What were streets are&gt;ew shambles. I don't think Shakespeare wild have imagined anything to come up to it. . . There are things happening out here that to hear them will almost take your breath many. But, then, that's war, or one of its aspeoft.</p>
