' •.; . ...: . :'.'...::::,.... '.,:::,::',:.:-:;,.:-.,...••:`:' : -'''.' ~...., ;t:.* .0:.;', ',•:', -1.-": ''..•:t : ' i.'''.':'-. • .t,' . . i • , . : t '.... . ' .'.....A.:••': ...,...:.'..'....:.. ' ...::•.:"..1. I..'‘ . ' 3/4Z: .':7'.'' : ' '' . ' ''''' '''''' ' : .4"''...: --::. • '1 ~..,,:i....,... „ . .... ...:;:',,,:i::::.'.'..":,..,;:,;.,,,',..::;:...: ~„,.......; . ~, , , ,;!..- 1 : , ~: .. ~ , . • . ''.4...i, . .: - . . .....k;:',.ii: ......i.::::::::: 1.. ..: .:: . ..: 1 .1. -.....::::::1 :':,...:: ..' .. 1 :. ',.. . ',it. .- : . ,' ,: : ..• ;.._; : . . ; (.... ; ..,' : :' . .:.'' ' ;-: ..... .. . . ...' ::•: . : . . ''':..! • 11: ':.: ...':. •:.: 1: A; : ;1; ;S.:: : .:;.: :::.: : ... :: ''''.....'..... • . ..,::: ' . - . ..; : ' ....' .' '' ' ..: ;;::: . -:- ' ' ' ".. .. - : . I • • . .‘• ' . . " ,... 'S . .; ~...- . 0</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>Villages Them—and Hem</head>
        <p>The Villages Them—and Hem</p>
        <p>Take any ordinary English country village, and walk through it at the close of a -nice summer's day, and what do you see? Nice, cottages, children playing round the doors, eot- tagers tending their gardens, clean streets, quiet by-lanes, a quaint country church, -and all that goes to make a peaceful country 8613I10; "Now change the place to Belgium. Avail: we have a country village, but we have 'time- thing else. What is it? Why, desolation! The cottages are in ruins, the villagers lava gone, the gardens are like shambles, the °banish is levelled all but the crucifix, and the streets are torn, and that which yesterday was apeace- ful country town is now only a rubbish 'Beal?. The crops and pasture lands are in just about the same state. They are torn by_ shells every- where, and trenches run in almost every direc- tion. The saddest things of all are the graves. They are here, there and everywhere; in ones, in twos and often in dozens. But, there, what' matters; all this is war. But surely there's a day of reckoning. The strangest thing of all is that in almost all the ruined churches I have seen the crucifix stands. Whether it is a coincidence or net, of course I cannot say, but from all I've seen out here—and it's not a little —I begin to think it's something more."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>Read of Mons—Then Joined!</head>
        <p>Read of Mons—Then Joined!</p>
        <p>Corporal Peck was 29 years of age. At the beginning of - Seetember, after reading an account of the battle of Mons, he was prompted to enlist, and he went to the Front •hertiy after Christmas. Re was, before the • ar, employed as a foreman at the Partington Steel and Iron Co. Two of his brothers are serving ‘in the Army. One, who is in the 2nd Battalion Cheshire Regiment, was drafted from India to France. The other is in the 3rd South Lanca- shire Regiment. Corporal Peck's father I,- rved four years in the Army, and was also for 20. years in the Cambridge Borough and the Cam- bridge County Constabulary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>BELIEVED TO BE DEAD.</head>
        <p>BELIEVED TO BE DEAD.</p>
        <p>LANCE-CORPL. G. SHAW.</p>
        <p>"Amongst The Foremost Of Those In</p>
        <p>The Attack."</p>
        <p>After an engagement with the enemy on June. 15th, Lance-Corporal George Shaw, of the 2nd Battalion of the Wiltshire Regiment, -wart officially reported to be missing. Since then no word has been heard from him ani inquiries made by his relatives only brought the follow- ing letter from the Captain of his company : " I have only joined this Battalion since the engagement of June 15th. In response to your- letter to the Adjutant I may say that I have made every possible endeavour to discover the actual fate of Lance-Corporal Shaw. He was last seen by one of his comrades during the attack and was then close to the enemy's position. He had been hit and the Corporal from whom I got the information believes that he was killed. Officer Believes Him Dead.</p>
        <p>"After carefully considering the facts I very much regret to say that I have come to the same conclusion. As the enemy's position -woo not taken it is impossible to say definitely. The only alternative is that he might have been wounded and taken prisoner. I am very sorry that I have no better information to convey, and would like to express my deep sympathy with Corporal Shaw's relatives and friends. It may be some slight consolation to them to know that he was amongst the foremost of those who took part in the attack."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>His Last Letter Home.</head>
        <p>His Last Letter Home.</p>
        <p>Writing—two days before the engagement referred to above—to his parents, the gallant Lance-Corporal tendered his thanks for a letter just received from home, and added : "Yon don't know how it cheers a chap up when he gets a letter from his people! .. . AnAbody who comes out of this hell alive is lucky! , • Just fancy being—as we have been—uader Xieavy shell fire for three days and without the oppor- tunity of hitting back. It struck me as being about as equal as a fight between two men would be if one of them had his hands tied be- hind him. . . . Cheer up, and pray that it may all soon be over !"</p>
        <p>A Good Bowler.</p>
        <p>Twenty-six years of age and single, Lance- Corporal Shaw joined the Wiltshire Regiment on September let and went to the Front between two and three months ago. Prior to senlisting he worked for Messrs. Ryland Bros., Ltd. He made his home with his father and mother at 5, Farrell-street, Warrington, and he -was eduoated at the National School. The Lance- Corporal was well-known in local bowling circles, being a prominent member of the Bull's Head Bowling Club, whom he helped to win a number of trophies. He has a brother—Privato S. Shaw—serving at the Front with the King' Own Scottish Borderers.</p>
      </div>
