      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>SCHOOL TEACHERS IN KHAKI.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL TEACHERS IN KHAKI.</p>
        <p>Education Committee Discuss War Allowances.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>MR. UPSON AND A "QUIBBLING SPIRIT."</head>
        <p>MR. UPSON AND A "QUIBBLING SPIRIT."</p>
        <p>"All teachers in the service of the committee, who are now. 'serving, or who shall serve with MM. Forces, shall be paid their full salaries for the time being, less the total amount of Army pay and allowances."</p>
        <p>The foregoing resolution was submitted by the School Management Sub-committee for the approval of the members of the Warrington Education Committee at their monthly meeting, 'held on Monday evening, and after a long dis- cussion was rejected. SUBSTITUTED FOR A REJECTED MINUTE.</p>
        <p>In mcoring the adoption of the minute, Mr. J. -Upson, said that it would be within the recollection of the_ members that a previous minute on the question was referred back to the sub-committee, and he thought that, in putting forward the substituted minute, the sub-com- mittee had tried to interpret what was the evident feeling of the majority of the members of the committee. There were two courses open to the sub-committee. One was to reinstate the original proposition, which was withdrawn when the minutes were submitted to the Coun- cil for approval. Another course was to deal on its merits with each case as it arose and make up the whole or some of the difference between the Array pay the teacher was at present getting and the salary he had been receiving before he volunteered. The latter proposal would, how- ever, render it impossible for the committee to deal with those who had already joined the forces, and it was felt that if those who joined now were to have the difference paid to them it was only fair that those who had joined earlier should also share in the benefit.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Longland seconded. MAYOR MOVES AN AMENDMENT.</p>
        <p>The Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) moved as an amendment that the minute be not con- firmed. He pointed out that the married teachers were at present having and would con- tinue to have their pay made up to the equiva- lent of their salaries. That was quite right. All the single teachers, as also all the other single employes of the Corporation were allowed 10s. a week, no matter what position they held in H.M. Forces. He considered that, in the majority of cases, that was very generous treatment; indeed, he went so far as to suggest that it was model treatment that was being meted out by the Corporation and that the Education Committee could not, in equity, pass that resolution that evening. The minute was originally withdrawn some time ago, and he then promised that it would be fully considered in all its aspects by the Council. sitting in General Purposes Committee. That considera- tion was duly given, each case being gone through,, and it was decided that if in the case of a single teacher or single Corporation employe there was reason to believe that the monetary recompense was not sufficient the 10s. could be and would be increased. Indeed, there were cases, whieh had been dealt with on their merits, in which the amount had been increased. Surely they should not need to say to any single man ' Join the forces and be patriotic and we will make your pay up and keep your job open !" The Corporation were holding out the best con- ditions that could be obtained in Warrington. Each Case On Its Merits.</p>
        <p>If it could be shown that injustice was being d,one to the dependents of any employe, the mat- ter had only to be submitted to the Council and they would deal justly and indeed generously in the matter. He did not want to help in doing anything to stop recruiting; on the contrary, he would like to insist on a lot more of their single employes going to join the forces. Still, per- haps, if they did not like to do it of their own free will there was another way! Seeing that in this matter the Corporation were model employers he respectfully suggested that the Education Committee woule be well advised to let well alone and allow things to go on as they had been doing. If the Council followed the committee's proposed action, what about the manufacturers in the town? Very few of them could afford to do anything near what the Cor- poration were doing, and were they —g-oing to force those men, who already found it a hard job to pay towards their men who had —gone to the Front, to pay towards people who did not want big extra allowances and who, in fact, had not asked for them? Councillor Wilkinson seconded. NO FINANCIAL PENALTY FOR PATRIOTS. Alderman Bennett said he did not think the previous speakers had looked at the matter in quite the proper way. Every man who ieined the Colours did so with his eyes open. He took his life, into his hands; it was a tremendous risk, and even if he did not lose his life, there was the ever-present possibility that he might be incapacitated for the remainder of his days. Whether or not, he was actually called upon to sacrifice his life for his country, it seemed to him (the Alderman) that the man who was pre- pared to make the great, sacrifice, was, in run- ning the risks of battle, making such a big sacri- fice that he should not, by his action, be penalised financially, least of all by Kt body such as that, which had adequate resources at its disposal. He knew, of course, that the monetary considera- tion weighed but little with men who really hived their country—it was quite a secondary consideration—still men who were prepared to make the supreme sacrifice were, at least entitled to expect that a public body that it normal times employed them should ease that sacrifice so far as they could do it. The fact that private individuals were not in a position to follow in the footsteps of a public body in this matter was not necessarily a convincing argument. A pub- lic!, body was altogether in a different position and stood, or should stand, as a model emeloy- ing,body, and whenever it 'could do so, sh—o7E—of inflicting serious hardships on the ratepayers, it ought always to hold the • standard higher than any private concern. THE MUNICIPAL GOOSE AND THE EDUCATIONAL GANDER.</p>
        <p>The Vice-Chairman (Mr. A. .T. Jolley) said that in his opinion the principle laid down by the Mayor and Councillor Wilkinson was a sound .one. Unkind epithets had .been thrown at the Town Council, but in his opinion the treatment it had meted out to such of its employes as had gone to the war bad been generous and extremely praiseworthy, and what was sauce for the municipal goose was sauce for the educational gander. Alderman James Evans_ doubted whether, in view of the action already taken by the 7oun- eil. the discussion Was izt,crder. The Chairman (Councillor H. Roberts) ruled that it was, adding "But the Council could throw our resolution out again', of course!" Councillor Pemberton failed .to see why the employes of one department of the Corporation should have preferential treatment over the employes of others. • Mr. Holman Kingdon supported the amend- ment "on economic grounds purely and simply." He did not think a single man was entitled to the same advantages as a married man,, either at this or at any other time. ,</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Upson asked the Director , , what the pregent scheme wits costing the committee, and wtat would be the cost if the amendment was adopted. The Director (Mr. J. Moore Murray) replied that the amendment would mean a saving of £568 1843. Bd. per annum. If the proposition of the sub-committee was adopted there would still be a net saving of £126 17s. 6d.</p>
        <p>SAVING OUT OF TEACHERS LOYALTY. Mr. Upson thought that answer cut the ground from under the feet of all who had spoken for the amendment. They were at the present time saving over £l6O because a certain number of the teachers of the committee had gone to serve their King and country. That was to say that they were at pre,s'ent saving over 10 a week because those men were plucky and patriotic</p>
        <p>enough to risk their health, their lives; every- thing that life held dear-in order that those at home might live in safety and comfort. And if the original resolution was carried they would still be saving £126 a year. Fancy the Education Committee of Warrington quibbling about these pettifogging allowances to men who were bravely taking their places in the ranks from which thousands of men had already given their lives on the battlefields of Flanders. He had :no patience with .the quibbling-spirit that would whittle down 'to the mipimum‘the allow- ances to be made to such men—men who had not counted their lives 'so dear to gem but f,hp.t they had-offered them, cheerfully and willingly, and from a sense of duty in the service of their King and their country. Education Committee Urged to Set an Example. The question had been raised: Why do for the teachers what it is not proposed to do for others who have similarly offered themselves? His answer to that was that that committee were only legislating for teachers and had no power to legislate for the others. If they had the power he maintained that they would pass a similar resolution to that submitted by the sub- committee for the benefit of all the other Coun- cil employes. If the Education Committee stuck 'to their .guns and passed the sub-commo,tee's recommendation it was possible that the other committees of the Council might come up to the scratch and do the same as the Education Committee were being asked to do. The teachers were valuable men.</p>
        <p>The Mayor: So they all are! Mr. Upon: They are,more valuable than the others.</p>
        <p>The Mayor: Not to the country.</p>
        <p>All Save One of Teacher-Soldiers Promoted.</p>
        <p>Mr. Upson: Some of these men have had two years' military training, and they were all partially qualified when they "joined up." They were familiar with Army drill, and as a proof of their worth I have only to mention that all have received promotion except one, and he is the most-recently joined. " The speaker went on to say that the vast majority of the Education Committees of the country were working along the lines proposed for the acceptance of that committee by the School Management Sub-com- mittee. "I app.-!al to this committee," he added, "in these days of tremendous crisis in our ntti.nia li tu say when men are prepared to lay down their lives for us, that it is not just and equitable that we should quibble about a few shillings per week when we are actually saving out of their patriotism 42300 a year. It does seem to me morsz..eus that we should let these men go, at the risk of limb and life, and then say, in effect We are proud of you. Your patriotism is noble, but we will tak.e from you, because of your patriotism, about .2C4.13 a year." THOSE WHO NEITHER FIGHT NOR PAY. The Chairman said that he felt as strongly as did Mr. Upson that they had no right to penalise any man for his loyalty to England, and men were unquestionably being penalised under the present arrangement. The voluntary army system was on its trial; how near it was to its end none of them could possibly, tell. It was ex- tremely probable that within the next few weeks the country might have it put before them, plainly and clearly, that it was necessary for them to put some form of compulsory service into operation. If they wished to maintain the voluntary system—he was .not quite clear tine: he did—they must put no obstacle in the way of men who were willing to offer their services. The amendment that was put forward that night was plainly imposing an obstacle. Why Not Treat Married and Single Men Alike?</p>
        <p>Personally he could see no reason why a pub- lic body should differentiate between a single man and a married man. The single man was throwing away his opportunities of saving and why should he be deprived of that opportunity and, especially, why should he be deprived of it by those of the community who were doing nothing? That, after all, was the point! The number of men, old and young, who were not contributing to the settlement of the war was very much greater than most people were aware of, and he thought that one of the results of the register which was about to be made would be to disclose a condition of affairs which .co.tid be an eye-opener. If a Corporation could not see its way clear to arrange that a man should not suffer in pocket because of his loyalty—well, so much the worse for the Corporation. He was prepared to support any resolution which would put any man—tharried or single—in as good a position financially as he was before he offered his services to his country. (Hear, hear.) The resolution submitted that night was one way of getting at the pockets of those who did not and would not contribute in any other way. Getting at the Shut-Purses. If the 'Corporation would apply the principle all the way through it might lead to a slight rise in the rates, but it would be hitting at the people who could not be induced to pay up any other way. Let anybody look through the pub- lic subscription lists and he would find that practically the same names kept appearing in them over and over again. The vast majority were doing nothing at all, and they could only be made to do their "bit" through the rates. He would have no compunction or mercy in compelling the class to which he referred to do their duty. If the committee ,put any obstacle in the way of voluntary enlistment on the part of the men serving under them they were but hastening the day when service of another character would have to be enforced. And he was not sure that it would not be a good day for England when such day dawned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>WOMEN TEACHERS FOR MEN.</head>
        <p>WOMEN TEACHERS FOR MEN.</p>
        <p>In reply to Councillor Dickenson, the Director of Education replied that the places of most of the teachers who had gone to the Front had been temporarily filled by lady teachers. Mr. J. Jarman thought that the resolution would be making a lot of people pay twice over. In reply to further remarks the Chairman said he was prepared to stand by his statement that the number of ratepayers in Warrington who were contributing to the war funds, Svc., represented by no means the majority of the ratepayers in the town; indeed the number who were not contributing in any way formed a large majority. In these days of national crii;3 and danger nob Ody should escape. _</p>
        <p>The amendment was carried by 11 votc.,3 to 7</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>SEVERED TENDONS.</head>
        <p>SEVERED TENDONS.</p>
        <p>Surgical Skill Restores Youth His Lost</p>
        <p>Wrist-Power.</p>
        <p>To the Deputy Judge, at the. Warrington County Court on Thursday, Ernest Ogden, a youth residing ftt 10, Soho A. Itw, Warrington, applied through Mr. A. Browne. solicitor, for an award for the payment into Court hy the rearson and Knowles' Coal and Iron Co., War- trington, under the terms of the Workmen's Compensation Act, of the sum of £25.</p>
        <p>Ati.-Brawne.explained to lais Honour that the youth was hurt by,a bottle, which broket, whilst he was at his work, and cut the tendons of his right wrist.' The company paia him I.os. a week compensation- until May last, when it was found that the tendons having been stitched, were beginning to work again and had &amp;ince so successfully continued to improve that all the doctors concerned in the case agreed that the lad was now fit for. light work and_ maul&amp; ultimately, recover the normal use of his wrist; probably in a month or two. After having heard the evidence of Dr. Martin—who detailed the condition of the in- jured wrist—his Honour made an award for the payment into Court of the sum_asked for by Mr. Browne. Mr. Percival (of Messrs. Venables and Sons, insurance brokers, Warrington) under- took to pay the money into Court the following day and also to pay Mr. Browne his costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>DRESS BASKETS, SUIT CASES,</head>
        <p>DRESS BASKETS, SUIT CASES,</p>
        <p>J. S.</p>
        <p>GARLAND,</p>
        <p>51, 53 &amp; 55, BRIDGE STREET, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>NATIONAL REGISTRATION.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—May I call attention to the advertise- ment appearing in your columns asking for the voluntary help of ladies, and gentlemen over military age, in the distribution and col- lection of the Registration Forms within the Lymm urban area? For the clerical work very generous offers have been received, but more distributors and collectors are needed..</p>
        <p>As this is an opportunity for those whose age or occupation debars them from the more exacting services to their country at this criti- cal time, may I make an urgent appeal to all such to render all the aid they can in this registration work? _ _</p>
        <p>Teachers at the various schools and members of the local platoons of the. Cheshire Volunteer Regiment could be of invaluable assistance. The list must be complete by Saturday night, July 21th.—Yours, &amp;c., R. HARRY GOTOBED, Chairman Lymm Urban District Council. Council Offices, Lymm„ July 23rd, 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>"THE LLOYD G EOll E BUSINESS."</head>
        <p>"THE LLOYD G EOll E BUSINESS."</p>
        <p>Weak Whisky Sold By Mistake : Summons Dismissed.</p>
        <p>At Lymm Petty Sessions on Wednesday, be- fore a Bench over which Mr. Watkin presided, Mr. David Molyneaux, licensee of the Plough, Lymm Station, was summoned for selling whisky that was 29.2 degrees below proof.—Mr. Hallard, a county inspector under the Foods and Drugs Acts, prosecuted, and the defence was in the hands of Mr. Charles Howard, of Manchester. A Small Whisky.</p>
        <p>Evidence was given by John Bracegirdle, the inspector's assistant, who stated that he entered the Plough on June 4th and ordered a small whisky. When he had been served he asked for half a pint in a bottle. Defendant's daughter was serving, and she drew the .second lot of spiri from the same keg as she had taken the first quantity. Witness then called the Inspector in. Cross-examined, the witness admitted that defendant's daughter said she had been told by her father not to take whisky from that par- ticular keg.</p>
        <p>The Wrong Keg.</p>
        <p>Mr. HaHard, giving evidence, said that when he told the defendant's wife and daughter, who were in the bar when he was called in, that a sample was to be sent to be analysed, Mrs. Molyneaux said that her daughter had no right to serve from that keg, as her father had told her not to, he having "made it up" owing to the "Lloyd George business." The whisky was analysed and found to be 29.2 below proof. A Satisfactory Explanation.</p>
        <p>Mr. Howard said that, on the 29th of April, Mr. Lloyd George, in the House of Commons, intimated that it was his intention to put a tax on whisky and make dilution by the publi- can legal. The new tax and the right to dilute came into force immediately, but were subsequently rescinded, and Mr. Lloyd George gave instructions that the diluted whisky was not to be sold. That was the explanation of the matter, and a perfectly satisfactory one, he submitted. All the other -whisky in the house was up to proof.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>"A Little Carelessness."</head>
        <p>"A Little Carelessness."</p>
        <p>The magistrates retired to consider their decision, and, on their return, the Chairman announced that he and his colleagues were convinced that the matter amounted to a mis- take due to the rescinded proposals of the Government They felt that there had been a little carelessness, perhaps not culpable, and had decided to dismiss the case on payment of the costs, which amounted to a sovereign.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>lIIS FOUR lILNDIIEDTII SERMON.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Minister's Record.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening the Rev. J. Gunnell, who occupied the pulpit at the Wesleyan Church, mentioned that the sermon he was about to preach would make the four hundredth he had preached since he first occupied the pulpit in 1889. That, he thought, was a record that could be rarely equalled by Methodist ministers owing to the system of. periodically changing them in vogue. He added that not more than 20 persons were in the congregation that night who had been present at his first sermon. He Wondered if anyone of that number could re- member the subject, for he was going to preach on the same theme once more, viz., Proverbs iv. 23: "Keep thine heart with all diligence, for out of it are the issues of life." The heart, he argued, was the centre of all things. As a man was in his heart, so was he in his</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>TO HELP POOR BELGIANS.</head>
        <p>TO HELP POOR BELGIANS.</p>
        <p>J A meeting of the Parish Council was held on Monday, Dr. Murray being in the chair. Mr. IWithinshaw was in tre vice-chair, and the ollowing were also present: Miss Tinsley, Nfessrs. R. Routledge, W. Davies and J. Wood- ward, with Mr. E. Forster (clerk). For Destitute Belgians.</p>
        <p>The Chairman announced that he was bring- ing before the Council a proposal to form a committee to run a fund, "jointly with Sankey," to help destitute Belgians in Bel- gium, the money to be raised by means of "Flag Days," &amp;c. Mr. W. Furness and him- self, he was going to suggest; should be ex- officio members. The committee, he would further suggest, should also contain one or two of the Parish Councillors and three or four representatives of ,the Penketh Tannery War Fund Committee. Miss Tinsley and Mr. J. G. Withinshaw, he suggested, should represent the Parish Council. Resignation Accepted and a Successor Named.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Midwood asked to be allowed to resign his position as manager of the Penketh Council School owing to his removal from the district. Dr. Murray moved that the resignation be accented, and that a vote of thanks be tendered to Mr. Midwood for his past services. Mr. J. Woodward proposed and Mr. J. G. Withinshaw seconded, that Mr. J. Backhapse be asked to take Mr. Midwood's place. This was carried. The National Register.</p>
        <p>Dr. Murray said that ladies were required to undertake the work of compiling the new National Register. If sufficient names were not forthcoming a special meeting would be necessary to devise ways and means of obtain- ing help. Misleading to Travellers.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. (1 Withinslaw , complained that the station name-board was very misleading to strangers coming to Penketh, as the designation of Fiddler's Ferry did not give travellers a clue to their destination. He therefore moved that the 'Clerk be instructed to communicate with the London and North-Western Railway Com- panv with a view to the alteration of the name of Fiddler's Ferry to Penketh. Dr. Murray seconded, and it was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>County Bursar.—At a meeting of the Cheshire County Education Committee at Crewe on Mon- day a county bursary in accordance with the County Senior Scholarship scheme was awarded to Annie J. Winnall, Grappenhall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>lettrts to the abitor</head>
        <p>lettrts to the abitor</p>
        <p>BUTTONS FOR DISCHARGED MEN</p>
        <p>am sending you 13 points on "Why should not a man discharged be granted 'a. button by the Government?" 1. .Young man goes to join the Army. Her does not know whether he will pass or not, because he has a slight illness. 2. He passes the doctor and is so delighted' that he will be able to serve his country. 3. He gets on well for a time, but finds out that his illness is making an effect on him and that he has to give up on a long march, or while going through some other part of his daily exercise. 4. The officer in charge notices him falling out and reports it to the doctor. 5. The officer tells him he must go before the doctor again. 6. He goes before the doctor, who tells him he will have to be discharged and that the soldier's life will not do for him.</p>
        <p>7. The young fellow is disheartened that his assistance to the Army; is finished. 8. He 'goes back to his work that he gave op to assist his country. 9. As he passes through the streets daily he is looked at and spoken of as. a traitor because he has not got a Government button in his coat.</p>
        <p>10. People stop him jn the streets and ask why he is not assisting his country, and he gets tired of showing his discharge paper, be- cause they won't believe him unless he shows them the paper. 11. This discharge paper is being shown in all sorts of weather, wet and fine, and he finds that it is getting wet and dirty and is getting too bad to show the people. _ _ _</p>
        <p>12. He comes ta the conclusion that his paper will be of no use to him shortly and that he will be unable to get another. 13. If they can give a man on Government work one, cannot the Government give a button to a man who has assisted his country and been rejected through no fault of his own?</p>
        <p>REJECTED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>SAND-BAGS FOR THE ARMY.</head>
        <p>SAND-BAGS FOR THE ARMY.</p>
        <p>Sir,—lt may not be common knowledge that sand-bags, in 'large quantities and continuous supply, are urgently needed by our men at the Front at the present time. The cast of materials is about sevenpence bag, and the work is such as can be undertaken by any adult with some experience in heavy sewing, but it is not work for young children. It might well be done by " handy " men be- longing to Church Men's Societies, or by the older members of Boys' Brigades and Scouts. The bags must be thoroughly well made, and of the exact required finished size-3a inches by 14 inches; otherwise they are useless. Full particulars may be obtained from Miss 31. L. Tyler, .Linden House, Highgate-road, London, N.W.</p>
        <p>I have, myself, already made and sent away thirty bags, and hope to send another ten in a few days- - I shall be pleased to give further informa- tion, from the papers in my possession, to any societies Seriously contemplating the work. ARTHUR DOWN. July 23rd, 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Sunday Scheel Treat.—The annual treat in connection with the Wesleyan Sunday School took place on Thursday, when upwards of tIV° hundred scholars and teachers assembled in the schoolroom at one o'clock and were marshalled in procession. They then marched to Victoria- square, where special tramcars were waiting to convey them to the Manchester-road terminus, Warrington, en route for Twiggery Farm, where the afternoon was spent. The weather was very favourable, and a most enjoyable time was spent. The children were served with tea at four o'clock by the teachers and workers, and a large number of parents and friends had tea an hour later, after which the scholars took. part in races, &amp;c., suitable prizes being awarded. The sports were ably carried out by Messrs. E. A. Whitfield and Keen. The usual nut-scrambling followed. At dusk the scholars assembled in the farmyard, and after giving three hearty cheers for Mr. and Mrs. Woodcock for their kindness in allowing them the use of the farm, and singing the National Anthem, tre7 -pro- ceeded to the special tramcars, which were again waiting. Stockton Heath was• safely reached at nine o'clock. '</p>
        <p>- :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>Death of Mrs. J. Strath.—We regret to an- nounce the death of Mrs. J. Smith,. who passed peacefully away at her residence, Fernbank, Houghton Green, on Friday, the 16th inst., at the age of 74 years. She was born at Great Budworth, Cheshire, and up to the time of her marriage lived in' that district. After marriage she removed to Houghton Green, and from thence to Finger Post Farm, Warrington, afterwards returning to Houghton Green, where she has lived for the last 16 years. She has been a regular attendant and active worker at the Wesleyan Church, Pad- gate,,whers she was very highly respected. The funeral took place at Christ Church, Pali- gate, on Tuesday afternoon. Previous to the interment a short service was held in the Wesleyan Church, conducted by the Rev. A. J. Morton. He paid a great tribute to her humble character and generous disposition, showing how, even in her retirement from active work owing to declining years, she had taken a keen interest in Church life and was always anxious to further the good cause she had at heart. Her favourite hymns, "Jesu, lover of MY. Soul," and "Nearer, my God, to Thee," -were sung, the organist playing Diendelssohn's "0 rest in the Lord" during the service. Many old friends attended the service to pay their last respects and the following were also pre- sent at the funeral: Mr. J. Smith (husband), Messrs.' John, Charles and William Smith (sons), Mr. Ezard (son-in-law), Mr. J. Ezard, Mr. W. Newton, Mr. S. C. Drinkwater, Mr. J. Sprotson, Alderman A. Bennett, Mr. F. Wright and the Rev. E. Chamberlain (vicar-in-charge of the parish). The bearers were Mr. W. Smith (nephew), Mr. J. Norman (nephew), Mr. J. Walker, and Mr. J. Howard. There were a large number of beautiful floral tributes, sent by: Husband and , son, "John and Roney," "Willie and Jeannie and family," "Edith and Herbert and family," "Herbert, Rolley and Cissie," Mr. and Mrs. Spencer, Mrs. Henry Smith, Mrs. William Smith, Mr. J. Sprosten and family, Mr. and Mrs. Drinkvrater, "Willi and Pollie and family," "Walter and Lillie," "Joe and Annie and family," Mr. T. Codling and family, Mr. and Mrs. W. F. Newton, W- and Mrs. T. B. Duckworth, "All at Lynd- hurst," Alderman and Mrs. Arthur Bennett, Mr. and Mrs. Joseph Ezard and family, Mrs. T. Bennett and "Nellie and Percy," Mrs. E. an° Miss Richardson, Mrs. J. Houghton and "Ada," "Fanny," Dr. and Mrs. Anderson, Mrs. Dean and son, Mrs. James White and family, Mr; and Mrs. H. A. Webb, Mr. T. and Mr. an(l Mrs. J. Sudlow, Mr. and Mrs. Roscollor all° family, Rosa M. King, Mr., Mrs. and Ali° Gandy, Mr. and Mrs. F. Wright and familY, Mrs. W. Royston and family, Mr. and MiS_l Granger, Miss and Miss Alice Howard, Mr. ana Mrs. Joseph Howard, Mr. and Mrs. W: Dakin• Mr. and Mrs. James Mason,and "Lily." The funeral arrangements were ably carried out hY Mr. Walter Smith.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>Mfir M Ma, C • • • • -• • 444, Al t,;* ‘.`s• .4. .4. I.'l • .4 t.l°.',Nvzpb. , A• 4</head>
        <p>Mfir M Ma, C • • • • -• • 444, Al t,;* ‘.`s• .4. .4. I.'l • .4 t.l°.',Nvzpb. , A• 4</p>
        <p>^</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 24, 1915.</p>
        <p>SOLDIER'S MOTHER IN GAOL.</p>
        <p>Tried To Get Money By Fraud From Army Paymaster.</p>
        <p>PRIEST'S PLEA FOR MERCY</p>
        <p>A woman of sixty-three was summoned at the Borough Court on Thursday for attempting to obtain money from the Regimental Pay- master at Winchester by fraud. The Chief Constable informed the Bench (presided over by Mr. J. RSrmer Young) that the case was undertaken by the Director of Public Prosecutions, Whitehall. He stated that defendant's son was in the Army. Prior to the war he had been employed by a local firm at a wage. of 188. a week. Of this sum she had stated that her son paid her 17s. a week for his board and lodging, adding that he ob- tained his pocket-money ih his spare time by other means. When he left the firm that employed him and enlisted. 9s. a week (half his wages) became payable to some dependent. Defendant had that, but found that there was a possibility of her obtaining from the Govern- ment a separation allowance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>Made A False Statement.</head>
        <p>Made A False Statement.</p>
        <p>By making false statements as to . her son having lived with her and, been her. support up to the ,time of the outbreak of war, she tamed a grant of 10s. a week. The Pensions' b if cer (Mr. Herbert Webster), however, hap- pened to notice in a report in "The Examiner" of defendant's son having been wounded, that the address given was not defendant's address. He therefore made inquiries and found that defendant had made false statements, in that her son had not lived with her for a matter of about three years. He had lived with some other "'relatives and paid 11s. 6d. a week for his board and lodging, so that he could not have had a great deal to spare to help to sup. port his mother. The defendant (Mr. Nicholls concluded) did not seem to understand what she was accused of. She said: "I have ob- tained nothing." She did not realise, appar- ently, that she was accused not of obtaining, but of attempting to obtain. Harold :Lawton testified' to filling up defend- ant's application form at the Parr Hall: Discovered Through "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Mr. Herbert Webster (the Pensions' Officer) told how he noticed the difference in the ad- dresses when reading "The Examiner," and also corroborated the statement that defend- ant's son made money in his spare time. James Baxter and Detective' Anderson fol- lowed in the witness-box. _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Another police witness said that defendant told him she would not have done it but for being "egged on" by other people. Asked if she wanted to make a statement, defendant, who appeared considerably upset, had little to say. She admitted that she had said the lad was her support, and it was true he did give her money every day.</p>
        <p>Similar Fraud's Rife in the Country.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said there was nothing else against defendant, and went on to state that the kind of fraud to which defendant had resorted was rife all over the country, and it was felt that cases should be dealt with in an exemplary manner. The Chairman: It's a question, whether the woman is stupendously idiotic and does not know what she is doing, or whether she is pretending to be stupid. The Chief Constable: A lot of them do that, your Worship. Country Swindled Right and Left.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (the Bench laving spent. sev- eral minutes discussing the 'Matter amongst themselves): It is a very painful case, and has caused us a good deal of anxiety. The prac- tice of fraud.is very widespread, and the country has been swindled right and left. Under the circumstances, we do not think we should be acting wisely or fairly if we were to let you off. The decision of the Bench is that you go to prison for a month. Defendant fell back against the wall of the box when the sentence was pronounced, and was removed in a half-fainting condition. Priest Says She Was Misled By Stupid People. At the conclusion of the ordinary business of the Court, an appeal was made to the mag- istrates, by a Roman Catholic priest, to modify the sentence, in view of various circumstances that called for mercy, amongst them the de- fendant's age and the fact that she had three sons in the Army, at present fighting in Prance. Defendant had never had anything else against her. Personally, he was convinced that her act was that of a foolish woman acting on the advice of a number of stupid people living near her. Bench Refuse to Revise Decision.</p>
        <p>The Chairman announced, ,after 'a consulta- tion with his colleagues, that the Bench felt it was a serious matter; that sort of thing would have to be stopped, and they could not see their way to alter their previous decision.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>DOCTOR AND MONEY-LENDER.</head>
        <p>DOCTOR AND MONEY-LENDER.</p>
        <p>Interpleader Action At Warrington Count Court.</p>
        <p>An interesting inter-pleader action was heard by Deputy Judge Sellars in the Warrington County Court on Thursday. The claimant was Dr. Gray, of Earlestown, and the execution creditor was Mr. Phillip Horivich, a Manchester moneylender. Mr. Arthur Browne, solicitor, 'Warrington, appeared for Dr. Gray and explained that at the Manchester Assizes in November .last Mr. Horwich obtained judgment by consent against Mrs. Adelaide Gray, the wife of Dr. Gray, for the sum of £125, including costs. Execution was stayed for six months. The amount not then having been paid Mr. Horwich issued a writ of Fi Fa and instructed the Sheriff's Officer to levy on the goods of Dr. Gray, alleging that they were Mrs. Gray's, and not the doctor's. The result was that the District Registrar of the High Court at Manchester had ordered an issue to be tried by the judge of the Warrington County Court as to who the goods belonged to. and directed that the sum of £142 4s. 6d. should be paid into Court. That amount had been duly lodged. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>Furniture That Was Seized.</head>
        <p>The Furniture That Was Seized.</p>
        <p>Shortly, the facts were that D. Gray, by deed executed on December 14th, 19054 purchased for a very substantial sum the practice at Earles- town of Dr. Gange, and in addition bought some of the doctor's household furniture, paying him £65 11s. 6d. for the latter. Other furniture in his house was lent by his step-mother, by the consent of his father's trustees, Such furniture being old family furniture. This furniture was seized by the Sheriff's officer, together with other articles that the doctor had bought from time to time, in proof of which purchases a large bundle of receipts was produced for the inspec- tion of the Court.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>His Honour's Finding.</head>
        <p>His Honour's Finding.</p>
        <p>Mr: Ray, barrister, of Manchester (instructed by Mr. Larimer Wilson,. solicitor, Manchester), appeared, for Mr. Horwich: After a lengthy hepring his Honour found in favour of the claimant, Dr. Gray, for the return of the Whole of the household furniture seized, with the exception of a roll-top desk and a Chippendale cabinet, which he assessed at the value of £5, and which Mrs. Gray had admitted, in the witness box, had been given to her by the doctor as birthdal-presents. His Honour there- fore ordered the sum-of £137 4s. 6d. to be paid out of Court to Dr. Gray, together with costs on the C scale, and gave judgment for Mr. Horwich for £5 with costs on the lowest scale, further ordering Mr. Horwich to pay the Sheriff's officer (Mr. Jas. P. Reynolds, of Liver- pool) the sum of £7 15s. as costs. On ,the application of Mr: Ray, his Hannur stayed execution for 14 days, with a view lo an appeal being lodged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>-,_ li DISTRICT.IEW3 1 r ". . I !- I ; .. ....</head>
        <p>-,_ li DISTRICT.IEW3 1 r ". . I !- I ; .. ....</p>
        <p>lIYMM</p>
        <p>Soldier Seriously 111.—Mrs. James Leah, of The Rookery, Booth's Hill, Lymm, yesterday received an official report from the War Office to the effect that her husband, Private James Leah, Of the Lymra Territorials, is in hospital, seriously ill. The Bull's Head.—Joseph Charles Gatley ap- plied at the Lymm Sessions on Wednesday morning for a temporary.. transfer of licence to sell intoxicating liquors at the Bull's Head, Lymm. It was stated that he had served 12 years at the Cross Keys, Knutsford, had an excellent character, and was a good man at his trade.—The application was granted.</p>
        <p>A toldier's.'Grave.—Under the fresh green sod in Lymm Churchyard lie the bones of a gallant old warrior who fought and won a medal at Waterloe a hundred years ago. His name is John Wheeler and his regiment was the 13th Light Dragoons. He was also awarded the medal for the Peninsula War, with bars for the Battles of Toulouse, Nives, Salamanca, Vittoria, and Cuidad Rodrigo. lie was wounded in an engagement near Badajos.</p>
        <p>Cheshire Volunteer Regiment.—The Lymm platoon of the Cheshire Volunteer Regiment, under the command of Captain Williamson and Sergeants Moston and Farrow, on Saturday met the Altrincham Battalion at the Market Place, Altrincham, and marched to Cheadle for extended order drill. The Commander of the Altrincham Battalion was in 'charge of the proceedings, which passed off very successfully, and the men were warmly complimented by the Commander.</p>
        <p>Higher Lane Baptists.—The anniversary of the Higher Lane Baptist Sunday School occur:ed on Sunday, when special sermons were preao.;ied by the Rev. J. Richards, of Bury. There w,3re ex- cellent congregations, and the collections %,ere very, gratifying. During the day the singing of the children, who had been trained /or the occasion by Mr. Atkinson, of Warrington, was immensely enjoyed.- In the afternoon, at a children's service, presided over by Mr. Harry Brown, an address was delivered by the Rev. Mr. Richards.'</p>
        <p>"The Peace Bell."—The Rev. Dempster, delivering a sermon at the Lymm Parish Church, some little while ago, inaugurated the peace chimes that sound every day from the church tower, between five minutes to twelve and the mid-day hour. The reverend gentle- man asked his hearers to devote five minutes every noon to prayers for peace, and, in order that they might not forget, the Peace Bell was instituted. During the service the congrega- tion sang the hymn written by Sir W. H. Baker :</p>
        <p>"0 God of Love, 0 King of Peace, Make wars throughout the world to cease; The wrath of sinful man restrain; Give peace, 0 God, give peace again."</p>
        <p>TERRITORIAL ROLL OF HONOUR.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>MILK FAT.</head>
        <p>MILK FAT.</p>
        <p>Summons Against Lymm Farmer Dismissed.</p>
        <p>William Moston, of Booth's Hill Farm, Lymm, was summoned at the Lymm Sessions on Wednesday, before Mr. A. Watkin (in the chair), Messrs. A. W. Callender, It. H. Gotobed, T. J. Ridgway, S. Davies and S. Gould, -for sell- ing milk which was 13.3 per cent, deficient in milk fat. Mr. Haßard, one of the inspectors under the Foods and Drugs Act for the County of Cheshire, prosecuted, and Mr. T. S. Steel, of Warrington, defended.</p>
        <p>Met the Man with the Milk.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hallard said that on 2nd June lie went to defendant's dairy at Booth's Hill, saw Mrs. Moston and asked her to supply him with a pint of milk. She served him, and he returned her one third of it, informing her that he was going to send another third to be analysed. He had obtained a certificate stating that the milk was 13.3 deficient in milk fat. When he gct the certificate, he went and asked defendant if he could account for the deficiency. Defendant replied that he could not account for it. He said he got the milk from a farmer and that it was just as he had received it. He (Mr. Hallard) arranged to meet the man who brought the milk next morning and took a sample of that. That sample was all right. Consequently, he could do nothing else but in- stitute proceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>"All That Was Left."</head>
        <p>"All That Was Left."</p>
        <p>A Magistrate: Was it morning or evenlng milk?</p>
        <p>Mr. Hallard : Morning milk in both instances. Mr. Steel: When you took Ihe milk at T.Le house, the quantity in the pan was rather small, wasn't it?—There was about a gallon and a half left.</p>
        <p>And the milk had been there, and people had been served from it, since 7.30 in the morning? —I should think it had been re-charged during that time.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel: No, it had not. Mr. Steel went on to cross-examine the witness on the matter of the effect that weather and changes of the cows' food may have on the milk, and also the effect of calving. Mr. Hallard admitted the force of these arguments and also that irregular milking was liable to make the morning milk poorer than the evening milk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>Seasons and Circumstances.</head>
        <p>Seasons and Circumstances.</p>
        <p>Addressing the Bench, Mr. Steel said that the standard of 3 per cent., of which the sample taken from defendant fell short by .4 per cent., as only a presumptive standard, and if they (the magistrates) were satisfied that the milk was sold as it came from the cow, it was a case for dismissal. The Board of Agriculture realised as was shown by their circulars, that seasons and circumstances altered the strength of a cow's milk. The defence in all such cases, then, was to prove that the sample of milk came from the cow. Their Worships were dealing that morning with people who had a long and honourable reputation. Mr. Moston had been carrying on business since the death of his father. Defendant's father and grandfather carried on the business, and although samples had been taken for three generations, from time to time, that was the first complaint—aiit had been made; and the milk was not Mr. Moston's, but Mr. Gibbon's. The milk, when it came from Mr. Gibbon's farm to Mr. Maston's house, was put into a pan. It was a well-known fact that cream could not be extracted from warm milk without the use of a separator. Mr. Moston hadn't a separator. May a Bad Month.</p>
        <p>In the course of his experience of cash such as they were now dealing with, he had .learned that well-fed cows fro ni time to time gave. Milk much more deficient in fat than the sainple under notice'. The month of May was a -tad month for milk, and it was only the 2nd of June when the sample Flu; taken. • It was in May that the cows were first turned out, which, of course, meant. that-they had a. change.of food. It was recognised that the Change of food caused a temporary change in the quality of the milk. Cow's milk was liable tn.4aily varia- tion, due to the fact that atmospheric Changes were always taking place. Harry Edward Gibbon, a farmer, of Yew Tree Farm. Lymm, was the first witness called by Mr. Steel. He stated that in May and late April four of his cows had calves, and a fifth animal that he bought had just calved when he purchased it. The witness also testified "to the fact, that the milk sent to Mr. Moston was straight from the Cow. Corroborative evidence was given by Wm. Cadman and Harry Cadman (both employed on Mr. Gibbon's farm), and by Mrs. Sarah Moston. The Chairman (after the Bench had retired) announced that he and his collenves had unanimously decided to dismiss the case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>Four Slightly Wounded And One Seriously 111.</p>
        <p>None of the four. Lymm Territorials who are this week reported wounded are seriously hurt, but one who was wounded a week or so back is seriously ill. He is Private George Taylor, of Rush Green. The other four are: Lance- Corporal F. Kay, Private H. -Kay, Private Haddon and Private Thomson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>PRIVATE GEORGE TAYLOR.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE GEORGE TAYLOR.</p>
        <p>Private George Taylor, son of Mr.dind Mrs. Oldham, of Rush Green, Lymm, was reported, some short time ago, to be wounded, but not very seriously. Yesterday morning, however, Mr. and Mrs. Oldham received a notification to the effect that he was lying seriously ill in No. 2 Canadian Hospital, Le Treport, France. Private Taylor, who was previously reported wounded in the stomach, was employed, prior to his enlistment, by Mr. Samuel Davis, farmer, of Heatley, He belongs to Ly-mm and attended Oughtrington School as a boy. 'He had been in the Territorials about two years and went out to the Front shortly after the outbreak of war. His brother, Harry, is at present in training for the Front.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>TWO BROTHERS WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>TWO BROTHERS WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal Fred Bay and Private Harry Kay have been slightly wounded, the former in the back and lefVoot, and the latter in the left arm.</p>
        <p>Their mother, Mrs. Kay, of Rush Green, received field postcards from them as first intimations, but yesterday had a letter from Sergeant Clare, giving more details and an assurance that their wounds were nothing serious.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>Lance-Corporal F. Kay.</head>
        <p>Lance-Corporal F. Kay.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal F. Kay, who is 26 years of age, is well known locally as a footballer. He Was captain of the Oughtrington Football Club. Commencing his football career with Ought- rington St. Peter's, in the 1905-6 season, Lance- Corporal Kay helped the club in its career in the Altrincham League. When the club dis- banded,, Lance-Corporal Kay joined Oughtring- ton Amateurs, and .he had a lot to do with the success of the club in winning the champion- ship of Division Two of the North Cheshire, League in 1909. During the time they have been in the Senior Division, the Amateurs have won no premier - League honours, but they have maintained a good position. Originally Lance-Corporal Kay played_ as right-half, but since then he has adopted the full-back game, of which there is, perhaps, not a more able exponent in the League. When he played as a half-back, Lance-Corporal Kay represented the Rest against the Champions in the annual match. It is interesting, and a testimonial to Lance-Corporal Kay's skill as a footballer and personality as a leader, that he has cap- tained every team he has played for since 1909.</p>
        <p>The brothers are both old Oughtrington school- boys and natives, of Lymm. Both joined the Territorials shortly before the outbreak of war. Lance-Corporal Kay served his apprenticeship with Mr. Burgess, farmer, but was working, at the time of his enlistment, for Mr. Henry Thomason.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>Private Harry Kay.</head>
        <p>Private Harry Kay.</p>
        <p>Private Harry Kay is 22 years of age, and was employed, previously to the outbreak of hostili- ties, at the Steel Works, Irlam. Mrs. Kay received the letter from Sergeant Clare yesterday morning. We quote from it : " I regret very much to inform y-ou that Fred and Harry are wounded. We were shelled heavily this morning. Harry is hit in his left arm, and Fred in the back and the left foot. Both, I am glad to say, are only slightly hurt and are doing well, and I feel sure will soon be quite all right." Mrs. Kay has a third son, Tom, at the Front.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>PRIVATE THOMASON.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE THOMASON.</p>
        <p>Private Thomason, who is marrled, and whose home is by Lymm Bridge, has been wounded by shrapnel. 'The news came through in a letter to Mrs. Shaw, of 4, Booth's Hill, from Sergeant Clare, who wrote:—</p>
        <p>"Will you please tell your daughter (Mrs. Thomason) that I am sorry to inform her that her husband. was wounded by shrapnel this morning? lie; was hit. in the back and left leg, very slightly," at 5.36. this morning (July 19th) - The remainder of us are all right, but I don't like to see our numbers growing less." Private Thomason is an old Volunteer, he having joined the movement 11 years ago. He is a native of Lyrnm and attended Oughtrington School. He was employed, at the time the war broke out, by Mr. Kingdon, butcher, of Heatley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>PRIVATE HADDON.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE HADDON.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Haddon, of Statham Crossing, had word on Thursday that her husband, Private Haddon, had been slightly wounded. When the war broke out, Private Haddon was engaged in the work of building the Co-opera tive Society's building at the Cross. He was born in Lymra and as a boy attended Ought- rington School. He is well-known locally as s goalkeeper, having guarded the goal for Lymm for some little while.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>"NONE LEFT IN LYMM."</head>
        <p>"NONE LEFT IN LYMM."</p>
        <p>Private Joseph ,Clare, brother of Thgent Clare, writing to his mother, says:—</p>
        <p>•. "The weather has not been so fine this last few days, bat it wouldn't do to be fine always, or else we should soon be crying out for water. . I should think there-will soon be none left in Lymm if • they keep on enlisting as they are doing."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>"SOON BE OVER."</head>
        <p>"SOON BE OVER."</p>
        <p>'Private Davies writes an interesting letter to his mother, Mrs. S. Davies. of Booth's Hill, say- ing that he thinks it will soon be over.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>REPORTED KILLED.</head>
        <p>REPORTED KILLED.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE FRED DANIELS.</p>
        <p>Comrade Says He Sam, Him Fall On</p>
        <p>The .Battlefield.</p>
        <p>-Private Fred Daniels, one of Lymm's many gallant fighters, is -reported (unofficially) to have -fallen. The news came through in a letter received by Mr. Starkey, lately of Brook Inn; Latchford. A comrade of Private Daniels's wrote to say that he saw him fall and lie quite still. Private Daniels's' home is at Newfields, Booth's Hill. His wife has four young chil- dren. Prior to his enlistment shortly after the outbreak of war, Private Daniels .was em- ployed at Long's Tannery, Grappenhall. Thirty-eight years of age, Private Daniels is one of the seven sons Of Mr. and Mrs. Samuel Daniels, of Clay Terrace, Booth's Hill.. He was born in Lymm and :attended the Parish School.</p>
        <p>A School Treat.—The children of the Higher Lane Baptist Sunday School had their annual treat on Wednesday in a field kindly lent by Mr. Cowner.,. Tea and the usual games constituted the features of a very happy day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>TRUNKS,</head>
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>SMALLPDX.</head>
        <p>SMALLPDX.</p>
        <p>Warrington Council Seek Loan For New Isolation Hospital.</p>
        <p>PECULIARLY SITUATED</p>
        <p>TOWN.</p>
        <p>Dr. W. W. E. Fletcher, a Local Government P.oard Inspector, held an inquiry at the Town ;t!-all on Wednesday .morning into the applica- non of the Town Council " for approval of the aPPropriation as a site for a smallpox hospital °F certain land belonging to them in the tow n- • Pof Winwick-with-Hulme, in the rural dis- riet of Warringten, situated about midway )etween Orford and Houghton Green, and ad- joining the north-eastern boundary of Orford Park"; also for sanction to the borrowing of 41,500 for the erection of the hospital. There were present at the inquiry the Mayor Alderman Peter Peacock), Alderman Bennett., Alderman James Evans (the chairman of the Ilealth Committee), Alderman John Evans, ;tr. A. T. 'Holloway (deputy•town clerk), Mr. A. • Ker (borough surveyor), Mr. M. A. Piercy (assistant borough surveyor), Mr. John Fair- hurst (borough treasurer.), Mr. James Hough- tOtl. (chief clerk in the borough treasurer's de- Partment), Dr. 13.. W. N. Joseph (medical officer of health. for the borough), Mr. W. T. Flood Chief sanitary inspector of the borough). Mr. I.;'urness (chairman of the 'Warrington Rural bistrict Council), Mr. J. C. Sutton (clerk to the Rural District Council), Mr. F. H. Kerfoot (Sanitary inspector to the Enrol District. Conn- • .and Mr. -Peter longton (a member of the IZural District Council).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>SITE AND THE HOSPITAL IT IS</head>
        <p>THE SITE AND THE HOSPITAL IT IS</p>
        <p>PROPOSED TO BUILD</p>
        <p>Isik• Rollaway, hi. opening ,the Councifs ap- Nivation, pointed out that the land on which it ' was proposed to erect the hospital belonged to the Corporation: The site to be utilised was 2.2 acres in extent and it was but a small part of the land, which was approximately 15,1, acres in extent. • The site fulfilled all the statutory l'equirements with regard to population, for in a radius Of a quarter of a mile there were only • live inhabited houses, with 27 inhabitants.' Within a radius of half a mile there were only . 67 inhabited houses, with 259 inhabitants. The site was on the north-east of the borough and ;vas a convenient - distance from the borough ooundary. The actual distance from Market ,Gate, which was the centre of the town and Lhe local' Piccadilly Circus, was 3:1 miles. The distance from the Lord Derby War Hospital was nearly three miles. The Corporation water . and gas were laid on close to the site, and to extend the supply. to the hospital would be an exceedingly simple matter. There could be no: question that every health authority which was doing its duty to the community- ought to pro- - vide the fullest means of protection against 4slnallpox and other infectious diseases, and that was the reason the application of the Corporation was being made. The Corporation smallpox -hospital was originally situated. at Sankey. Old Hospital Now A Consumption Sanatorium. -Vortunately there had bun no occasion. to Use it for the purpose of tretithig,smallpox, and live years ago the Corporation used it for the treatment of consumption. Since .that time. tile former smallpox hospital-had been approved as a sanatorium under the National 'lnsurance Act. It had, therefore, become necessary to Provide accommodation . elsewhere it was originally suggested that there should be six .. teen beds and that the building should be some, • thing like the one it was proposed to replace.- i Then there were negotiations with - the object . ' •of - carrying out a cheaper scheme and plans were provided accordingly, and the institution was to be built of brick. In all probability those plans Would have been proceeded with iibut fOr /the outbreak of the war. Then the . Corporation were, faced with two difficulties— ' , an enormous increase in the expenditure on the building, prices having jumped up approxi- mately 50 per cent., and the need of public bodies exercising all possible economy. •Of course, the Corporation, who were advised to '?conoinise, were pleased to do so, but would it . ana a, wise economy to postpone the building of 6111. aill'ax hospital?</p>
        <p>Great Convenience to the War Office.</p>
        <p>There pitais • llcre no less than five Bed Cross hes- bear' in Warrington and its. immediate aeigh- th urlioothe Tlond, as well as a large hospital known as accoL-401.'d Derby War Hospital, .and altogether ruodation had been provided for 3,100 4(.1 soldiers frtim the Front. It would he a treat convenience to the War Office to have accommodation close at hand for the treatment of smallpox patipats. There had no official request from the War Office, to Ptcvide if, but the Corporation. had been in- 'formally asked to 'provide the necessary accom- laudation for .the authoritiesat Winwick.• The •Vhairman of the Health Committee of the Varrington Corporation tAlderman .James "n 5 was a prominent member of the com- mittee which administered the war hospital at Winwick, and he (Mr. ,Hallaway) therefore 81)0ke with some authority when he _referred. to , .the need of a smallpox hospital. There was a desire on the part of the military, authorities that the corporation should co-operate with Ahem in their work at Winwick. The block of . buildings which it was proposed to erect would r consist of a pavilion with accommodation for ,twelve Patients, an outside block, a laundry, a washhouse, a mortuary, and an administra- tive block, and the whole would be so arranged that there would be no interference with pro- Aiding further extensions if necessary. It .would be constructed of wood-lined asbestos, . • And that was a form of construction which the Borough Surveyor could thoroughly recommend. The building would be completed within 18 Inonths, and the object the Corporation had in view was to be ready for any sudden outbreak - 'of smallpox. The sum asked for was £1,500, and the life of the building Nytts put at twenty 3,larg. It was thought, however, that it would inn false economy to ask for a twenty years' I°an, and a period of ten years had been de- cided upon, subject to. the sanction of the Local Government Board. There was no opposition to the scheme on this Occasion, and that was source of satisfaction to the Health Com- lathes and the Corporation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>REASONS WHY THE HOSPITAL IS URGENTLY NEEDED.</head>
        <p>REASONS WHY THE HOSPITAL IS URGENTLY NEEDED.</p>
        <p>The Medical Officer of Health, in giving evi, 4enee, said he would like to impress upon the I,"Pector the Urgency of the immediate proyi- "oil of the hospital accommodation for which the loan was sought. Obviously all towns °Eould,have some Provision for at once dealing ith an outbreak of smallpox, and he ventured le advance six special reasons why Warringtofl. should have such facilities and accommodation .without undue delay. , They were:— (1) The peculiar situation of the town: It Was• 4:ni all the main roads in this part of the t°Eu!try, and, therefore, the number of tramps 7ntinually passing through was, very high. ere were no fewer than 33 coMmon lodging- iouses in the town, a muuber which, he thought, was higher than was.to be found in , 11,13' other town of the 'size in .Lancashire. eg4in, the position of the town on the Ship -anal had tel.* taken into account. , (2&gt; The town had had no epidemic of small- for ten years; the last epidemic .of this ;I,',6ellse being in 1901, when thero_ were 47 eases. long 'immunity rendered an outbreak all le .more likely. Coupled with the low, period of immunity illere was the fact that the number of exemp- tons from vaccination was rising in an alarm- hug, 'Manner; indeed the number rose by almost 1k undred each year. They had risen from 38 1— 1907 to 1,774 in 1914, as the following table ,weuld show :-.--1907, 38; 1908, 99; 1909, 169; 1910, 1210; 1911,, 953; 1912, 251; 1913, 347; 1914, 407; liking a total of 1,774. This meant that .11 rge unvaccinated and therefore unprotected I)(3oUlatiOn was growing up in the tow!!. • c 4) • There was frequently. infection in the 4;elghbourhood of the town, and although so aim there had been no cases in the town this ulmer, there had been • outbreaks quite re- ;72-I,tlY in towns as near as Salford and Oldham, "El, last week, at Royton. The Warrington Rural District Council 4(1 no accommodation .for eases, and would, Probabl c" 3, look to the •Corporation if a cape„ ~urred in their area, as they had looked in Elie past. tis 6) .There were a number of 'unitary hospi- jilt., in the immediate neighbourhood, includ- °f tla specially large one at Winwick. None glith"3" places was prepared to deal with an r-eak of billallVOKl but would rely upon</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>Furness, Corporal P. Hare, Rev. Horace Gray (headmaster), Captain J. W. Pearson,</head>
        <p>Furness, Corporal P. Hare, Rev. Horace Gray (headmaster), Captain J. W. Pearson,</p>
        <p>Many who have seen a well-equipped corn. pally - of boys of military bearing marching through the streets of Warrington, have been much exercised in their minds as to the reason for the production of so youthful a body of soldiers. The explanation is quite simple, and 'in every respect satisfactory. Although the boys are attired in the correct uniform of the South Lancashire_ Itexjment they are not, as yet, of the line. They are the Grammar . School Cadet Corps, so thoroughly in earnest that they have qualified for Government recognition and have been affiliated witly,the 4th South_ Lancashire Regi- ment—our local Territorials who, officered</p>
        <p>largely by Grammar School Old Boys, have already made history for themselves in the present war, following upon the good work they did in South Africa fourteen or fifteen years ago.</p>
        <p>The -Grammar School Cadet Corps was formed immediately upon the outbreak of the war agaihst Germany last August, and it was " Gazetted " in February. In its initial stages the Corps was trained by Sergeant- Major Newall, of the 4th South Lancs., and since then Captain Pearson has taken full charge of the boys, who have made great strides towards efficiency. The primary object of the movement is, of course, to train</p>
        <p>the Corporation. Moreover, large numbers of soldiers were billeted in the town: ,</p>
        <p>In answer to the Inspector th, Medical Officer said he thought the site was pi.rfectly suitable for the purposes for which it w as intended to utilise it.</p>
        <p>, Fourth Row :—J. C. Liston, IL E. Burdett, Lance-Corporal T. R. Atherton, Corporal W. V Corporal C. G. Tyers, Corporal F. J. Sobee, Lame-Corporal R. Smith, A. V. Shadbolt. Front Row :—E. Davenport, F. Jenks, J. E. Snietluirst, J. G. Stephens, A. Stewart, P. i Tennant„ k. E. Dowling; G. Maddock,</p>
        <p>the Solis of the middle classes in military matters, so that Warrington may take- its share in filling the ranks of the officers in the Army, upon whom the war is levying a heavy toll. Already the Corps has provided one officer, for Ferguson major, who was .a Cor- poral in the Cadets, has got a commission in. the 10th Battalion of the South Lancashire Regiment, and has left his young colleagues for the sterner work of camp life. Before Ferguson major was -given his com- mission the Corps numbered 71, including twe officers. It ,now numbers 70, including •.two officers—ClTtain J. W. Pearson, and Lieutenant J. Pape, masters at the school.</p>
        <p>Burthem.</p>
        <p>These gentlemen are well supported by an enthusiastic' body of non-commissioned 'officers, consisting of Sergeant Witts, Cor- porals" Sobee, Tyers, Hare and Furness, and Lance-Corporals Atherton and Smith. Drill, which takes place regularly every Tuesday afternoon and Saturday morniu, either in the school grounds or in Victoria, Park, lent by the Corporation for the purpose, is entered into with determinatim and whole- heartedness that has, from the first, foretold success. The boys look really smart in their South Lams. uniform, towards which they have individually contributed 305., a head, the balance being' foUnd by tha governors of the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>MILITARY' HOSPITALS WOULD LOOK TO</head>
        <p>MILITARY' HOSPITALS WOULD LOOK TO</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Major Elwin Harold Thomas Nash, of the R.A.M.C., called as a witness by Mr. Rollaway; said lie was the chief physician and was in charge of the isolation ward at the Winwick War Hospital, At present ,the authorities of that hospital were faced by the anxiety that they had no means of isolating smallpox caSesi should such occur. An outbreak of that scourge would be a very serious matter indeed. It was not the practice in military hospitals to isolate cases of smallpox either iii the ordin- ary hospital buildings or anywhere near them; they wanted them taken right aWay. In the event of smallpox breaking out in any of the military hospitals in the neighbourhood the. Army authorities would certainly look to the Warrington Corporation to provide facilities for their isolation and treatment.</p>
        <p>RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL ASK FOR- AND GET-AN ASSURANCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Furnes'B said the Warrington -Rural District Council, .of which lie is chairman, did not offer any opposition to the Corporation's scheme, but they would like an assurance from the Borough Surveyor with regard to the sew- age from the hospital. In the past the Cor- poration had been extremely accommodating to the Rural District Council in, the matter of the treatment of smallpox .cases from the rural area, and he took it that the *same assistance as .had been given in .the past would be held out in the future, _ . _</p>
        <p>The Borough Surveyor replied that he did not anticipate any . difficulty in dealing safely and satisfactorily • with the sewage from the hospital. The Mayor anti. Alderman 'fames Evans hav- ing supported the Council's application, the inquiry was closed, mid the Inspector went to visit the site.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>WHAT OUR READERS AN I\l ED.</head>
        <p>WHAT OUR READERS AN I\l ED.</p>
        <p>What our ~readers IN-anted was proof that cures were lasting. And this is exactly What is given here, in the word of one of our neigh- bours in Warrington; _ _</p>
        <p>On January 6th, 1913, Mr.' J. Lakin, of 16, llolland-street, Warrington, said :--" I work in a hot place, and coming out into the cold air was the cause of my kidney trouble. I was at- tacked, by acute pains In the region of the kidneys, which seemed to drag me down. As my work is a strain on the back these pains made it difficult for me. I was a bit Mazy and in the mornings felt weary and tired. " The water was in an unnatural state, and I knew from this my kidneys were affected. "I tried Doan's Backache Kidney Pills for this complaint, and proved them entirely satis- factory. After taking the pills the water became clear again and my back was free from pain. Since then I have usually kept a box in the house and use them if I feel any signs of the old trouble._ (Signed) J. Lakin."</p>
        <p>1561 On Marc , 1915—over two years later— Mr: Lakin said :—" I have kept well since I used Doan's Pills. They cured me splendidly." If you have any such clear signs of kidney and bladder' disbrders as backache, urinary troubles, gravel, 1 dropsy, lumbago or rheuma- tism, persevere with Bonn's Backache Kidney Pills until, every trace of the dread kidney disease is gone. Doan's Pills assist the urinary system like 'a laxative assists the bowels. Price 2/9 a box, 6 boxes 13/9; of all dealers, or from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford-street, London, W. Don't ask for back- ache or kidney pills—ask distinctly for Doan's Backache Kidney Pills,- the same as Ur. Lakin had.</p>
        <p>A stitch in time saves nine cuss words. lie laughs best who laughs pot too loud.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>DEAD HEROES REST CONTENT.</head>
        <p>DEAD HEROES REST CONTENT.</p>
        <p>Bishop Of Liverpool Consecrates War Cemetery At</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>Warrington,</head>
        <p>Warrington,</p>
        <p>"HAD IT NOT BEEN FOR THEM</p>
        <p>The Lord Bishop of the Diocese (Dr'. Chavasse) visited the Warrington Cemetery on Saturday to consecrate the Church of England portion of ground which the Corporation has decided to set apart for the burial of, those soldiers and sailors who die in the war hospitals of the neighbourhood. This portion is 418 square yards in extent and is Situate on the</p>
        <p>left-hand side of the main walk, the first avail- able plot upon entrance to the Cemetery. The weather was fortunately fine and there was a large and reverent attendance of the general public, who entered into the unique service and singing most heartily. His Lordship was met at the Cemetery Lodge by the Rector (Canon Willis), and the. Revs. F. W. E. Chadwick (chaplain to the Forces, Lord Derby War Hospital), A. M. Mitchell (Burton- wood), B. Percival •(St. Peter's), T. Vickers and S. G. Stanton • (Parish Church), E. L. J. Roberts and T. P. Gwen (Holy Trinity), Mr. R. Farmer .(registrar of the diocese), Col. MOggridge (com- manding the depot), Colonel Simpson,. R.A.M.C. (Lord Derby's Mar Hospital), Colonel Mocatta</p>
        <p>(recruiting officer), Alderman Bennett, and Councillors Cunnington and Shaw, Mr. liana- way (deputy town clerk), Messri4:_George Knott (Parish Church) and J. Hesketh (Trinity), and others.</p>
        <p>Preceded' by Mr. IL Young (the registrar of the Cemetery), who had made all the arrange- ments with the utmost care, 4nd .the combined choirs of the Parish Church and its two mission churches, under Mr. Darby, the Bishop and gentlemen named walked round the ground singing Psalm xe.: "Lord', thou hastkeen our refuge from one generation to another." , Standing in the centre of the ground to be consecrated,. the Bishop called upon the Fegis- trar of the Diocese to read the "sentence ofi consecration"; and Mr. _Farmer read a long dOcument recording the legal history of the Cemetery, and the fact that the, Corporation of Warrington had decided to set aside 418 square yards for the burial of .sailors and soldiers of His Majesty's Navy and Army who shall die in or 'near Warrington (luring, or consequent •upon, the present war. The ground wag to be</p>
        <p>set apart from all' common and profane uses, consecrated for the purposes of Christian burial, and from this time was holy ground. : ,</p>
        <p>_ The Bishop having announced that the ground had been consecrated; the hymn "0 God, our help in ages past" was sung. Then followed special prayers by the Bishop and the hymn "Brief life is here our portion," and the concluding portion of the service, which ended with the singing of the National Anthem. The singing was led by the choir and the rand of the 2nd South Lancashire Regiment, under Bandmaster R. E. Cahill:</p>
        <p>'Superintendent Williamson had charge of the police arrangements. THE MEANING OF CONSECRATION.</p>
        <p>The Bishop, before the closing passages in the Service, said that before they separated he would like to explain briefly the. meaning of consecration and of the seivice in which they had been engaged. When We consecrated a burial ground we did not infuse any virtue into the land. The land was the same after con- secration as it was before. By consecration we simply set it apart from all profane and com- mon uses for the burial of our dead, just RS when we consecrated a church we did not infuse any virtue into the stones or bricks, but set the building apart from all profane and com- mon uses to be used as a .House of Prayer. We should be shocked, and rightly shocked, if a consecrated church were used, for a dance or a theatre or concert, when it was set apart for the. worship o God. That piece, of land had been thus set aside for the burial of those brave men who would die of wounds or sickness con- tracted in this awful war. They would keep it ,with the greatest care. No children would be allowed to play there, no building would be erected, no sheep would graze upon the land—</p>
        <p>it was holy. It had been set apart for the burial of His saints., That was the meaning of consecration : the setting apart from profane or common uses of a piece of land or building.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <p>on oft.</p>
        <p>Proprietors</p>
        <p>EXAMINE</p>
        <p>OFFICER (severely) : "Is this rifle supposed to have been cleaned?"</p>
        <p>PRIVA3E ;</p>
        <p>ft Well, 64—yes.</p>
