      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <p>74 Nursery for Future Officers of the British Army.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>LEARNING TO DRILL AND TO LEAD.</head>
        <p>LEARNING TO DRILL AND TO LEAD.</p>
        <p>Pliotographbs Parkinson</p>
        <p>6001) MUSTER.</p>
        <p>Top Row :—W• S. Dunlop, D. Allen, E. B. Allen, E. W. Gorton, D. Murray, C. Whitehouse, B. L. woiff_llyiands, 11. Wottliington, J. Shaw, H. B. Silk, J. It• Locker, C. W. Bennie, F. Earle. Second Pitchford, B. N. Linaker, S. IL Percival, G. V. Owen„ N. G. Ormiston, J. P. Loilgon, F. K. Ewart, W. W. Randall, A. W. Yule, IL, G. Teare, 11. Knott, J. W. Morton, D. B. Weaver, I W. IL Payton, G. L. Brown, R. D. Tennant, H. C. Taylor, H. R. hope, A. F. Sterling. . fluid Row :—J. Kay, S. J. G. Goinall LII utuer, G. y. Howard, A Okes-VoYseY, J Wilcock G. F. Atherton, H.-A. Dodd, J. Lee, F. R. Ferguson, S. Smith, W. 11. Hughes, S. W. Fleming.</p>
        <p>Wilkinson,</p>
        <p>Howard, 11. 31. Nicholls, E. IL Dakin,</p>
        <p>Lieut. J. Pape, Sergeant L. J. Witt,</p>
        <p>school, The authorities have not, as yet, aspired to a band, but there is ono drum that strives worthily to complete the real martial air connected with the .movement. At a miniature rifle range alongside the school the boys learn to shoot at 25 yards, and there is a signalling class that is making satisfactory progress. Already two field days figure in the records of the Corps—one at Hatton and the other at Appleton. From what we know of Warrington Gram- mar School boys we are led to expect to hear something in the days to come of the mem- bers of this Cadet Corps.</p>
