      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>THREE-FOLD SERVICE.</head>
        <p>A THREE-FOLD SERVICE.</p>
        <p>The service in which they had taken part was of a threefold character. It was a symbolic act, a legal act, and att act of prayer. First at all 'came the symbolic act. They bad moved slowly round the ground singing one of the Psalms of David. Thousands of years ago, before writing was invented, when a piece In land was transferred from one man to another, the transference took place 43y his walking across or round it. When the people of Israei marched round Jericho they claimed Jericho by the act for God. So.that day, when a pro- cession of choristers, officials, officers and others moved round that piece of land, ' singing a Psalm; they claimed it for God. Then- came the legal' act, the Registrar of the Diocese reading the deed of consecration and so placing the lanu under the protection of the law. No one cello henceforward use this land for any other pur- pose unless an Act of Parliament- were passeu authorising it. Finally, they praed God to accept mid bless the land .for the purpose for which it was intended—the burial of his ser- vants. They also prayed for those who woula bring their dead there that God would comfort them in their'. sorrow, would give them hope in their loneliness, and enable them to thank Him for their dear ones who were resting in Christ; . and - they •prayed for themselves "whether we live or die."</p>
        <p>To this piece of land belonged this peculiar honour: it was to be the burial place of some of our British heroes, some of those brave men who, in answering their country's call, hao risked life and limb to save her in her need, and who had fallen in doing so.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>IMPRESSIVE SCENE AT THE FRONT.</head>
        <p>IMPRESSIVE SCENE AT THE FRONT.</p>
        <p>I know full well, Lid the Bishop, bow our soldiers, brave men as they are, desire to have Christian burial. • Let me give you an instance. One whom I love very dearly is a Chaplain at the Front. Not very, long ago he came to a battery of guns and the first thing the men asked him to do was to read the-burial service over a sergeant whom they had buried a few days before without a service, no clergyman being near at hand. Of course that young clergy- than was only too glad to be able to read the service, but it was broad daylight. They were in full view of the German guns, and if they had held the service then they would have been shelled, and they put the service off till it grew dusk in the evening; and then, he told me, the men of the battery 'gathered round this little grave, which was beautifully kept just under the shadow of n huge embankment. There the burial service we know so well was read, ano when it was over the young clergyman spoke a few words of cheer to the soldiers and they sang a hymn together, the hymn that you often sing its church:</p>
        <p>"Abide with me, Fast falls the eventide."</p>
        <p>The young Chaplain, looking up, saw a number of shadowy forms passing along the slope bt the embankment, and as they came near and could hear the hymn these men, he says, took it up and. began to sing it, softly. They were now on 'their way to the trenches, where they were to be all night, some of them going to certain death. It was, he says, one of the most impressive scenes of his life, and that one oi the most impressive sights was to look up in the dusk and see this moving force of men pass- ing along singing as they went that beautiful hymn. "Abide with me" until their voices died away in the distance, and their forms were lost in the darkness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>GLOWING TRIBUTE.</head>
        <p>A GLOWING TRIBUTE.</p>
        <p>That shows you how our brave soldiers value Christion burial; and the officials in 'Warrington have .done a wise and a kindly act iii separating this beautiful portion of their cemetery for the high and holy work of TeeeiVing the bodies, of our brave men. For the day will come when this war shall have passed away, when you will bring your children and your children's children to this place anu</p>
        <p>say : "here rest the brave men who died foi England. Here rest men who left homes, wives children, parents and those whom they loveu best to face hardships and wounds and death itself that we might be safe. Had it not been for them we would have been a vassal province of the German Empire:" _ _ .</p>
        <p>That is what you will say; and I think if the dead whom you will bury here could speak, they would say what is written on a monument raised to the brave men who fell on Wagon 11111, outside Ladysmith.. On the column that marks the last resting-place of those heroes are written these words:</p>
        <p>Go thou, passer-by, and tell England that we who died here in her behalf</p>
        <p>rest content. Yes; the hero rests content, for he has given his life for his country; and he has followed very closely in the footsteps of our Lord Jesus Christ, who said : Greater love bath no man than this: that a man lay down his life for his friend.</p>
        <p>With all my heart I thank your Burial Board for this inspired thought—for such it is—of having had this beautiful piece of your War- rington Cemetery set apart for so noble a purpose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>SEEN FROM AFAR.</head>
        <p>SEEN FROM AFAR.</p>
        <p>Anglo-Americans &amp; Dissension And Slacking At Home.</p>
        <p>GERMAN PIN-PRICKING.</p>
        <p>BY H, WHITHAM.</p>
        <p>Yes, and with pins that are both sharp, and heavy.. The situation is this. Out here in California, where I am placed, it so happens that an opportunity is afforded to those of British and Irish blood, and of several other nationalities, to meet the Teuton in argument. This latter fellow is forceful in debate. He is something more than this; he has pre-empted the barbarous idea that "necessity knows no law," and he certainly acts down to its full limits. From murder to lies, or from lies to Murder the idea that he has taken full posses- sion of he works for all that it is worth. His quotations are always correct, and his state- ments are always truthful. Does he not carry around "Fatherland," and "The issue," and are not these journals full of quotations taken from the leading London papers? What's the difference to .the man in the 'street when I, or any of my friends, point out the truth that these journals are owned by a man named Northcliffe; a correction comes from the Tue- ton quick—" Lord Northcliffe." There you have it, Lord Northcliffe, a man of influen ye, and one who tells the world that the British Government is made up of incapables. We have descriptions given of another, and Possibly a worse state of things—we are a lot of lazy, drunken skulkers. A paper containing these statements, was a few days ago, shoved into my face, and I was pressed to read it aloud. Things happened. The Allies had a majority, and the happenings were not serious. These things couldn't happen, and couldn't be made in Germany:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>Enemy Of Our Own Household.</head>
        <p>Enemy Of Our Own Household.</p>
        <p>Among the British-born you that are at home are having a deal the worst of it, but believe me those who have found their hoihes in foreign lands are not without their anxieties aii to the results of this fight for the right. What they fear and dislike most is the enemy of their own household.</p>
        <p>I have just read a speech that was made in the House of Lords by one fresh from the front and, Mr. Editor, I wish you to reprint it:— "Lord Stanhope said he had come back from the front the previous day, and was returning the next day. What they at the front waited to know was when they were going to get a backing at home. He knew of a brigade which has spent 32 consecutive days in the trenches, and the whole of that time they were liable to being shelled, and for a great deal of the time they were shelled. They might judge from that what was the nervous strain on these men. He did not think he was giving' away any secret when he said that the French, speaking broadly, held their trenches by few rifles, sup- ported by that wonderful 75mm. gun. We, on the other hand, held our trenches mainly by rifle fire. The first entailed a great expendi- ture of ammunition and the second an expendi- ture of life.</p>
        <p>Was it going to be said that every single slack day spent by manufacturers or workmen, every day wasted in the factories, meant more lives lost at the front, and that every man whtir wasted his time and did not take the fullest advantage of his employment was responsible for those lives? It appeared to him that the man who refused to work ought to be made to fight. The man who refused to do his duty in the workshop should be sent to the front, whether he liked it or not."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>LITTLE BIT OF POETRY FROM THE FRONT.</head>
        <p>A LITTLE BIT OF POETRY FROM THE FRONT.</p>
        <p>When as a lad my thoughts would roam, To fortunes, made in a land unknown, . A soldier's life appealed to me, It seemed so open and so free. And so •my thoughts of wealth untold Were driven away by thoughts more bold; And, wishing all "Good-bye" at home, I went to serve my King alone.</p>
        <p>Five years I spent my time in training, A faithful servant still remaining; My time expired with the colours, Back home I went, like many others. I got good work and got good pay, And then got wed one summer's day; Yes, as you read, you will be taught, Our married life was sweet and short.</p>
        <p>A few brief months in my new home, My soldiering thoughts were left alone, To build a home for my young wife. But I was doomed to disappointment— The Germans had caused some great ,resentment. Poor Belgium they had crushed and maimed. But Britain's honour still remained: Her sons for France were quickly sent, And I, like thousands, gladly went.</p>
        <p>A bell was rung, the platform cleared; The soldier lads were gladly cheered. My wife turned no hesitation, Yet who could tell our destination? She looked on calm, she spoke a prayer "God in Heaven, he's in your care." The whistle blew, the train was gone, So now, "0 God! Thy will be done!"</p>
        <p>For months I've worked and gladly done 111 battles great, so nobly won. Back from the Marne the Germans went, Then by the Aisne, their fury spent: In these big. battles I was spared, A welcome rest my nerves repaired, Now on to Belgium, crushed and weak, Fresh fields of victory we went to seek.</p>
        <p>The foe from Belgium must be driren, Then surely Peace, it will be given; And so in fight, by day and night, To bring about this future bright. Perhaps, in time, my turn will come— Eternal life, my Duty done. If that is so, just hear thy prayer, For those at home, left in God's care— " l'lease turn to them hi hours of need, Don't wait to hear them beg and plead; RemeMber! We have given old- all, In answer to Old England's call." "GOD SAVE THE KING."</p>
        <p>J. MYERS. Private James Bryers, who is in the,I?..A.M.C., with No. 4 Clearing Hospital "in the Field," is the son of Mr. James Bryers, Bowes-street, Warrington. He has many relatives fighting and in training.</p>
        <p>The Canny Wooer. It was fair time in Selkirk, and Sandy and his sweetheart were wandering rouad arm in arm enjoying the sights. Presently they espied a smart-looking pie shop, which they promptly entered. Sandy ordered one pie, sat down and commenced to eat it. Meanwhile the looked shyly on. " Is't fine, Sandy?" she timidly asked. "Ay, 'tie Awful fine, Jeanie !," he answered. 6hould bUy one :"</p>
      </div>
