      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>Local Vicar and Tea.</head>
        <p>A Local Vicar and Tea.</p>
        <p>Of course I am aware that the " tea fight" —to quote last week's daring if not irrever- ent headline—has long been a .cherished Church institution; all the same I confess that I was startled on reading the Rev. A. M. Mitchell's admission that he is a tea- drinker. I had somehow imbibed (yes, that's the word) the notion that so earnest a food reformer. would at .least have shared the objections to " the cups that cheer but not inebriate" notoriously held by most vegetarians. However, at the last meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians, we had the fearless Vicar of Burtonwood at the outset of a speech on the subject proclaim- ing himself a tea-drinker. So I learn from " Thq Examiner" account of the proceed- ings; whereat I rejoice and am exceeding glad, for, as I have intimated, I had feared that in this Mr. Mitchell was not in accord with his brethren and with ecclesiastical tradition. Oceans Of It.</p>
        <p>Certainly, when Mr. Mitchell begins a dis- cussion with the self-illuMinating phrase "As a tea-drinker," he is to be understood as meaning that he indulges moderately and n!Lit excessively in the mild stimulant in question —in contrast, for instance, with Dr. John- son, whose habits in this respect are known and read of all men. Mr._ Mitchell knows his Boswell, and will remember how that veracious chronicler wrote of the master he adored and served so faithfully that "the quantities which he drank of it at lI hours were so great that his nerves must have been uncommonly Wong not to have been ex- tremely relaxed by such an intemperate use of it. He assured me that he never felt the least inconvenience from it, which is a proof that the fault of his constitution was rather a too great tension of fibres than the con- trary." Johnson himself, replying to Hanway's "Essay on Tea," declared.himself to be " a hardened aind shameless tea- drinker, who has for 20 years diluted his meals with only the infusion of this fascin- ating plant, whose kettle has scarcely time to cool; who with tea amuses the evening; with tea solaces the midnight, and with tea welcomes the morning." I have known many factory operatives in Lancashire who had tea four or five times a day—and dyspepsia or some other ailment in consequence.</p>
        <p>Methodism. The reference in last week's Church news to the Wesleyan and United Methodist Con- ferences set me thinking afresh of the character and achievements of the great man whose name is now borne by organised millions throughout the world. I turn to the latest " History of Methodism," to which many distinguished ministers contribute chapters dealing with the various periods of that history and with different phases of Methodist life and development, and I am particularly struck with the contrast between the immortal Wesley and his famous con- temporary, Whitefield. While the latter moved the passing crowds with his pheno- menal Oratory, the former has his imperish- able monument in the greatest evangelical group of churches in the world. I have lately mentioned in this column the proud boast of prominent adherents that, in chang- ihg the face of the nation at a critical period, John Wesley saved the country from a rePetition of the horrors of the French revo- lution. One of seven volumes of a History of England published not long ago is on " England Under the Hanoverians," and the Oxford tutor in history who wrote it has a fine chapter containing a notable apprecia- tion of the founder of Methodism. This system, as pointed out by a still more eminent critic—let it be repeated—diverted into religious channels a vast volume of social discontent which in France swelled the tides that submerged Church and State in 1789.</p>
        <p>An Early Steam Carriage. As I have read "The Hub's" weekly column on cycling and motoring I have often felt tempted, not by any means to enter the lists against him, but to invite the attention of interested readers to certain considera- tions with which lie is not called upon to deal. II must be between 50 and 60 years ago since the Earl of Caithness successfully experi- mented with a steam tricycle carrying three passengers. I call it a tricycle because, like a familiar typo of child's perambulator, it was mounted on three wheels, the front one being fitted on a pivot so as to be operated by means of a steering handle. The boiler (fixed, with the engine, behind, with a seat for the stoker) had to be 'replenished hourly, or after a run of ten miles or set, the water con- sumption averaging about a gallon per minute. Engine and boiler occupied only sft. of space; the expense of running was a fraction over id. per mile and the cost of the carriage was some 22d0, so that it was not a very extravagant luxury. Much Older Forms.</p>
        <p>As a matter of fact, ambitious inventors were exercising their ingenuity on the like problem generations, before the enterprising Earl of Caithness astonished the natives by travelling along the highway without horses. So long ago as the middle of the 18th century there_ was a good-Size chaise propelled by the feet. In 1802 a practical !item carriage was put on the road, and some years later was run in the form of a stage coach. There were other similar inventions, but they were strongly opposed by interested or prejudiced sections of the public, and rendered un- profitable by the obstructions of highway authorities and Parliament itself. These continued down to within little more than a score years ago, so that even a steam tricycle patented so recently as 1881 was restricted in speed to four miles an hour, must have two man in charge and one walking ahead bearing a danger flag! Naturally, therefore, France took the lead in thesd things. 1897 wit- nessed the introduction of the motor cab to London, and my younger readers know of the amazing progress and development mean- while achieved. Fiction.</p>
        <p>I was much interested in what Miss Fleming had to say at last week's meeting of the S.W.A. On the subject, as a whole, in view of the authoritative and generally accepted assertion that 80 out of every 100 books issued from the Free Libraries belong to the different departments of fiction, let me point out that, even if none of these are to be deprecated as positively harmful, lots of them while free from immorality, are vapid anCI frivolous. There are millions of books not worth reading at all, and to spend one's leisure on these (constituting, I do not hesitate fo affirm the great bulk of modern fiction) is shamefully to waste one's oppor- tunities of self-improvement. Incorrigible.</p>
        <p>As (write thus I do not forget that the late Lord Goschen had an insatiable and in- curable weakness for such stuff as Gaboriau's police romances; that most of our Bishops are great novel readers, and that certain of them gre credited with a liking for some of the naughty French order; that the late Mr. Chamberlain did not, and Mr. Balfour, Mr. Birrell, Mr. George Wyndham, Lord Rose- bery and other distinguishd intellectuals do not disdain what some of us ordinary mortals have the impertinence to denounce as trash; and that not a few, such as the late Walter Headlam of undou*d eminence as a scholar, actually loved the shilling shocker. All the same, none of these things move me.</p>
        <p>How to Read. I trust it is hardly necessary to explain in so many words that I do not at all share the diehard prejudice that has passed down</p>
        <p>Continued in next column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <head>TEACHING THE TURKS:</head>
        <p>TEACHING THE TURKS:</p>
        <p>Convincing Lessons Our Boys Are Imparting.</p>
        <p>CORPL. KAY AND CHANGED TIMES.</p>
        <p>Corporal Frank Kay, a former member of our reportorial staff, who is now serving in the vicinity of the Dardanelles, as a member of the Army Service Corps, has written an ex- tremely interesting letter to an old colleague, from which we extract the following : Things are very different "here" (somewhere near the scene of operations) from what they were in old Warrington, and 1 pretume things are very different there from what they were a few months ago. Last year at this, time we were all working so comfort- ably at home, secure and safe, we thought, from /all tile horrors of war, whereas now in June, 1915, we are in the midst of one of the most unholy and Titanic struggles the world has ever known. Yes, just about this time last year " knuts" and " knutesses" were abroad in all their glory, varieties in colour and style of dress their only worry. nut now, what do we find? Underneath that which we *thought was only a shell there was a kernel. We used tp see so very little of the kernel that we had doubts as to whether it really existed. The shells were cracked in August of last year and the quantity and quality of the kernels have surprised everyone, not least our German "friends" across the water; To-day we find the " knuts" even prouder than ever of the new creation they wear—the khaki uniform of the King, and the " knutesses" are far prouder of the " knut " in hobnailed boots and puttees than .they were of him last summer in his patent leather shoes and dazzling multi- coloured socks.</p>
        <p>Strang, Unshakable Unity in the Ranks.</p>
        <p>The manhood of the country—that of War- rington by no means least—has responded to the clarion call, and the gentler sex are seeing to it that the boys in khaki are well looked after in the trenches. Yes, those self-same young men whom some may have thought had no backbone in them at all have undergone, and are still undergoing, hardships and trials of the most severe and painful order, and— doink it with a heartiness and cheerfulness which does one good to see! Men of all classes and ranks are working and pulling together in a fashion which would have seemed well-nigh impossible twelve months ago, and, incidentally I might mention here, that to us "out here" it seems strange that there should be anything bordering on disunion at home when we are enjoying and feeling the great , benefits of strong, unshakable unity in the presence of the enemy. There is purpose written on the sun-tanned face of every " Tommy " I have met, and "Mr. Turk," straining on the leash held by the German officers, has had a good chance of learning what that set look means, and—he has learned to his cost,. Turks Have Had It Hot.</p>
        <p>Our men have done splendidly and one is glad to be able to say that during the past few days their efforts have been rewarded— they have done that wllich Britons can always do against an unrighteous foe—advanced! For two days and nights now the Turks have hati it exceptionally hot, and, as I write, the firing has by no means slackened down. I saw one of our warships at work quite close to where we were. Guarded against submarines by several destroyers, the big ship went as close in shore as possible, and then her big guns spoke, and with no uncertain voice. The noise was terrific, and even the trawler which I and another member of the unit were on, vibrated as each of the two broadsides was delivered. For a few minutes we could not see any signs of the damage done, but soon a dense column of smoke rose up from the peninsula, and it was plainly evident that good work had been accomplished. Anyhow, it woke the Turks up, for whereas they had been very quiet prior to the visit of the warship, they commenced now to fire at where the ship had been, and then, when they discovered that the intruder had vanished, they peppered the batteries on land. That is a game for two, and our gun- ners are no Mere tyros—that also the Turks know! We are teaching them a few things, eh?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <head>" Annies From Asia."</head>
        <p>" Annies From Asia."</p>
        <p>The boys in France speak of the "Jack Jehnsons," but here the big shells are called " Annies from Asia," and they don't half sing through the air. The Turks cannot hold out much longer now, and the general feeling is that Constantinople is not so far off now as was thought a few weeks ago. Yesterday was a very good day for our troops, who made an appreciable advanc; in an important direction. The other day I ws very much impressed by two simple graves which I saw. Two com- panions had been killed in action, and they had been buried on the edge of a cliff, side by side. All that marked their resting-places were two simple crosses made out of biscuit- box wood, with their names on them and the pathetically symbolical letters R.I.P. "But little he'll reek if they let him sleep on, In a grave where a Briton has laid him." Such sights and the thought that there are thousands of our heroes sleeping the long sleep with no mark over their graves makes one realise that war is not as some people seem to think, all trench-banter and humour, the sing- ing of "Tipperary," and other popular ditties, but is a hideous, diabolical creation for making innocent people suffer. I saw a barge full of sun helmets the other day, and upon inquiry what was the matter with them I was told they had belonged to men of various regiments who had died the hero's death.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>Talk-Much and Do-Nothings.</head>
        <p>The Talk-Much and Do-Nothings.</p>
        <p>There is not a single man here who will not be thankful when all is over, but we want "all " to be over first—and " all " means such a lot. Men are needed' and men must come. It makes one sick to think that here are 'men giving and .sacrificing all willingly for :a noble cause when there are some at borne, quite eligible and free, arguing lightly on this great crisis and saying which way things should be done, and doing nothing to help in this time of national stress. lam keeping quite well in health and hope all at home are the same. I quite enjoy "The Examiner" every week and hope to get it as regularly as I have done all along.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>TUCK'S WAR PUBLICATIONS.</head>
        <p>TUCK'S WAR PUBLICATIONS.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Raphael Tuck and Sons, Ltd., are employing their well-known talent and endless artistic resources upon the production of post- cards and pictures of a military, naval and patriotic type. Their, recent series of postcards has been followed by photogravures portraits of the Royal Family. The issue is historic. -These portraits depict their Majesties the King and Queen, the Prince of Wales, Prince Albert and 'Princess Mary at a period when the greatest war ever waged by the nation is at its height. Produced at the remarkably low price of one shilling per copy, it is hoped that they will find their ,way into every British home, school, club and public institution.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.— Continued. %</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.— Continued. %</p>
        <p>through generation after generation, from the early part of the last century, when the Uovel was regarded as a dreadful companion for the young, and religious folk refused to admit the class of literature then current to their homes. Pure fiction has its legitimate mission. My protest is against abuse, and my purpose is to impress the young with the importance of giving the modern novel of the three-volume order a good long rest. Read- ing is really intended to furnish food for thinking. Of the books in existence (they are being published at the rate of 25,000 per annum) one could not hope to read even the titles, and at best can become acquainted with comparatively a few. -While this is the case, as a reverend friend of mine put it some years ago, if people would only read ten pages a day, which any man might do as a rule if he chose, they could read all the books best worth reading. Ten pages a day would enable them to read seven to eight of the greatest books in the world every year. And that application, in a little over ten years, would enable them to read one hundred of the finest classics known to man.</p>
        <p>w,N7_! 4741</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 24, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 24, 1915.</p>
        <p>CONCERNING PARISH "BOBBERS."</p>
        <p>An Obsolete Official who Wakened Sleepers and Ejected Dogs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>BETTY FINCH OF TRINITY CHURCH.</head>
        <p>BETTY FINCH OF TRINITY CHURCH.</p>
        <p>By ARTHUR</p>
        <p>A century ago a Warrington young man sang of his family in an easily-remembered verse :</p>
        <p>My father's a clerk, My sister's a singer, My mother's a bobber, And I am a ringer.</p>
        <p>Ho was right in supposing that such an ecclesiastically employed family ought to be celebrated in -verse, and in the matter of his poetry he certainly raight,have done worse. It would have. been a kindness to 'posterity to have given biographical details,* though perhaps a meritorious modesty kept him from that. It would have been better still had he left a picture of his gifted parents at their Sunday tasks. We Must not complain. Let us make the most of the fact that Warrington had such a family, and enjoy,. whatever lustre they may have left upon the annals of the town.</p>
        <p>The name of this female "bobber " was Betty Finch. Her ecclesiastical domain was the Church of Holy Trinity. Tradition has it that her husband. excelled in the grace of meekness, and :that Betty :towefed high in the masculine virtues, so that while the hus- band held the office of clerk and sang ,the Psalms, Betty's masculine form paraded the aisles with a long rod, bestowing sharp blows on the heads Of the drowsy ones who snored during the sermon., Her rod was made long enough to reach the remote corners of the remotest pews, where wily sleepers might take refuge, and though light as a fishing rod had a " bob" fastened on the end•to give momentum. Hence the name " bobber " by which the vulgar multitude described the honoured ecclesiastical office of " sluggard- wakener." THE CHURCH A CENTURY AGO.</p>
        <p>It was a hundred years ago' and the modern reader may require some help in an effort to picture a Church service of that period. Let him therefore place promi- nently in the church a . three-decker pulpit, the bottom deck filled with the clerk and the middle one by the parson at prayers. The sister who sings is in the gallery .sur- rounded by stringed instruments and the players thereof. The `,` bobber" has a reserved seat at the back of the church, the rod held ostentatiously in a bracket ready to fall with a crack upon the head of sleepers, whether those heads wear the bristles of youth or the baldness of age. Such was a Church service of the 18th century. The inquisitive reader may see it if • he will seek out Hogarth's picture "The Sleeping Congregation," wherein the parson discourses upon •" All ye that are weary " to a congregation who would be much the better .for the services of an energetic "bobber." The " bobber " had a more delicate method with ladies. A crack from the descending rod might have done damage to millinery and given room for an action at law; or \milli9ory might make the blow ineffective. The tact- ful sluggard-wakener was equal to the diffi- culty and tied to the rod a fox's tail, which could be. applied with stimulating effect to a lades neck.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>INGENIOUS TORTURE.</head>
        <p>INGENIOUS TORTURE.</p>
        <p>The ingenious ‘,` bobber" was allowed to use his intelligence. One of them found a pitch-fork device more effective and more silent than the commonplace knobed stick, the advantage being that it 'could be applied from behind and was equally suited to mascu- line or feminine gender. Here is a descrip- tion of the instrument of torture given by an old correspondent who saw it used :</p>
        <p>"I watched as the bobber mounted with easy steps into the gallery. At the end of one of them, there sat in the front seat a young man who had very much the appear- ance of a farmer, with his mouth open, and his eyes closed, a perfect picture of repose. The official marked him for his 'own, ,and having fitted his fork to the nape of his neck, he gave him such a push that, had he not been used to such visitations, it would probably have produced an ejaculatory start highly inconvenient on such occasions. But no, everyone seemed to quietly acquiese in</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>MOUNFIELD.</head>
        <p>MOUNFIELD.</p>
        <p>the usage, and whatever else they might be dreaming of, they certainly did not dream of the infringement upon the liberties of the subject, nor did they think of applying for a summons on account of the assault."</p>
        <p>The " bo!)ber " was part of the fixed order of things. The church-goers had grown up with him. He was there to spoil what little comfort might be got out of the ser- mon, And like the sermon, he had to be tolerated. Of course there were good " bobbers " just as • there were bad ones. The good " bobber " was the one who stpmbered through the sermon himself. Naturally there were setions poeple who believed strongly in the importance of •slug- gard-wakening. Some of them (perhaps meditating in their , last hours on the. good things they had missed or might have missed by sleeping in Church) left money to support a " bobber" in their own parish. In Staffordshire there are two churches which have endowments to support a " sluggard- wakener and dog-whipper," and provide him a cottage to live in. And at Chjslet, in Rent, there is a field known as " Dog-Whipper's Marsh," the rent of Which was left .to port a " bobber " until the end of the world.</p>
        <p>where show amounts paid to th 6 whipper Thus at 'Burnley.</p>
        <p>1728. April 29. For whipping dogs 0 4 0 And Croft</p>
        <p>1718. For dogs' whipping And Youlegreave, Derbyshire 1609. To Robt. Walton, for whipping ye dogges forth of ye Church in time of divyne service, is. 4d.</p>
        <p>... 00 07 06</p>
        <p>The, offices of " hobber " and "dog whipper" were generally held by one person, who having purged the, church of four- footed intruders, exchanged the whip for the rod and made himself a terror to thq chil- dren.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <head>WHEN DOGS WENT TO CHURCH.</head>
        <p>WHEN DOGS WENT TO CHURCH.</p>
        <p>(By permission of the Proprietors of "Punch.") The KAISER: "To the day-- . DI:4T11; —.v." Of reclicailav !it</p>
        <p>The mention of dog-whipping rominds is that in addition to his rod the " bobber" of earlier days had a business-like whip with which he dealt with the dogs. Our ancestors suffered much from dogs. Lest the state- ment should puzzle the modern reader we had better explain.</p>
        <p>The church-es of, earlier times were used for fill sorts of secular purposes, and the dogs followed their owners thither. Old St. Paul's was a business • centre—and worse-,- and in a day when large buildings were few the Parish Churches were used for gather- ings and- purposes which we shudder to remember. Those who know dog-nature to even a slight degree will know that if a dog follows his master through a certain doorway on the six working days of the week ho will require solid reasons to convince him that he ought not to enter on. a Sunday. The solid reasons were supplied in the form of a dog- whip, and a gentleman endowed or paid to apply it. One of these whips is preserved at Baslow, in Derbyshire. If we remembef aright, the thong is a yard long and of per- suasive appearance. Another argument for visitirig dogs was supplied in the form of iron tongs with spikes at the end. By the aid of these the " bobber" could extract a dog from under a seat as a dentist extracts a. difficult: tooth, and could, moreover, keep him at a safe distance, Whilst conveying him to the churctiyard gate. The subject of dogs in churches could be written upon it length. In ruder days mastiffs were kept loose in the church to guard the treasures against robbers. Pos- sibly it will come as a shock to the pious to learn that chancel rails were first erected to keep the dogs from entering the sanctuary. As late as- the reign of Elizabeth, dogs went regularly to church, and one of the duties of the clerk wa to kdep the church clear from the " shomeryng of dogs." In the. High- lands to the present day the shepherd's_ collie lies quietly under the seat during service. But our Engligh anrestors in the 17th arid 18th centuries made war on dogs. The clog- whip hung threateningly near the door in the " bobber's " stall, and the parish registers from year to year show that the war was constant and relontless. In the registers of Wakefield a salary and special clothes , are mentioned, from 1616 to 1820. The "dog nawper " (" nawpin " is hitting on the head in Yorkshire dialect) had " hafts, slides and hoses" found for him and 16 shillings a year in wages.</p>
        <p>The old rhyme Bells are ringing, Cats are singing, Dogs are going to Church belongs to the days of which we are writing. While cats sang three-thrums on the hearth the dogs might be quarrelling under the seats at church, and then was the office of " dog- nawper " glorified. Parish accounts every-</p>
