      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>" ROBBERS' WHO BUNGLED.</head>
        <p>" ROBBERS' WHO BUNGLED.</p>
        <p>To round off an article which is concerned with sleepy congreiations it will be permis- sible to tell a story or two of sleepy officials. Sometimes the clerk, forgetful of his im- mense responsibilities, slumbered as deeply as any of the people he was supposed to keep awake. At St. Martin's,. London, in the course of a sermon the minister was urging that his hearers should pray that God would "endue His ministers with righteousness." The clerk was at the moment soundly asleep; but aroused by the familiar words he thoughl the minister must be in the midst of thf intercession. Sliding down to the hasseck a his feet he sang out the response "And mak, Thy Wiesen people joyful." The "chose people" , who were present were "joyful indeed.</p>
        <p>One of the duties 9f the clerk was to gi a hearty " Amen' " at the close of everything including the sermon. Sermons were gener ally lifeless and long, and all slept who could A Canon of Durham tells that on a summei afternoon the preacher paused at the end oi his " firstly." The clerk, suddenly awaken. ing, thought that, the. discourse was ended and uttercd a massive " Arun,i men." The congregation rose and the preacher taken aback, could only stammer out the Benedic- tion. The squire took the clerk aside as they left the church and put half-a-crown in his hand: " Thomas," he r hispered, " y managed it splendidly nild y.,11 ,Jese.eve a little present. I will give yen the hallitl Lext time."</p>
        <p>And Thomas stood wondering whatever ii was that had profited so much by his manage ment.</p>
        <p>Lt Le It :e 7, ye</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>HUSBAND'S GRATITUDE Belgian Soldier's Letter To Bank Quay Mission.</head>
        <p>A HUSBAND'S GRATITUDE Belgian Soldier's Letter To Bank Quay Mission.</p>
        <p>A fortnight ago a married daughter of the Belgian refugee family *whom the members of the Bank Quay Working Men's Mission are maintaining in comfort in a house in Got- borne-street—Madame Plaquee—went to Havre to rejoin her husband, Maurice, who is a First-Sergeant-Major of Grenadiers in the gal- lant little Belgian Army. After fighting bravely in the field throughout the winter, the First-Sergeant was attacked with rheumatism and recently he was sent to Havre to agsist in the superintendence of a magazine depot. Al- though his wife has nnw joined him her lot is by no means the comfortable one that it -Was before the outbreak of war, for daily she works hard at the production of war munitions, as do thousands of her homeless countrymen and women, and still more thousands of the French women among whom she now makes her home. Back With Her Warrior Husband.</p>
        <p>A day or two ago the Secretary (Mr. A. , Tilling) of the Working Meffs Mission received the following lettei of thanks from the grateful husband: At the moment that my new situation has allowed me to re- call my wife near me, I wish you to accept my thanks—which I tender from the bottom of my heart—for the good care and hospitality which you . . . have afforded to her, and which you are' still affording to my relatives remaining in Warrington. My wife, who will hold an eternal remembrance of the kindness which you and your compatriots have shown to her, wishes me to express to you her undy- ing remembrances. Since she has rejoined me she has not ceased to praise the kindness that you have shown to the Belgians. Thanks to you, I hope, one day, again to see my rela- tives, just as I have been able once again th see my wife. Many of my compatriots who, like myself, • have had and still have relatives in your •great and good country, appreciate the kindness of the English people, and in spite of the sad state in which our country finds itself, we do not regret having fought so terrible an enemy, and we are very happy to have been able to repulse, by the bravery of our soldiers, the Germans, and to have, prevented them from coming to massacre the people of your country, and to have ravaged it, as they have massacred our people and ravaged our coun- try. I hope that one day I shall have the pleasure of being able to express my thanks in person, and, in the meantime, I pray you to etcept this written expression of my profound gratitude."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0242" n="art0242">
        <head>.mmo</head>
        <p>.mmo</p>
        <p>CYCLING AND MOTORING.</p>
        <p>Local Regulations That Puzzle</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0243" n="art0243">
        <head>Motorist.</head>
        <p>The Motorist.</p>
        <p>CYCLIST HARVESTERS.</p>
        <p>(Queries and correspondence should be addressed to "The Hub," c/o The Editor. Replies can- not be sent by post and all queries will be answered in our columns.) •</p>
        <p>In all Parts of the country farmexs are find- ing it hard to secure sufficient labour for the liar vesting of the hay crop. This is naturally much more marked in some localities than it is in others. And the difficulty is likely to be equally great when the corn is ripe. During the past week I have been cycling over a large part of the Midlands, and there the shortage of labour is very apparent. In one district, however, a new source of harvesting recruits has been found. .A local club, having its head- quarters in an adjacent town, is organising among its members teams for this work. When- ever possible they cycle out to a farm, perhaps two or three or 'half-a-dozen together, according to the requirements .of the farmer, and put in as much time as they can—tedding, raking, quiling, or pitchitig. The labour is gratuitous, but meals and liquid refreshment are served out ad. lib. It is a good scheme all round. The cyclists have their ride, together with healthy exercise, while they have the knowledge that they are doing work of a most veiluable nature.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0244" n="art0244">
        <head>IMPROVED MUDGUARDS.</head>
        <p>IMPROVED MUDGUARDS.</p>
        <p>There are very few roadster qycles which are not fitted with mudguards. As a matter of fact in this country they are a necessity. The early days of July demonstrated the fact that the weather can be as bad in the summer as in December. Two additions to the ordinary form of guard greatly iftcrease the comfort of the rider. An eAtension some nine inches in length; extending from the fork crown over the top of the front wheel prevents the mud being blown on to the rider. It is not an unsightly atta,chrhent, but rather adds to the appearance of the machine. To hinder the boots becoming muddy, a war-shaped piece of leather, about seven or eight inches wide, fastened to the bottom of the front mud-guard and coming to within three inches of the ground, is very ser- viceable. The two additions are particularly needed by all-weather riders.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0245" n="art0245">
        <head>PUZZLING LOCAL REGULATIONS.</head>
        <p>PUZZLING LOCAL REGULATIONS.</p>
        <p>The variety of bye-laws and regulations which have been made so frequently of late prove puzzling to many motor cyclists. In some areas it is compulsory for two white lights to the front to be carried on all three and four- wheeled machines. All side-carists should pay attention to local regulations, since summonses are being taken out against those who do not have the additional light. One lamp must be situated on each side. The districts where the regulation is now in force are :—The Glamorgan and Stafford County Council areas, and the districts 'under the Town Councils of Neath, Merthyr Tydfil, Hanley, Dudley, Newcastle, Stafford, Monmouth, and, during foggy wea- ther, Birmingham. When riding in a strange 1 country it is a wise proceeding to ask the first policeman if there are any special restrictions or regulations. It is better to pull up and waste. a few minutes than be summoned for non-compliance with the special bye-laws.</p>
        <p>THE PRICE OF SECOND-HAND MOTOR</p>
        <p>CYCLES.</p>
        <p>The majority of motor cyclists have been accustomed to allow, a considerable percentage every year off the estimated value of their machines to cover depreciation. Judging by the present state of the second-hand markets in the country, it is not necessary to look upon a three or four-year-old machine as worth very little. Taken all round. the present value of a second-hand motor cycle is about £lO higher than was the case a twelvemonth ago. Motor cycles three years old, but in good condition, are fetching £4O and more apiece, whilst a side-car outfit frequently changes hands at £6O. There is naturally a large number of second- hand machines for which considetably less is being asked, but as a rule these are of low power. Prices have stiffened very much of</p>
        <p>A FINE RECORD.</p>
        <p>British Submarine Commander Sinks Battleship.</p>
        <p>Commander Max K. Horton was the officer commanding the British submarine which tor- pedoed and sank the German battleship Pom- mern in the Baltic on July 2nd.</p>
        <p>This was stated in the House of Commons by Dr. Macnamara, in reply to a question by Com- mander Bellairs. No official report, he said, had been transmitted to the Admiralty, but from eemi-otlicial communication received</p>
      </div>
