        <p>GETTING OUT OF SAND.</p>
        <p>I came across a very practical suggestion short time ago in an American newspaper. is not often that on gets stuck fast in sant', but there are occasions on which the followingt may prove useful. "Many motorists are lot able to extricate a car when stuck in 6311,` without resorting to the rope. When unable to obtain traction in sand do not continue t° apply the power to the rear wheels, as it Oil result in the wheels bedding in. A simple, method, and one not generally known, is to le', the air out of the rear tyres. This will agora the required traction, as the width of the We is greatly increased. It is much' easier t° inflate tyres than to dig and use boards, rsils, &amp;c. If the power is applied gradually the tfr? will not be injured." One would not exPec: the tyres to suffer, if, as suggested, the Pore's be applied gradually. I wish I had known 0,1 this method in the early part of 1909, when got into a similar, fix in the North-west Scotland.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0248" n="art0248">
        <head>NEW CHICAGO TRACK.</head>
        <p>THE NEW CHICAGO TRACK.</p>
        <p>A new two-mile track has just been core' pleted in Chicago, and the first race took plecAe at the end of June. The track is built of Ivo': and it is stated to be the fastest in the work'. The first race to be held was ode of 500 miles', open to cars up to 4,915 c.c. There was e° accident, a thing which is rather unusual in American racing. In the eliminating triale Resta (Pougeot) attained a speed of 110.1 ra.lol* completing a lap in lm. 4/ssec. Another driver did the same distance at 109.09 m.p.h. The first six competitors were remarkably close to- gether, the winner, Resta, attaining an average speed of 97.60 m.p.h., and the sixth man, Anderson, 94.09 m.p:m. The record made at Brooklands on Ist October, 1910, ,namely 500 miles at 94.74 m.p.h. was beaten by the first four men on the Chicago track. The prizes were £5,000, £2,000 and £l,OOO respectively for the first three men.</p>
        <p>THE HUB.</p>
        <p>that from the Russian Government it apPeered Appointed to tlllOcommand of submarine in March, 1914, Commander Max K. Hort" G °I September 13th, sank off Heligoland the ael% light cruiser Hela, and then for several be° had to dodge a number of enemy destroYere'bis The following day, in accordance with orders, Lieutenagt-COmmander Horton , e'a amined the outer anchorage of Heligolonoi. ,0 service attended by considerable risk. Foe acts the gallant commander was aWardo D.S.O. - be. A month later, when patrolling oft the Bel'',o6; torpedoed and sank the enemy's de,troyor the - the name of the officer referred to wag 'd mander liorton.</p>
