      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0250" n="art0250">
        <head>CHILDREN'S SUMMER</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S SUMMER</p>
        <p>CAMP.</p>
        <p>First Batch Of Poor Children Start Holiday To-day.</p>
        <p>Three hundred of Warrington's poor children are to be given a week's holiday by the silvery sea, for in spite of the war that excellent insti- tution, the Children's Summer Camp at Pres- tatyn, is to be opened muclii as usuat And arrangements have already been completed for the reception of the first batch of the happy ?Toungsters to-day. We used the phrase much as usual" because owing to the nn- Certainty as to railway charges and the high cost of food-stuffs the committee have decided to send only two batches of children this sum- mer, the first batch consisting of 150 girls, and the second batch, a. week later, of a like num- ber of boys.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0251" n="art0251">
        <head>"Joy Week" for 150 Girls.</head>
        <p>"Joy Week" for 150 Girls.</p>
        <p>The girls who have been fortunate enough to be selected for a free holiday will be conveyed in reserved compartments attached to the 10.28 a.m. train from Warrington this morning. They will be accompanied and cared for by a num- ber of voluntary officers, who, throughout the week, will do their best to see to it that none of their charges come to any harm and that they are all safely restored to their parents at the end of the week, sun-tanned and generally im- proved in health.</p>
        <p>More Funds Wanted.</p>
        <p>The Honorary Secretary (Councillor W. H. J. Jenkins)—to whose kindly thought for the less- happily circumstanced youngsters the move- 'neat which leads to the provision of this annual seaside holiday was due—has had charge of the arrangements, and he appeals to the generosity of the people of Warrington to see t° it th.a.t the coffers do not lack "the neces- ZVI There is urgent need for further sub- The school children of the town cop„„:een doing their best by donating their and if those with larger means only villPortionately as well, the committee who e this excellent institution will, have no for qualms.</p>
      </div>
