      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0259" n="art0259">
        <head>TO-DAY'S TIES.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S TIES.</p>
        <p>Four More Games Down For Settle-</p>
        <p>ment.</p>
        <p>Eight teams are drawn to meet in the " Fin- layson " Cup Competition, this afternoon, when Mr. W. Bridge will be the referee, and some more close and interesting results may be con- fidently expected. The teams drawn against each other, with the times at which their tourneys are due to start, are:—</p>
        <p>2.o.—Crasfield's Soap Department V. Long- ford No. 1. o.4s.—Grappenhall Tannery v. P. and K. Bewsey Forge. 3.3o.—Crosfield's (Cheshire) V. Vulcan Engineers. 4.ls.—County Asylum v. Monks, Halt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0260" n="art0260">
        <head>LIBERALS VICTORIOUS.</head>
        <p>LIBERALS VICTORIOUS.</p>
        <p>Beat Commercial Travellers In Singles</p>
        <p>And Doubles. Bowlers representing the Liberal Club and the local Commercial Traveller? Association spent several pleasant hours on the green of the Cat and Lion Inn, Stretton, on Saturday alter- They drove to the venue of their tourney in taxi-eabs. The appearance of the sky in the morning gave rise to apprehension, but the afternoon turned out gloriously sun- shiny and the outing-tournament proved most thoroughly enjoyable. The arrangements were made by Mr. Wm. Owen, the secretary of the Commercial Travellers' Association, and Mr. George Marshall, the secretary of the Liberal Bowling Club. After the match the bowlers sat down to a capital tea. Mr. H. Walsh, of the Travellers' Association, expressed the pleasure he and his fellow members felt at having had their good friends, the Liberal bowlers, with them, and hoped that when the return match was played on the Bewsey-street green they would be able to get their revenge. Councillor J. T. Locker expressed the thanks of the Liberal bowlers to the Travellers for their hospitality. Mr. Wm. Walker, of the Travellers' Associa- tion, moved a hearty vote of thanks to Messrs. Owen and Marshall for the able manner in which they had carried out the arrangements. This was carried with applause, the,, compli- ment being briefly acknowledged by bot gentle- men named.</p>
        <p>THIRTY-EIGHT POINTS TO THE GOOD. The Liberals, were victorious both in the singles and the doubles, winning the former by 13 points and the latter by 25. The detailed scores were:— I Singles.</p>
