      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0264" n="art0264">
        <head>ASSURANCE AGENTS ON THE GREEN.</head>
        <p>ASSURANCE AGENTS ON THE GREEN.</p>
        <p>Jolly Outing And Some Good Games.</p>
        <p>The members of the Warrington Assurance Agents Works' Competition bowling team on Thursday participated in an enjoyable picnic to Bollington. After they had done full justice to an excellent tea at a local hostelry, a bowl- ing main was brought off, when, following some exciting games, Mr. S. Robinson (Liverpool Victoria) beat Mr. A. Oxley (Brittanic) by 13 ,points ,to 12. Mr. Mehers (Prudential) was third, and Mr. It Jones' (Refuge) fourth. ,Four industrial offices were represented in the semi- final. A consolation tourney was afterwards played, when a popular win was scored by the hon. secretary (Mr. J. Delaney), with Mr. E. Wilcocks as second. A vote ,of thanks to the secretary and com- mittee brought a most enjoyable outing to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0265" n="art0265">
        <head>Association Footballers Who Answered the Call.</head>
        <p>Association Footballers Who Answered the Call.</p>
        <p>ORFORD ST. JOHN'S</p>
        <p>--</p>
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL FRED SMITH. Wounded, and Returned to the Front</p>
        <p>On several occasions we have called attention to the patriotic action of the members of the Orford St. Sohn's Association Football Club, who abandoned the game at the outbreak of the war to join the colours. This week we give the photograph of three brothers Smith, of 21, Norris-street, who were in the team, who are proud to be doing their "bit."</p>
        <p>PRIVATE JOHN SMITH. now with Ammunition Column.</p>
        <p>dropping on a dug-out and killing five and almost the sixth. I began to scrape away the dirt and wood aid I came across a poor fellow who was not dead, so I went for a stretcher- bearer, and he said that he could not live, but I told him to get hold of his head and we would carry him to a safer part of the trench. Anyway, he was taken into hospital, where I hope he is coming round. I got down and thanked God for the courage that he had given me,"</p>
        <p>F.C.</p>
        <p>r PRIVATE ERNEST SMITH, Wounded—in Hospital.</p>
        <p>came home and was put on ammunition column work at Birmingham.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE ERNEST SMITH,</p>
        <p>who is in the Territorials, was wounded in the great charge that cost the battalion dearly but brought it lasting glory. Writing this week from Wellington Hospital, he says he is doing Al. "I was out at a garden party yesterday," he says, "and had a clinking 'burst up.' I might be able to get home for Bank Holiday. We are going to Northampton this (Thursday) afternoon in motor .care to visit a pantomime. I A ' toff's' life! Do you think I should ever have been lucky enough in the ordinary course of -events to drive in a car?"</p>
        <p>Private Ernest Smith was a pattern-moulder at Richmond's.</p>
