      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0268" n="art0268">
        <head>FUTURE OF FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FUTURE OF FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Payment Of Association Players Suspended.</p>
        <p>Important meetings of the_.Association foot- ball authorities of England were held on Mon- day in. London, at which the.modied arrange- ments for next season's play were completed. The Southern League had already decided to recommend that all competitive football be abandoned. Now the English League have resolved (and the English Association have acquiesced)— To arrange a number of competitions which may include clubs in membership of 'the League and other clubs in the respective localities, so that there may be throughout the country, so far as possible, football played which will meet the requirements of the people, provided always that any Football League club has liberty to refuse to partici- pate in any such competition. _</p>
        <p>on the question of Payment to players the League Management Committee decided by 21 votes to 19 to pay players, but later in the day this was quashed at the English F.A. Council meeting by the passing of the following resolu- tions :</p>
        <p>I.—That no international matches or the Chal- lenge _Cup and Amateur Cup matches of this</p>
        <p>Association be played during the next season. 2.—That Associations, Leagues and clubs be allowed to arrange matches without cup, medals or other rewards to suit local con-</p>
        <p>ditions, provided that they do not interfere with the work of those engaged in war work. 3.—That clubs may join any combination of clubs which may be convenient to them. 4.—That matches be played only on Saturday</p>
        <p>afternoons and on -early closing and other recognised holidays. s.—That lie remuneration shall be paid to' players, nor shall there be any registration of players, but clubs and, players should be sub- ject to the rules and conditions applicable to them on April 30th, 1915. 6.—That agreements with players for Fervice after April 30th, 1915, shall be suspended watil further order.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. C. Clegg waa re-elected chalrman of the counoil and Mr. Charles Crump vice-2..11-dr- man. As representative of Division- 11., the election for which had resulted, in a tie between Mr. W. E. Ord (Preston North End) and NIT. C. E. Sutcliffe (Lancashire Association), Mr. Ord was chosen.</p>
        <p>Water.</p>
        <p>"How did that Number Seven furnace happen to go out? No excuses, now I" bellowed the Hades Head Stoker.</p>
        <p>"We threw in a High Financier," the Imp explained, "and neglected to take out of his pocket a bunch of stock that he had."</p>
        <p>No Misrepresentation. Boarder (at summer resort): Didn't your booklet say there were no mosquitoes here? Proprietor: It did, sir, and it told the exact truth. We had that booklet printed in Feb- ruary, and I am prepared to prove that there wasn't a blamed (slap) mosquib within tOO miles of here at that time.</p>
        <p>Tolstoy's Opinion of Kings. The, sovereigns now living, instigators of violence and massacres of all kinds, are so far below the moral standard of the majority that they cannot even inspire disgust. They are but unfortunates, who deserve to be pitied. We should neither allow our indignation to rise against those creatures, who are void of the most sacred feeling of humanity, nor should we combat them."</p>
      </div>
