      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0270" n="art0270">
        <p>HOME-MADE POISON.</p>
        <p>Sir John Simon On Cultivated Hysteria.</p>
        <p>Sir John Simon, the Home Secretary, ad- dressing a patriotic demonstration on the Ley- ton Cricket Ground on Saturday afternoon,said he had come down to ask them to consider with him how they stood after nearly twelve months of war. "I do not mean that I came down to tell Cabinet secrets," he added, "except this Cabinet secret—that the Cabinet is a united Cabinet devoting itself with all its strength to the single purpose for which it was called together." If, he said, a restraint was imposed upon the political leaders of the nation, so that they could all throw themselves into this single ob- ject with a single mind, then there was a cor- responding duty of restraint upon the public at large. This war will be won, and it has got to be won, by resolutely avoiding all sterile controversies whatsoever. But do not imagine that I am asking people to postpone criticism for ever. (Laughter.) There will be lots of criticism some (lay, and I tell you frankly I do not care how much criticism there is. and I do not care how strongly it is expressed, and I do not care what consequences come from it, as long as that criticism is held back until we have first beaten the enemy. Sterile contro- versies about the past, unworthy panic about the future, cultivated hysteria about the pre- sent—all these things are poisonous gases emitted, whether those who emit them know it or not—with no other result save to asphyxiate and to confuse the judgment and temper of the nation. I say plainly that, if we do not do more in the future than we have done in the past, we are going to be beaten, and it is just because we are not going to be beaten but are going to win, that it is perfectly certain 'there is no sacrifice this country will not willingly make, there is nothing that it has got, nothing that it can get, and nothing it can con- tribute in any form that it will not throw into the scale. It is because of this that I am so completely confident of the ultimate success of this tremendous Wand overwhelming struggle. ("What about LorA Haldane?") That is just the -sort of question, which I do not think is well to ask. I have only one question and that is, "What about beating the enemy?"</p>
        <p>Continuing, Sir John remarked that in the debit side of the account first and foremost there came the terrible toll of human • life which was involved in that awful struggle. Then there was the appalling destructiveness of modern instruments of warfare. A Russian general bad told him that in four hours the Germans fired as much explosive shell against the fortress of Przemysl as the experts of the Russian Army would have thought to be suffi- cient for a 'siege of six months. The three duties of democracy were unity, cheerfulness, and fortitude. Let us remember our reputa- tion for keeping our nerve. What was the good of discussing in a spirit of alarm, panic or curi- osity what we were going to do when the next air raid occurred? What we did at the last air raid was to stay in our beds, most of us, and know nothing about it. (Laughter.) Ger- many did not suppose she could conquer Eng- land by gas bags; she only thought she could frighten us. Let us show her that she could not. (Cheers.)</p>
        <p>She Economised. "What' is this?" asked the fond husband, 'holding up 'a garment that has .just been. de- livered, to his -wife. , . "Part of my summer wardrobe," replies the obedient wife. '4l suppose it is; but what is it? A collar?' he asked. "Why, no, you goose! It's my bathing dress." "Your bathing dress? As short as that?" "Yes, dear. You know you told me business had been poor And I must economise on every- thing."</p>
