        <p>• Preparations are being made for the .enutn- eration of the grown-up people of Warrington in order, that the local edition of the National. Register may be Completed as expeditiously and as satisfactorily as possible. During the week the 'town Clerk (Mr. S. Lyon Whittle) has re- ceived forms of instruction from the Local Government Board, and the Registrar-General, and, in accordance with suggestions contained therein, the Borough has been divided into nine wards, each of which will be sub-divided into smaller areas, so as to provide about 160 enumeration districts. Accordingly it follows that at least a similar number of persons will be required to deliver and collect the forms, which all adults will be required to 11l up. The Borough Gymnasium, in Palmyra-square, will be utilised as a central office for the pur- poses of the enumeration, aid there all the clerical work in connection with the prepara- tion of the forms, &amp;c., will be carried out. Other offices will be opened at the Bolton Council School, the Emmanuel Church Insti- tute, the Eirelyu7street Council School, the St. Bariabaa' figh92l, th 2 iitrAt-staect h.**, the</p>
