      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0292" n="art0292">
        <head>MR. EDWARD ROSE.</head>
        <p>MR. EDWARD ROSE.</p>
        <p>" Head " Of Blue Coat School About To Retire..</p>
        <p>AFTER THIRTY YEARS.</p>
        <p>After having been headmaster of the War- rington Blue Coat School, Winwick-road, for a matter of thirty-three years, Mr. Edward Rose is approaching the eve of retirement Under his' a4gis great improvements have taken plate in the old school, and the number of scholars has come ,near to doubling itself, for when he was appoint-Rd they numbered but 42, and now there are Seventy. Considerable structural alterations and additions have also been made. In 1888, ,for instance, the Board Room, where the Governors meet, was improved by the re- moval of the old flagged floor and the substi- tution a a wooden floor. A new dining hall has been added to the amenities of the insti- tution and additional sleeping accommodation has had to be made by the building of a new bedroom for the scholars. In 1895 a new two-manual organ was installed into the school chapel, and in 1904 a large amount of house and.other property on the north side of Oliver- street was pulled down in order that the boundaries of the school grounds might be ex- tended. Other minor improvements have been effected, until the school now is as nearly per- fect for its purpose as an institution of the kind could be made.</p>
        <p>Old Scholari Who Have Earned Distinction. Mr. Rose is intensely proud of his school and of the boys who during his long term as head- master have passed through his hands, and he has good cause to be, for not only has the school been improved in a great many respects, but aany of his old boys are now adorning various Ivalks of life, locally and in many parts .of the country, .and, indeed, in other quarters of the world. One of them, Mr. Robert Crompton, is now a barrister practising in. the Courts in the Fiji Islands, whilst an- other, Mr. R. C. Essenhigh, has considerably distinguished himself in the art world, having won a brilliant art scholarship. Mr. Essenhigl is at the moment serving with His Maje Ky's Forces. It is interesting to recall 'the fact that the Blue Coat School has helped to pro- duce at least one Mayor of Warrington within recent times, viz., the late Alderman Forshaw, who was Mayor during the years 1908-9-10, and who died on July 24th of last year. The lath Alderman, however, had left the school before Mr. Rose was appointed to the headmastership.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0293" n="art0293">
        <head>Mr. Rose's Scholastic Career--.</head>
        <p>Mr. Rose's Scholastic Career--.</p>
        <p>Mr. Rose was trained for the scholastic pro- fession at St. Mark's College, Chelsea, and be- fore he became associated with the Bluecoat School he was the headmaster of the Chester- street Board School, Ardwick, Manchester. Further back still, he was the headmaster ot the National School, Mold, Flintshire. In the Queen's Scholarship Examination of 1563 he gained seventh place among student teachers drawn from all parts of the kingdom. ' When he quits his scholastic duties Mr. Rose. with his wife, proposes living in retirement ht Stockton Heath, a place which holds famil: ties for him, from the fact that one of his snns is headmaster at the local school and organist at the Parish Church; whilst one of the two daughters is an assistant mistress in the girls' department of the same school. Another of Mr. Rose's sons is in practice as a solicitor at Newcastle-on-Tyne; whilst another daughter is the head of the Practising School in connect.ou with the Derby Training College,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0294" n="art0294">
        <head>PRIORY SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>PRIORY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Royal Drawing Society Examination. The following are the results of the recent Royal Drawing Society's Examination, held at the above school:—</p>
        <p>Illustrating Syllabus. • . . Full .Certificates Division V1.:7-Second class pass: Elizabeth Armstrong.</p>
        <p>School Certificates.</p>
        <p>Division V.—Pass: Lilian Forrest. Division 111.—Honours: Alice Wilkinson. Pass: Sybil Tinnion, Division ll.—Honours: Amy Wilkinson, Gwen Bennett, Vera. Nicholls. Division I.—Honours: Ada Cook, Sybil Tin- nion, Amy Wilkinson, Gwen Bennett, Molly Plumpton, Sybil Bate, Mary Teare. Pass: Marie Byerley, Joan Anderson, Mary Allen, Vera Nicholls, Nellie. Parkinson, Kathleen Byerley. Preparatory.—Honours: Mary Allen, Joan Anderson, Sybil Bate, Kathleen Byerley, Nellie Parkinson, 'Molly Plumpton. Pass: Dorothy Elughes, Marjorie Landon.</p>
        <p>THE CHARGE.</p>
        <p>In Which The Warrington Territorials</p>
        <p>Took Part. We thank you, lads, with all our hearts, For noble deeds you've done, To try and rid the world at large Of the cruel, heartless Hun. The charge was great and glorious, By it you've history made. Your General in' his pride cried out "Unbreakable! Brigade."</p>
        <p>Through the gas, grenade and shrapnel, Tenaciously you fought, Until victory crowned your efforts, But, oh! how dearly bought? When your pals around were falling, Vengeance your hearts enflamed; 'Twas then you shewed that British Pluck, For which we've long been famed. We honour you, who are living, We grieve for those who fell; For justice, honour, truth and right, You faced the mouth of Hell.</p>
        <p>To Parents, Brothers, Sisters all, To Wives and Aweethearts dear, Deepest sympathy we offer, • And shed with you a tear. In your loneliness and sorrow, Their spirits hover near; Think, that after all, your loved ones Did their duty without fear. RD. OSBORNE. 4, Lilford-etreet, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0295" n="art0295">
        <head>KING AND DRINK.</head>
        <p>THE KING AND DRINK.</p>
        <p>His Majesty Not A Teetotaler, But Against War-Time Drinking. The KitYg'S attitude on the question of drink- ing in war time was the chief point of a con- vincing address by the Rev.. H. Ogden, de- livered at Bewsey-street corner, last night, under the auspices of the Warrington and Dis- trict Temperance League. Mr. J. Jarman, who presided, observed that it was a remarkable thing that, in spite of the King's example and appeal to the people, so much alcohol continued to be sold. The Rev. H. Ogden reminded his hearer, that the King, who had issued the appeal , which that meeting was called to support, was not an absolute but a constitutioaal monarch. The Unlit of even his power was to suggto the people. Had the Kaiser spoken, in Ger- many, it would have been an edict forbidding the whole of the German nation to consume alcoholic drinks. One might be sure, the reverend gentleman said, that there was no , touch of bigotry in the King's action. Tem- perance people might be fanatics. The awful power of the drink was a sufficient apology for strong action. But the King's attitude was not the attitude of the ganaral and only grave circumstances would lead him 'to suggest prohibition to the uation an-d-Wa1....- tise it in his household. The .Ving stood as the head of our social life, and moderate in- dulgence in alcohol was part of that social life. Surely then, those who would not listen to teetotalers might reasonably be expected to listen to and respect the judgment Of the King! A number of pledges were taken at the close of the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0296" n="art0296">
        <head>Cure for "Slackers."</head>
        <p>A Cure for "Slackers."</p>
        <p>On July 9 the following advertisement ap- peared in "The Times":— Jack F. G.—lf you are not in khaki by the 20th I shall cut you dead.—Ethel M. The Berlin correspondent of the "Cologne Gazette" was so much struck by this threat that he telegraphed the following version to his journal If you are not in khaki by the 20th I sha4 tack pm te death ihicke ich dich ru Todo.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0297" n="art0297">
        <head>STORIES FROM .THE TRENCHES.</head>
        <p>STORIES FROM .THE TRENCHES.</p>
        <p>" VERY BRAVELY THEY FACE DANGER."</p>
        <p>Rev. T. T. James Writes To Wycliffe</p>
        <p>From France.</p>
        <p>The Rev. T. T. James, pastor of the Wyciiffe Congregational Church, who has been for some weeks now with the Forces "somewhere in France," has written the followinc, letter, dated July 12th, to Mr. Ben Farrar. the secretary of the Wycliffe P.S.A. Brotherhood, and which was read at a gathering of the Brotherhood on Sunday "Just a line to say that I am often thinking of you at the P.S.A. meetings on Sunday after- noons. I hope you are having good times, with steady attendances and a good spirit in the gatherings. You may be quite certain that you are never forgotten. Will you remember n2e to everyone and say that all the lads out here are bright and cheerful? Very bravely they face danger and very bravely they go through suffering. And many a lad has got a grip on God and found it to be the only thing that counts at a time like this. We have some splendid services amongst the men here. They are splendid audiences to address."</p>
        <p>"TOMMY'S' TONICS."</p>
        <p>How Letters From Home Stimulate The</p>
        <p>Boys At The Front.</p>
        <p>We have received the following interesting letter from Corporal H. Pritchard, an old War. rin,gtonion, who is serving in France w&amp;thNo. 1 Siege Co. of the Royal Engineers:— “As I am a native of Warrington you way be interested to hear how. I.am getting on out at the Front. I have now been here for nearly nine months, so have had some experience of what real war means. At• present our company is occupied in strengthemng our front tuie of trenches, and I think we have now got them as near impregnable as is' possible. . A Spot Where We Have to Work.</p>
        <p>"When the work is exposed to the fire of the enemy we do it at night, for safety, though even then there is a certain amount of danger attached to it. For some time past we have had rather a quiet time in our part of the theatre de guerre; but we never know what moment things may alter,, and we have always to be on the alert.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0298" n="art0298">
        <head>Another Warringtonian Wounded.</head>
        <p>Another Warringtonian Wounded.</p>
        <p>IFI see the Warrington Territorials have been doing good work, and I am only sorry they have not been operating in our vicinity, as' I should very much like to come across them. Up to now one Warrington man in our com- pany has been wounded, viz., Sapper Burns, and he is on his way home to be patched up, so as to be fit to resume his share in the good work the company is doing. I, in conjuction with the rest of the Warringtonians in the com- pany, was very pleased to hear of Mr. A. H. Crosfield being made a Baronet, and hope that at the next General Election he may once more be successful in being returned to Parliament. There are several of my old friends in Warring- ton whom I should be very pleased to hear from, and I sincerely trust that now they know my address they will write to me, as a letter from anyone in dear old Warrington is always a good tonic to us out here, and goodness only knows that we sometimes require tonics to stimulate us."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0299" n="art0299">
        <head>READY FOR THE FRONT AGAIN.</head>
        <p>READY FOR THE FRONT AGAIN.</p>
        <p>Two Members Of Wycliffe Brotherhood In Hospital.</p>
        <p>• Writing from a Territorial base camp "some- where in France," to Mr. Ben Farrar, the secre- tary of the Wycliffe P.S.A. Brotherhood, Private C. E. Norris, of A Company of the 4th (Territorial) Battalion of the South Lames. Regiment, says:—" I often think of the pleasant Sunday afternoon I have spent with my brother members at Wycliffe, and I sometimes wonder how long it will be before I am privileged to spend another afternoon with them. I live in hopes that I shall spend many more such after- noons as those—but one never knows! I ex- pect you will have heard that I have been in hospital at Rouen with concussion, but I am pleased to be able to tell you that I am myself again. .. . Another of our members was in hospital with me—A. Payne. He too is again in the best of health, and we expect to go up the line together. We spend some pleasant evenings in hospital and in the Y.M.C.A. tent, without which I don't know what we should do. Those in charge of the Y.M.C.A. tent supply us with almost any game we ask for, and with refreshments. Besides that we have some of the best of concerts. . . I learn that the Rev. Mr. James is out here, and I have been looking forward eagerly to seeing him; but I suppose he is at one of the other rest camps. I received a letter from him when I first came into hospital. • • • Wounded Wycliffian Sent to England.</p>
        <p>" Another member of our Brotherhood who has been wounded is J. Bond, and he, I believe, is in hospital at Cambridge. . . lam sure that you all in the old town were sorry to hear of our big casualty list in the charge on the 'l6th of last month. We got words of commen- dation from our Brigadier, and the manner in which we did our duty was a credit to our town. .. • The weather here is very wet at the moment, but up to a day or so ago we had not had any rain for at least three months. . . . Please give my best wishes to all fellow mem- bers of the Brotherhood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0300" n="art0300">
        <head>PHANTOM TOWN.</head>
        <p>A PHANTOM TOWN.</p>
        <p>What Our Men Are Fighting To Prevent.</p>
        <p>Driver J. W. Beckett, R.F.A., writing to Mr. and Mrs. Lloyd, 36, Wakefield-street, says:— "You will be surprised to hear that our guns are covering the good old S. Lancs.' trenches. I have met that glorious regiment, but it was in the dark, while I was going up to the guns with ammunition.</p>
        <p>"I have had a few Warringtonians by the hand since I came to this part of the globe, which is no amiable picture to view. I have read in the good old 'Examiner' that it makes anyone cry to witness the sight of ruined homes out here, It is absolutely wieked. The hardest hearted of men would have a slight shiver if he could see this once-beautiful town. The Huns are not satisfied- with transforming beautiful buildings into ruins, but they turn the heaps over with the seventeen inch shells, which are worse than "coal boxes," • because they are so enormous. They make very little noise; therefore we christened them "carpet slipper" shells. The other day we obserted the effects of one of these dreadful missiles when it hit three houses at once and opened a cavity into which the three houses disappeared.</p>
        <p>"Ii is an awful sensation to pass -through this phantom town at night—ruins everywhere, and some awful smells of the unfoitunate ones buried in the ruins, cattle strewn everywhere, and drains laid open with shell holes. If only a photographer could visit this doomed town he would take pictures that would be written on the Britishers' minde for-generations to come. The .vez7 thing that the good old South Lanca- shires gave their lives 130' *dearly for is. to pre- vent a repetition of this barbarism in our own homes.</p>
        <p>Proud of Warrington.</p>
        <p>• -"I •shall always be proud to say I came from Warrington, where the glorious fighters come from:</p>
        <p>a I received 'The Examiner all right, and am very sorry to read about some of the chil- dren's- sports being spoiled on Walking Day; it is to be hipped that next year will see the best Walking Day that has ever been in Warrington. Am sorry to hear Bill Hodson has been killed, but he gave his life for his King %Ind country, his dear old home and a good cause—so that the children of Warrington may continue to enjoy their Walking Day without interference from the mean and cowardly Hun."</p>
        <p>Thelwall Heys Military Convalescent</p>
        <p>Home.</p>
        <p>The Thelwall Tleye. Military Convalescent Home will be open to the public from 11 a.m. to 6 p.m. on Thurstlax Pat. Viclig efts axe es.rotitly solicited.</p>
