      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-07-24_WidnesExaminer_art0301" n="art0301">
        <head>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>"WEST END BILL AT THE HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>This week's Hippodrome pregramme is full: of good things. There is not a single weak turn in it, and at least three of them are of out- standing merit. Jean Schwiller, the Anglo- Russian 'cellist, who holds Belgian acadeznie honours, provides an entertainment that is a real source of delight to the really musical, and ‘, the lady vocalist who assists him, no less than 4 i the lady pianist, who accompanies, imparts just that right air of the popular into the turn ' which is requisite to make the show a com- plete success with the class of entertainment - lovers for whom it was devised. The talented 'cellist with his classical selections—played with a high appreciation .of technique and of the dignity of the interpreter of good music—the lady vocalist who sings songs that are popular without being too badly tainted with the chearm 'l' ' ness which is too often met with in the variet/ - houses, mid the clever pianist, form n talent trio. Quite in another vein and extreme/i clever is the gymnastic turn afforded by th. Three Ernests, a trio of the " slickest " bar acrobatics it has ever been our pleasure te award the Order of the Glad Hand. There i# humour and originality about their show, a° well as more than ordinary daring, and the will be an acquisition to any mukc-hall pro' - gramme. Described as "the rag-bag Of vanity," Tom E. Hughes proves to be quite °I novelty among the genus comedian, his strongest; number being that in which, oostumed in the habiliments of a decidedly frayed "swell" 13°' pours out laconic sympathy for the sad lot of the " poo-er." Tom is a " gradely turn." Hugl;. Hughes is one of the smartest, "cutest things .. in the way of lightning black ana white artist-artistes it has been eur good luck to glimpse and, in conk' mon with the Test of the audience, present when we were there, we enjoyed his turn immensely. Clifford and Grey do almost anY" thing with hoops except make 'em speak. Owe!, and, Willis sing and dance and crack jokes, an°, Phil Dooley and Minnie Thompson whistle an" vocalise and show no mean terusichoreaa ability, whilst the pictures—well, like all the rest of the programme, they are Al. The ma° who wanted anything back at the box office after this show would be an arch-misanthrope!</p>
        <p>For Next Week. - g The bookings for next week are such promise a most excellent entertainment. Ph," Rees and his little coppers" will "tap tne bill," and Will Van Allen, "the tramp core; than, with music added," will occupy secolw. place of honour on the programme. Other artistee will include: Lily Morris, comedientlel the Sisters Holman, duettists and dancer Henry Bekker, magical entertainer; Baker all' Bunn, comedy cartoonists; and Ft-Allennovelty comedian.</p>
      </div>
