        <p>AN APPRECIATIVE NEPHEW. Alfred Long writes "79, Orford-avenue, Warrington, July 17th, 1915. "Dear Uncle Ned,—l heartily thank you for the prize which I received on Tuesday morning. I have read many other stories, but none are quite so interesting as the one which you have sent to me. lam sure you could not have pleased me better than send- ing me a book which contains quite a lot about mechanism. So now I will close my letter.—l remain, your affectionate nephew, ALFRED LONG." Very glad you are pleased, Alfred. It is not always easy to select just the book that is wanted. MORE NEW MEMBERS. I have received a letter sigtied W. WATICINSON and J. FORSYTH, and headed 7 and 10, Heaton- street, Warrington, applying for membership. Welcome, W. and J. ! Your numbers will he 471 and 472, respectively. NEXT WEEK'S ESSAY. For next week's essay I give you no subject: each choose what you would like to write about and—write about it.. Don't forget to let your compositions be neat, nice and nephew-ly (or niece-Iy. Well, dear children, before I ,say an revoir till next week, let me ask you all to remember that, if I do not prove to be so successful an uncle as your beloved Uncle Ned—as I'm very much afraid I shan't be ram not an uncle in so many places as he is. I'm afraid:l've only one niece—a very small bundle with very pink toes and a nice little snub nose (her name is Peggy)—and no nephews at all, but I've done my best and know you will appreciate the amateur efforts of Your Affectionate Uncle, HI7BERT.</p>
