        <p>PRISONERS IN GERMANY.</p>
        <p>IN THE HANDS The above photograph, taken in Germany, has been forwarded by' Sergeant Abel Whit- taker, whose mother lives at 6, Naylor-street. The Sergeant was "missing" for three months, but he was then reported to be a prisoner of war, and he has now sent this photograph from the camp at Doeberitz, Germany. He is the sergeant in uniform on the extreme left of the picture. Sergeant Whittaker, who, up to sixteen years ago, was employed in the Bewsey Forge, was called up in the Lichfield Militia in 1899, to proceed to South Africa. Since then he has been in the Regular Army and is now in the First Rifle Brigade. He writes to thank his relatives for the par- cels sent and says "the loaf came as a great surprise; please repeat it." He says he has plenty of clothing, and asks for tobacco instead of "fags." THREE SOLDIEII-BROTHERS. One Wounded And One A German Prisoner. Private John Gannon, of the Warrington Territorials, is at present in hospital in King's- road, Chelsea, London, recovering from a wound in the , left buttock, received in the glorious charge of June 16th. Writing to his brother and sister-in-law, Private and Mrs. T. Gannon, who live at 11, Clara-street, Bank Quay, he says: "The doc- tors .have not yet found the bullet, although they have had the X-rays on me three times. It was in the third line of the trenches we</p>
